      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>ANNUAL fEETING.</head>
        <p>ANNUAL fEETING.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. E. Warren presided over the annual meeting of the Warrington Football Club which was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Tuesday evening. There was a large attendanceand amongst those present were :—Messrs. T. Pemberton (treasurer), H. Ashton (secretary), T. Bowers, T. Davies, W. Hewitt, T.Locker, W.Chalmers,and F.Barber (members of the Committee.)</p>
        <p>REPORT AND ACCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>The annual report and statement of accounts which we published last week, were laid before the meeting. In moving their adoption Mr. E. ENGLAND said as , one of the oldest members of the club he did so with pleasure. He would be voicing the opinion of all of them when he said that they were delighted with the , _ _ .</p>
        <p>manner in which the club had been conducted during _</p>
        <p>the past year. (Applase.) They were all pleased ti; see the team win the Northern UntonCup,and it seemed to him that it was won, not on the ground at Broughtem alone, but in the committee room, because of the excellent way the committee had looked after the players. (Applause.) At the same time he congratu- lated the players on the sportsmanlike manner in which they had conducted themselves. (Applause.) He hoped the day was not far distant when with one or two more wins the cup would be looked union as belonging to Warrington and be presented to them. (Applause.) Mr. LESTER seconded. The committee ,were deserving of very greet praise and the best way they could show their appreciation would be to re-elect them. (Hear, hear). Mr. S. W. JEFFERY referred to the subscription of £2O to the Widnes Club. He did not know whether the gift was a new departure, but if it was to help a struggling club he did not think £2O was sufficient. They ought to have granted a sum more commensurate with the debt which they owed to Widnes and he *bought the committee would have been better advised, before making the grant, to have come to the general meeting for instructions. For his part, had they done so, he would have been quite prepared to propose a much larger sum. The PRESIDENT Said it was pleasant to find that the report and accounts were so satisfactory that there was so little for discussion. With regard to the gift of £2O it was a matter that the club could well leave in the hands of the committee: They had an application from Widnes for assistance and the committee in their wisdom voted £2O as a Suitable sum, and the Widnes Club was thankful. He did not know that the general meeting had anything to do with the matter. Mr. PtI,IBERTON said the whole of the West Lanca- shire clubs were appealed to and Warrington gave £2O to teach another and more wealthy club a lesson. That other club was in duty bound to help, Widnes as much as were Warrington and had given £5</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>ELECrION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECrION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>31IR.WARREN AND TEIE FUTURE OF THE UNION. Mr. PEMBERTON proposed the re-election of Mr. Warren as president. He had, he said, devoted a great amount of time during the past twelve months to foot- tall matters, and he would be proud to see him in the position of president again. (Applause.) Mr. T. WALLINGTON seconded, and said he had always been a strong advocate for the president of a largo club being one whom they could see. He was never in favour of one being elected who was merely a figure head, so to speak. (Laughter.) The motion was unanimously and heartily carried. _ .</p>
        <p>_ Mr. WARREN, in reply, said lie thanked the meeting uery cordially for the honour which had been conferred upon him in electing him President for the second time in succesion. Circumstances had arisen under which he would have preferred not to• take the position for another year, but, considering that the election had been so unanimous, he must accept the office, although if a suitable man had been put up against him he should have felt inclined to have withdrawn in his favour. He felt he must refer to the gentleman who occupied the post of President last year, namely Mr. Niel. The club was the poorer for the loss of such a man. (Elear,hear). He should also like to allude to another gentleman _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>whose death was a loss to the club, Mr. George Smttla,a hard working member of the committee at one time,and +me who was very much esteemed. (Hear' hear). He (the President) had never worked with a better com- mittee, who set a good example to all public bodies. The attitude of the players generally, had been courtesy itself. They had had a difficulty with some of the players, but their treatment of him had been that of gentlemen, and the members generally hid also shown -Lim the greatest rsspect. Twelve months ago they had some,discussion as to the future of the Northern Union and he then suggested an amalgamation between the Northern and Rugby Unions. That had not made much progress but still there had been some movement. The first thing they undertook was to convict the Rugby • Union of professionalism and they were now on a very fair way towards accomplishing the object and in the end it might lead to that bringing together of the two Unions to present a united from that to the constant encroachments of the Assoiciation code. (Hear, hear). It had been suggested that they should endeavour to win the League. It would be a great. honour to do so. The cup and the league had been won, but it was a tremendous feat. They ought next year to concentrate their energies on winning the League championship— (lend applause)—and from the beginning make every match a cup tie, and if they succeeded no one would be prouder than their President. (Applause). Mr. S. W. JEFFERY proposed the re-election of Mr. Pemberton as hon. treasurer, Mr. Ashton as hon. secre- tary, and Messrs. T, Wallington and W. Webster as hon. auditors. He said it would be impossible to improve</p>
        <p>on these gentlemen. Mr. Ashton was the right man in the right place, and they wanted one who had had experience on and off the field to steer them through the troubled waters which were ahead. Mr. BAWLING seconded, and the motion was adopted.</p>
        <p>_ The retiring members of the committee, Messrs. T. Bowers, T. Davies, W. Hewitt, T. Locker, W. Chalmers, P. Barber, were re-elected, the voting being :—Bowers, 217; Locker, 209 ; Hewitt. 206; Barber, 203 ; Chalmers, :169; Eavies, 162; Nevins, 105; and Plinston 44. Mr. .1. T. Thornley had been nominated but withdrew. Messrs. Bowers and Locker were re-appointed trustees.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>MEDAL FOR JORDAN.</head>
        <p>MEDAL FOR JORDAN.</p>
        <p>The PRESIDENT said he was pleased to ii,form the committee that the Northern Union had given per- mission to the club to present E. Jordan with a cup medal. (Hear, hear, and applause). A MEMBER asked why it was necessary to specially mention that matter. Jordan was entitled to a medal as he had figured in earlier rounds of the cup tie. It was the same thing every year—local lads did not get</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
