      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>WORKS COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>WORKS COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. HOPB suggested that in the future instead of running a Works Competition at the fag end of the season a Works League should be formed and run throughout the football season. The SECRETARY said there would be the greatest difficulty in getting grounds, and in the past it had been found that when the bottom clubs found they bad no chance of getting into a respectable position they gave up. Clubs had made application to the committee, who had granted them 412 or £3 to purchase outfits, and it had done no good whatever. He (Mr. Ashton) wished something of the kind could be done, as it would form a nucleus for players qior the Warrington club. In the Northern Union at the present time there was a dearth of such competitions, and the game was tabooed by the police and the army, and the younger player who desired to play Rugby had no opportunity. On the other hand the Association game was encouraged in schools, and shields and medals were forthcoming for competitions in all directions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>SMOKER TO BE HELD.</head>
        <p>A SMOKER TO BE HELD.</p>
        <p>Some discussion took place as to the celebration of the cup victory, a smoker and a supper being suggested. The PRESIDENT said the suggestion to provide a supper was a big thing, as there were over 90,0 members in the club.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. EtA.YES asked what Was the cost of the last social.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY replied that it was ,EGO odd. Mr. HAYES said he thought a smoker or social was a good method of keeping the members together. (Hear, hear.) He proposed that a smoker' be held, and that the arrangements be made by the committee.</p>
        <p>Mr. AI.COCK seconded. - An amendment was made that a supper be held instead, and it was seconded. The SECRETARY: Were would you hold it? (Hear. hear.) Where is there a place large enough to hold 920 members? (A Voice: Parr Hall.") It would prob- ably run the club into an expenditure of £lOO. Mr. G. Smrra said they had had enough of spending money. As Mr. Jeffery had said, there were troubled waters ahead and they should watch the expenditure even if only with the object of helping clubs like Widnes. (Hear, hear.) Ultimately the amendment was withdrawn aw:l the motion approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>"r HERITAGE OF DIFFICULTY."</head>
        <p>"r HERITAGE OF DIFFICULTY."</p>
        <p>Liberals Congratulate the Governmeat</p>
        <p>on its Success.</p>
        <p>The report to be presented to the meetitg of the National Liberal Federation at Plymouth next mouth pays a warm tribute to the work of Sir Edward Grey at the Foreign Office. "This agreement as to foreign affairs, unhappily, does not extend to the Colonial Office," the report proceeds, a though, as a fact, the greatest achievement of the present Government is in connection with the office over which Lord Elgin presides. "The heritage to which the Government succeeded in South Africa was one of the utmost difficulty, but a marvellous transformation has been effected by the fearless application of Liberal principles as exemplified by the grant of representative and responsible Govern- ment to the Transvaal, to be followed by a similar grant to the Orange River Colony. , "It was in the teeth of bitter Tory opposition that ' the Lyttelton Constitution was replaced by a new con- stitution, as the result of which General Botha attended the Colonial Conference, the loyal Premier of a loyal colopy." A review of the home legislation of the Government follows, and, after describing the action of the House of lsords, the report says : "Lord Crewe has made it elm that the Government is not, concerned with the constitution of the House of Lords, but with its power over legislation. The problem is to adjust the relations between the two houses so as to secure that the opinion of the country, expressed though the House of Com- mons, shall not be thwarted, checked, and flouted by a hereditary House of Lords, responsible to nobody. The problem is no light one, but it must be solved, unless the country is content to be governed permanently by the Tory party, through the action of the House of Lords." The Irish Council Bill is chars,ctArised as "a measure which honourably redeems the pledges on the Irish question made by the Liberal Government before the last general election."</p>
        <p>NATIONAL FREE CHURCH COUNCIL AND THE HAGUE CONFERENCE. LETTER FROM DR. J. RENDEL HARRIS.</p>
        <p>The President of the National Council of the Evangelical Free Churches, Dr. J. Rendel Harris, has fssued a letter on the forthcoming Hague Conference to every Minister of the Free Churches of England and Wales in which be says :—" The practical things to work for at the present time appear to be five (1) The removal by mediation of those international difficulties which lead to expensive naval and military armaments ; (2) the limitation and ultimate reduction of armaments; (3) the compulsory delay of incipient hostilities; (4) the increase of personal civic and national intercourse with the rest of the world ; (3) the earnest opposition to the spread of the military spirit at home. Of these .the three first belong to Statecraft, and will no doubt be the subjects of the deliberations of the Hague Con- ference. We can do nothing except the outsider's part in these matters; but who shall say that the praying Christian is ever an outsider? It is his habit to move the Hand that moves the world : and the hearts of Kings are in the hands of Him whom we serve. We can bear our testimony, we can practise our intercession, and we can make public profession of our faiths and hopes. The two other points are personal and practical; we can all do something to make the world one, and to remove the term foreigner from our vocabulary; and while we cannot acoomplish much by festivities or dine and wine men into the millenium we can practise the. Christian grace of cordiality and the Christian virtue of hospitality. But 'where can do most is where the adverse power is most busy; we can resist and oppose the military developments that are being forced upon us, from the top of society to the bottom ; we can save our schools from being turned into barracks, and our playing fields into rifle ranges ; we can educate the young to save life and not to destroy it."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>• TRAD.R UNION RESTRAINT. TOO MANY SOCIETIES:</head>
        <p>• TRAD.R UNION RESTRAINT. TOO MANY SOCIETIES:</p>
        <p>A circular was issued on Tuesday to trade unions affiliated to the Trade Union Congress. the Trades Federation, and ,the Labour party in connection with the recent agitation for restricting the number of unions formed for similar objects dealing with general (or unskilled) labourers. The circular is signed by Mr. W. C. Steadman, M.P., Mr. J. Ramsay Macdonald, M.P., and Mr. Mitchell, secretaries of the joint board of the three organisations named, and it points out that since the decision of the board on January 9 last, attempts have been made to form another t.nion catering more particularly for principal workers, and calls upon all trade organisations to support the joint board by refus- ing in any way to countenance any new trade union unle.s its foimatien has been endorsed by the joint board. 4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>WOMAN'S REALM.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>A WOMAN'S UNIVERSITY IN TURKEY.</p>
        <p>The University for women students in Con- stantinople, established according to the example of American colleges, is shewing a good attend- ance. One hundred and twenty-two students have graduated at that University, and more than iialf of that number aro actively engaged in their different professions. The curriculum is based on that of the American universities, so far as Oriental customs will permit. The official 4Anguage used by the tutors is English, and the teaching 'staff is composed mostly of American ladies. There are at present 160 students in the college, of whom eighty are boarders and eighty daily attending students. The nationalities are varied, students are drawn from many countries. Greece and Albania send some, so does Asia Minor from the borders of the Tigris and the Euphrates; they also come from Egypt and Spam, from Roumania and Bulgaria, and even from Russia.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>111ERCERI8ED Liwx DRESS</head>
        <p>111ERCERI8ED Liwx DRESS</p>
        <p>The accompanying sketch chews a dainty little dress of pale green mercerised lawn. The full bebe, bodice in finely gathered on to a yoke of Valenciennes lace, and is prettily trimmed with straps of lawn edged with narrow Valenciennes lace. The straps are taken over the shoulders to the waist, back and front, the centre one finishing</p>
        <p>in a point at the yoke. There is also a lace- edged strap down the side of the full sleeve, which finishes below the elbow with a lace-edged cuff to correspond. The skirt is very full, and is pleated in at the waist, the hem being finished with three large tucks. With the dress is worn a hat of pale biscuit-cololired straw, trimmed with large clusters of cream-white roses, and a band of We green ribbon round the crown.</p>
