      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>Of all the rOles which a modern woman can fill, perhaps the most noble, the most remarkable, and the most grateful, declares the World, is that of the perfect hostess. There may be numerous exceptions, says Ideas, but the business girl is not the ideal wife for the business man. The intimate experience of shop or commercial life is n6t always an acceptable accomplishment in a mate. "What a bright little thing!" exclaimed the society Woman, patronisingly cooing at a baby out for an airing in the park, says a Philadelphia paper. "Whose little one is this?" "Yours, ma'am," replied the nurse. "I'm the new nurse that kern pstherd'iy." A NEW SALAD.</p>
        <p>Vegetarian dishes are now so much in favour that I shall not be reproached, I know, says "Hobo" in tho Gentlewoman, for telling of a new salad for warm weather that is nourishing as well as refreshing. Boil for twenty minutes a cup of rice (Carolina); rinse in boiling water, 'strain, and put on one side to get evld. Mix two tablespoonfuls of finest olive oil and one of vine- gar with a suspicion of made mustard, salt and pepper to taste, and -a dash of castor sugar. Pont- over the rice and add chervil and tarragon ever so finely chopped, or neatly cut dice of tomato, celery, and beetroot may be substituted for the chervil and tarragon. Best of all cold (cooked) new peas, when the dressing should have very finely-chopped mint as its addition. • THE ÜBIUITOH3 • " PINAFORE."</p>
        <p>In our illustration is shown a charming Pina- fore blouse, which could also be worn with &amp; skirt of the same material, making a becoming complete dress. It could be carried out in almost any plain material, but is especially intended for Shantung silk, either in it natural colour or in one of the many beautiful colours that are now to be obtained. The blouse is arranged with two largo tucks, to hang well over the shoulders, this</p>
        <p>/11</p>
        <p>short cape-like sleeves being simply hemmed at the edge, The plain fronts of the blouse are adorned with large buttonholes, through which is threaded a soft, satin ribbon a shade or two darker than the material. The ribbon is left to hang leose in front, and is finiahed with ribbon- covered balls. teneath the Pinafore blouse is' worn a full blouse of lace or ring-spotted net, the full sleeves being finidhod with a small puff and a narrow frill at the elbow.</p>
