      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>I National Floral Emblems.—The difficulty of hitting upop a floral emblem for the Empire, seems hardly to be met by Lord Meath's suggestion of the common daisy for this' purpose. It is true it grows in all parts of the Empire, but the daisy is not beloved by gardeners wbo pride themselves on their lawns, and who regard it as a weed to be uprooted and destroyed, wherever it is to be found. The cornflower does not synchronise with Empire Day, 24th May, and is already appropriated by Germany, or else th' would have made an appropriate floral emblem. We not however, the only people in t ; such a difficulty, as orway now that she has her own King and Queen i thinking of adopting a national ! floral emblem, although one would think she need not look beyond her own dark pine for this purpose. That would be as appropriate to her, as is the eidelweiss to Switzerland. In France it is the lily, the fleur-de-lys, which is also the national emblem of Italy, whilqt in this country we have the rose, shamrock, thistle and 1 leek, and the Canadian floral emblem is the maple leaf. j The Sassoon Family.—Lady Sassoon, whose portrait Iby M. Sargent is regarded as one of the masterpieces lin this year's Academy, is the daughter of the enor- I mouily wealthy Baron Gustane de Rothschild, and the 1 wife of Sir Edward Sassoon. When in London, Sir Edward and his wife reside in the mansion in Park-lane, which the late Mr. Barney Barnato had built, but was Ifated never to live in, and here Sir Edward and Lady Sassoon entertain on a princely scale. The Sassoons I have always been among the intimate friends of King !Edward, and when Prince of Wales, his Majesty was (frequently the guest of members of the family, all of ' whom have been noted for their lavish hospitality, and ! magnificent entertainments. One of the comparatively I recent country house visits of the King was to Mr. and 1 Mrs. Arthur Sassoon' at Fulchan, in the Highlands. I A General Field for Social Work—The Deptford I Fund, of which the annual meeting was held this week, : owes its success to the very active interest which is 1 taken in its progress by H.R.H. the Duchess of Albany. ! Some of the local people say that the fund would effect i a still greater amount of good if it were managed only Iby Deptford people, instead of beim governed very ; largely by aristocratic folks in the West End, who are : apt to be taken in when they drive to the locality to ' see how the people are getting on. But however that ! may be, the idea is a most admirable one, and there I would certainly be less misery in East London if those in high places would select each a portion of that • terrible Alsatia, and make it the object of their special interest. Perhaps the most practical and eseful phase ; of the work associated with the Deptford Institute, is I the club for girls. The district is a poor one, and therq I are many girls employed in factories, whose only means ' of rational recreation are those afforded by the institute. • Happily, the number of suchrclubs is increasing, not only ' in the poorer parts of London,but in the great industrial towns of the provinces, and it cannot be very. long , before the value of the work which they are able to I accomplish, is recognised everywhere. . ; Dances.—Dancing is as popular as ever with the rising . i generation, and Society hostesses, who, like the Duchess ' of Northumberland and Lady Cairns, make a point of I giving dances for young yeople, never have any fear for the success of their entertainments. Even the fashion- able man who for some long time past has been too bored and limp to make himself agreeable at dances, ' has to some extent reformed, and a feature of the present season is the exceptional number of balls that ' are being given, and also fixed for June and July. Although card rooms are always available for the , devotees to Bridge, for once in a way hostesses are not i complaining of a dearth of dancing men and there are , many charming d6butantes who are looking forward to : their first ball this season. One of the prettiest is Lady ' Rosemary Cairns, who has inherited the remarkable I beauty of her mother, Olivia, Countess Cairns. 1 Few Shapes and Many Trimmings.—An economical ' ides for the girl who is always trying to make every 1 shilling of her dress allowance go as far as eighteen- pence, is to purchase one becoming cream, or slightly I " burnt " straw hat for slimmer wear, mad a silk scarf to match. each linen and muslin gown. The scarf should be fringed at the ends, and tied in a bow behind, the I brim at the back being bent down to allow the fringed 1 ends to fall partly—only partly over the hair. By changing the scarf, such a hat could be worn with any I costume, and for more dressy occasions a floral mount I could even be substituted. 'Very few hats' and many trimmings, is a plan of campaign worthy of the atten- tion of the impecunious, as thereby it is possible to ring the necessary changes, at much less cost, and in I much better style, than when several trimmed hats of ; the cheap, and consequently very nasty persuasion, are I indulged in. For the fashionable fringed scarf, crepe de I chine, or soft silk ribbon should be obtained, of the I length and width required, also fringe to match, which I , must be neatly applied to .esch end. Thus, two scarves : are possible, at the price of one,—as demanded in shops i land.</p>
        <p>Concerning Foliage.—The progress made during the past year or two, in the arc of flower-making, is truly wonderful, and of late, considerable attention has been given'to foliage. Not so very long ago almost any kind of unnatural greenery was tolerated, but now-a- days we are quite as particular in our choice Of the foliage, as van are in the selection of the flowers them- selves. With all wild flowers, our humble friend the carrot top, faithfully and perfectly copied, is greatly to the fore, so also are the leaves of the wild geranium, but more modish than all perhaps are the green and white striped grasses, with sharp points. rany of the newest hats are trimmed solely with foliage, and very successful they prove, when worn with a green gown. Shunting Silk.—The demand for our old friend tussore silk, which by the way is now known more generally as shunting, goes on apace, and as is usual when the demand is great, prices have risen consider- ably. A silk we could have obtained a year sa'o for half-a-crown, is now three and sixpence, and those individuals who have a length or two in store May be considered fortunate. Those among us too, who have friends or relatives in China, and who readily take a hint, are in luck's way. They should immediately be given to understand that a length of Shunting would prove acceptable, as would also some of the wonderful Chinese embroideries so greatly in request just now. The Masculine Straw. Hat.—lt is commonly supposed that men are too sensible to be made slaves to fashion, but the masculine straw hat of the season does not bear out this supposition, it having a very narrow brim, which is made to look still more diminutive by the hat itself being high in crown. Presumably men wear straw hats in summer because they are lighter and cooler than other styles, but there is no justification for the straw hat that does rot shade the eyes and keep off the sun. From a ntilitarian noint of view, it is an absurdity, and yet we find men quite as ready as women to sacrifice commonsense and comfort to atyle and fashion in their attire. How to Walk.—Tn order 'to preserve a graceful figure good walking and a correct balance are essential. The shoulders should be thrown back, the spine straightened and the back just below the waist line curved inwards. The leg should swing freely from the hip joint, and the weight of the body should be thrown upon the halls of the feet, not upon the heels. Correct walking means a light, elastic, springing step, and a corresponding sense of freedom of action. If one walks chrrectly one does not easily get tired, and the'exercise is beneficial to the whole frame, bringing the muscles into vigorous action, and aiding the proper circulation of the blond. MADGE.</p>
        <p>,p.-_-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>CHRONIC COUGHS.</head>
        <p>CHRONIC COUGHS.</p>
        <p>AND OLD-AGE BRONCHITIS. LADY OF 70 CURED.</p>
        <p>The following testimony to the efficacy of Veno's Lightning Cough Cure, especially in coughs, &amp;c., so common in old age, is interesting. Mr. Thos. H. Puntis a well-known tradesman, residing at 75, Mount Pleasant Road, Southampton, says :—" It is with the utmost pleasure that I write to tell you of some remarkable men by your great Cough Cure. During the last three years my wife and seven of my children have at different times all been cured of nasty colds and coughs by your medicine. Last winter, also, my wife's mother—au old lady of 70 years—was taken very ill with a bad cough, which became chronic, and the doctor said she would never get better. However, Verto's Lightning Cough Cure has entirely cured her at last, and she says that she would not have lived if she had not taken it. _ - -</p>
        <p>....... "Recently, lam thankful to say, slimy household have checked the inflenza by taking your valuable remedy I have spent pounds on other so-called cough cures, but but never derived any benefit till I triel Veno's. I thank you for saving me many a doctor's bill." Veno's Lightning Cough Cure is free freAu all danger- ous drugs, and is the safest, purest, and most effective remedy extant for coughs, colds, bronchitis, asthma, catarrh, whooping cough, and all chest and lung troubles. Price 94. d. Is. 11c1. and 2.5. 9d. of all chemists and stores everywhere.</p>
        <p>The late General the Hon. Sir Robert Rollo, Colonel of the Black Watch, has bequeathed £1,500 to the Wanehope and Black Watch Memorial Home for Soldiers, at Perth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <p>and stifled his half-articulated call for help with a couple of lace antimacassars, which he stuffed into his throat, while the Greaser tied him up with the velvet curtain cords. ; They waited no longer, but pushing a heavy oak cabinet against the door, the trio, care- fully taking the Murillo, hurried through the open window into the park as cries re- sounded through the hall, for the butler's shots had aroused the household. A handker- chief, knotted above the bullet-wound of the One-eyed Man, served until more efficient aid could be given. Figures shewed through the trees in pursuit, but the trio reached the Panhard safely and were soon beyond the reach of their pursuers. A significant fact was that whereas when they came the Num- bers read •A. M. 231, it was now S. H. 611. So, even if it had been noticed, the new, number would hide its identity. They carried a number of fictitious plates and numbers that fitted a specially-contrived slot. The car was run through Riscall to Selby and on through Howden and Ferriby to Hull. At Ferriby its number was again changed, in ease it had been noticed in the early morning passing through Selby. The Greaser was dropped at Hull with his precious burden, which was simply rolled up in .a piece of oil- cloth, so that there was absolutely no atten- tion called to a man who carried such a pack- age. The disguise was admirable. He took the first morning train from the station to Hornsen,, arriving there about 6.45 a.m. Leaving the station, he walked briskly along the cliffs, keeping a bright lookout sea- ward. He had not long to wait, for soon s speck shewed upon the water, and with his glass he descried the Submarine. A few minutes later the collapsible boat was shoved off in answer to his signal; then, hurrying down to the shore, he was met by two of the crew.</p>
        <p>Handing them the roll of oilcloth, he lade them be careful, and complimented them upon being in such good time. We were ordered," 'said the leading man, "to be a mile and a-half east'ard of Horn- sea at 7 a.m., Wednesday, the 19th, and, being orders, we're 'ere." "Good !"- said the Greaser. "You shall be well paid." The man grinned. "We saw you coming, sir, so was all ready. This is good!" he pointed to the oilcloth. "Yes," said the Greaser; "a roll of can- vas would attract attention, but a roll of oil- cloth can easily pass muster." They got in the boat and were rapidly rowed to the waiting Submarine. _</p>
        <p>"I wish to be 'landed at Robin Hood's Bay," said the Greaser. "I am going to Whitby, and prefer to walk in." This was done. The Greaser was dropped ashore at a quiet spot, and by 10 a.m. was in Whitby. •</p>
        <p>The. Panhard had a successful trip south, and the Greaser went to Harrogate the next day. He was a fool, he knew, to play with fire, but he vent, and had spent a happy fort- night in the company of the fair American .when a message arrived asking him to repair at once to Paris as important business was under discussion. That night he bade his final adieu to Mamie Chessleridge. That she cared for him he knew, that Ile cared for her he knew only too well, but he shut his lips and told her only that his holiday was over. He crushed his hopes under his iron will. "You are sorry?" he. said, quietly.</p>
        <p>"Yes," she mUrmured; "but I all sure to see you again. You must see us in the States. After these pleasant days, Mr. Rising, we must not 'close so happy an acquaintance- ship." "You are more than kind, Miss Chessle- ridge, but I'm afraid we must say our adieux permanently. I go abroad at once, and my return ls not even conjecture." "I had hoped—" She stopped, a trifle nervously. "I may not,'? he said, "I may not. It is because of this I go, Mamie." He used her name for the first time, and the colour ran red to her hair. "You must find a better man than I, and I pray you to forget Eduard Rising. Forget he lived a mouth in your life. Forget his memory even." " Why? " she whispered. "If I—if I will not?"</p>
        <p>"Listen," he said. "I dare not, for your sweet sake, link your life with mine. lam not a good man, I am a desperately wicked one. This is my confessional." "Hush!" she cried. "Hush! Ted, you are good enough for me." She smiled in an inno- cent triumph that cut him to the heart. Never had he seen himself so black, never so hope- lessly lost.</p>
        <p>"1 am wicked, Mamie, wicked. I have sown the wind, and now I reap the whirlwind. I am a criminal."</p>
        <p>For the moment she swayed, then she looked at him tenderly. "Ted," she said, sweetly, "there is always hope. I love you. Come with me. If I am willing who else should mind? See, take me with you." She held out her hands imploringly. "I am a criminal, Mamie, but I am not a villain. No woman has ever suffered through me, and none shall. I wrong no girl, no woman. You do not know me—l -am one of the worst."</p>
        <p>She shuddered violently, but her hands still clasped his, and her tears fell silently. "I have broken your heart," he said, hoarsely—" I have broken your heart." "Ted," she whispered, "may I reclaim you?" The passion in her voice shook his will. Cut himself adrift from all, from the gang, his crimes, and his evil ways? Start with a new world before him in some quiet place abroad? Ah ! there was a path opening indeed. Then, like a flash, there came before his "ming the deaths that lay upon his soul—a river that might not be bridged by love itself. No, he could never bring her to that. Criminal and worse he might be, ' but villain and black- guard never—to a girl. "Mamie," he said, "I may not. I cannot. Forget me. Some day another and a better man will stand with you as I stand to-day. Pray for me, if you can, for your prayers shoitld avail Much. Adieu, sweetheart, adieu." He kissed. her gently, then, turning swiftly, he left his heaven behind him and walked on to the road he had cut out, for himself, and which he must tread alone.</p>
        <p>Strange to say a smile was au his face, and his step was light and free; his heart was full of joy. He had been sorely tempted, but the last spark of human kindness in his nature had been wakened to life. He had ~fought and won, and for once the Greaser was a gentle- man in the full sense of the word.</p>
        <p>[Next week: "THE PERIL OF VIE SUB- MARINE," a thrilling tale of s midnight chase in the Channel.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>HERO OF LUCKNOW.</head>
        <p>A HERO OF LUCKNOW.</p>
        <p>Sir Joseph Fayrer, physician extraordinary to the Ring, who died at the age of 820 n Tuesday at Falmouth lived one of the fullest lives which can fall to the lot man.—After serving in the Burmese War Sir Joseph was appointed Residency surgeon at Lucknow. There he witnessed all the troubles which led up to the mutiny and went through the terrible seige of Lucknow. His house was both a hospital and a fortress, and he was both soldier and surgeon. One by one he ticked off on the wall of his house the names of those that fell.— After the trouble was over he diddurther work in India before returning home to settle down. ' But he was soon beck again, accompanying the Duke of Edinburgh. This honour was followed by a greater one, for he was selected to accompany the King. He was with the King when he shot Mg first tiger, and personally showed him the gruesome tally of the dead on the wall of his house in Lucknow. When he retired from the presidency of the Medical Board at the India Office be took with him a baronetcy and the rank of sergeon-general. He was to have been made a fieeman of Falmouth in July.</p>
