      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 25, 1907.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 25, 1907.</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.] clear of the muddy way, but he was too sincere to follow her. THE LITTLE SAVAGE Only then did be realise that he was no better • off than before. He knew neither her name nor I her address. They had apparently no mutual BY ALFRED TENNYSON, acquaintances. She taught in a school. In all probability there were some twenty schools in Author of "A Legend of the Shires," "The Poissy. Fate seemed determined to prevent their Baronet," "The Little Princess," "Sir, nearer acquaintance. He was doomed in future, Jasper," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>he supposed, to fall back on those daily encounters, once so delightful, but in the future so insipid now that he had heard her voice and looked into her eyes. At most could he expect a frigid conventional 'bow; but even in this he was disappointed. The next day he came by the street of the garden walls, and the next, and the next, and for many days after, but not a sign of her did he see. He paced most frequented and the emptiest streets, and consumed his leisure hours in tramping the town, but he caught no glimpse of the slender figure and the proudly set little head. He made searching inquiries, but made also the discovery that the schoohnis,resses of Poissy were more jealousy guarded than the ladies of a Sultan's harem. He became at last quite melancholy, and deserted the salle-d'-arines so many evenings running that M. Legros, the good - natured fencing master, made a call in person to see if he was ill. After that he had a few days of feverish energy. He practised hard with ep.,.e and fieuret, and had a terrific encounter with M. Pinaudau, the Protestant pasteur, who was considered the most cunning tireur in the cerele, in which the latter gentleman was easily worsted. ! "By the way, mon cher," said M. Pinaudau, resting in rather a melting condition after his bout—for he was a gentleman of considerable proportions—"ma femme commissioned me to ask you to dinner on Thursday; a petite anglaise, very charming, also dines with us. "Indeed ? " said Thompson rather languidly, as he was no enthusiast where French family parties were concerned. i "Oh, yes," continued the pasteur, amiably, ! "excessively charming, but savage—c' est epatant Believe me this is the fourth time that she is invited and the first time only that she comes. Bali! But the English have no society—they love , only the sport." "Who is she, then, this young English lady ? " ! asked Thompson. "It is a little miss who has lost both her parents and makes her living by teaching in a , school here in the Place Canrobert. The wife is most anxious that you should come, for she believes that the miss amuses herself but little." Thompson felt excitement and delight tug wildly at his heartstrings. Could it be that fortune was on his side at last, and that he was ion the point of finding his little friend once more? The Rushed, bearded countenance of M. Pinaudau appeared to him in a sort of I nimbus like the countenance of an angel bringing a message of good tidings. He felt an inclination, which he happily restrained, to clasp the stout pastor to his heart and kiss him fervently on both cheeks in the foreign fashion' but he answered , with a calm which surprised himself: "Please make my compliments to madame, ! monsieur, and tell her that I shall be delighted I to come."</p>
        <p>Poissy is a long, straggling town, which strays unmethodically up and down hill, about eight miles from the coast of Brittainy. No one who walked for the first time down the principal street, the Rue de Crdbillon, would imagine that it was the main artery of a populous city. In this street, cart, carriage, and automobile are mixed in a clamorous and endless confusion, and if it is a holiday, the babel is further com- plicated by a continual stream of vociferous and dilatory foot-passengers. Every now and then a warning toot clears a lane through the moving mass, and a tram is allowed to go by with its impassive official at the helm, more or less unimpeded, though frantic drivers and refractory horses struggle half on the pavement and half in the street. Above the noise of bicycle bell and motor horn or the voices of passing humanity sounds the stunning rattle of wheel on cobble- stone, and in the quieter streets one can hear the noise of the traffic as if it were the roar of a high sea on a beach. At the end of the Rue de Cribillon and at the foot of the hill runs the canal, a picturesque alley of water which stagnates between an avenue of trees and diffuses a faint but unmistakable odour in its immediate vicinity. The streets are all narrow, ill-lighted, and ill-paved, the drains a positive and poignant offence in warm weather, the houses shabby and unlovely. Certainly someone is needed to im- prove and beautify Poisay, according to modern standards, into spacious thoroughfares and imposing squares. The truth is that it has grown too rapidly with the increase of manu- facture. Eighty years ago it was a small but historic town, celebrated in various eras of political and religious persecution. Now, if the place is known to fame outside the schools, it is for the flourishing trade in biscuits, cheap wines, glassware, &amp;c., which it carries on with other countries. It was owing to the fact that Great Britain had need of these products that Bernard Thompson was for the time being an inhabitant of Poissy. For nearly three months in the capacity of consul he had been in charge of the interests of the British colony. It was at the beginning of spring that he first saw the girl. He had been climbing one of those hilly streets which are so common in Poissy, and then at the corner he had met her. He remem- bered for days a slender form braced against the fresh blowing of the wind, with ankles indis- creetly peeping beneath a tempestuous petticoat and figtwe audaciously outlined by the uncivil blast. It. may have been a fortnight after when the second meeting took place, and it occurred to him that she might be in the habit of passing home that way. And so it came to be a regular feature of his life in Poissy, this afternoon walk, this brief glimpse of a hurrying figure. Though he always dressed his best (and Thompson was a man of excellent judgment with regard to male apparel) before making his diurnal expedition up the street that ran between high garden walls, over whose copings peered the tops of green trees, he came no nearer acquaintance with the girl. He passed her with scarce a look most often. In the first place, he had no idea who she was. She was not seen in the drawing-rooms of the elect, neither did her feet move in society. Even at that early stage of their acquaintance Thompson felt that it would be impossible to speak to her without a suffieient reason. He was sure that such attempts would gain a well-merited snub. In the end it was a mere chance of the weather that introduced them, rather than any spontaneous resolution on his part. It was one of those bright, mutable days of sunshine and shadow which are perhaps the most beautiful from an artistic point of view, but convey a menace in their very beauty. The sunshine which had lasted up till four o'clock was suddenly obscured by a flying scud of 'clouds. The rain descended with tropical suddenness, the foot-passengers ran for shelter to doorways and street corners. Those who were fortunate enough to have coats or umbrellas braved the squall with b‘nt figures and flying garments. Thompson, who had both, walked briskly forward, and in the violence of the storm nearly missed what he had come out to see. A girlish figure came swiftly down the hill towards him. The wind, chill and gusty, smote through the thin jacket, and the rain fell in fitful gusts. Thompson, comparatively warm and dry with his umbrella and overcoat, felt a sudden feeling of .pity for the girl. Her black dress seemed thinner and more worn than ever in the stabbing rain. With a sudden resolve and a brave disregard of convention, he offered her his umbrella in French words, indifferently chosen and pronounced. " Merei monsieur," began the girl, and then with a blush and downcast grey eyes she added: "Oh! but I am English, you know." "English ! " said Thompson, with a sudden inexplicable leap of the heart. "In that case you really must take my umbrella. I could not let you go home without it in this weather. Why, -you're wet through already," he added, with a glance at her sleeve. "Yes 'said the girl, "I suppose I am rather wet. I was foolish enough not to bring mine out this morning; it looked so fine when I started. But what will you do if I take yours ? " "Why, I have a thick overcoat,' and you have nothing but this pool: little jacket; but let me see you home, and you can return me the umbrella when we get to your door. I know it's a great liberty I'm taking, but I hated to see you looking 4n wet and cold." "I'm really most grateful, but I've got such a short way to go it seems hardly worth while." "It hardly seems worth while to get wetter than necessary. If you do not absolutely forbid it, I think I ought to prevent you from getting cold."</p>
        <p>Madame Pinaudau was a short, dark lady, inclined to embonpoint, with rather merry blank eyes and a Southern complexion of olive. Thompson made his bow and satisfied himself by a rapid glance round the room that no one else had arrived.</p>
        <p>"We dine quite en famille this evening, M. Thompson," said madame. "It appears that our little English lady is very shy, and she begged us to be quite alone. As a hostess, of course, I could not do that, but I have arranged the smallest possible party compatible with my conscience—" and she emphasised her words with a slight bow to Thompson—"so we expect you to amuse her very much and give her a great deal of attention."</p>
        <p>"You pay me a great compliment, madame," said Thompson, "but I'm afraid I shall not fulfil expectations. You know we English are not conversationalists."</p>
        <p>I"Well, I propose that you shall prove the i contrary this evening. Poor little girl! It is ' very sad for her. I'm afraid she is very poor and has to work hard to support herself, so it is almost impossible to get hold of her. We have been asked to be kind, but we could not make her come before. Truly she is, to all appearances' a real little savage." A ring at the door-bell made Madame Pinaudau pause and Bernard Thompson hold his breath. Those few moments in the passage seemed to him so many hours. He could hear the visitor taking off her wraps and talking in a low whisper to the maid. In the effort to obtain some measure of calm he fell to looking at the simple objects around him. His mind seized upon the merest trifles with a sense of relief. The vivacious French matron,sitting stiff and upright in her scat; the portlf kindly French pasteur ; the uncomfortable armchairs in their hideous, yellow coverings, planted each in its own place, more for show than for use, and so obviously used only on occasions seemed a queer setting to his emotion. Mechanically he rose to his feet as the door opened, and then—a rainbow cloud of delight passed before his eyes, dying everything with subtlest colours. It was she, the girl whom now he loved with a mad, irrational passion, born of the moment like all great inspirations. Madame Pinaudau rose. Her dark French face was lighted up with a smile very pleasant to see. She stepped quickly forward to the girl, who stood hesitating rather shyly by the door, and took both her hands in hers with a kindly gesture. "I am very glad to see you, ma chere," she said. "Do .you know my husband and our friend, M. Thompson ? " M. Thompson bowed in the manner that con- vention demands, and his face half-hidden and in the shadow escaped notice, but madame perceived to her surprise that the girl started and blushed vividly as the presentation was made. Perhaps she put it down to sauvagerie, perhaps to other things; but whatever her thoughts were she concealed them, for she was a wise woman. As for the evening, madame and monsieur would' have told you very likely that nothing Very particular occurred. The dinner was well cooked and punctual, and Therese got through the meal without dropping any dishes or upsetting any wineglasses. One of the party, however, if you could have shaken from him his impenetrable English reserve, might have told a very different tale. After this introduction Mademoiselle Long and Monsieur Thompson met more frequently, but their acquaintanceship did not progress A rapidly as the latter would perhaps have wished. Generally she was so cold and haughty that he was in despair at his efforts to please her. He began to say to himself that she was indeed the little savage which his French friends had proclaimed her to be. Strangely enough, unless Madame Pinaudau was given to matchmaking, it was nearly always at her house ,that they met; sometimes even she would make up a party for the theatre or the circus, to 'which they were both invited. In spite of everything, however, Thompson was obliged to confess to himself at the end of some three months that he had made little progress in his love affairs; indeed, if anything, she seemed to him to be colder than before. Yet it was no wonder that he cursed fate when some business summoned him away to a town at a considerable distance from Poissy for the space of three long weeks. He succeeded in seeing her before he went; but she was so indifferent to his departure that in the bitterness of his heart he was almost glad to go. The town which he had to visit was on the seaside. It was lovely summer weather. Day after day the sun shone with undimmed brilliance, and the waves looked their best in a garment of deep stainless blue, glittering with countless diamonds. All day long the sandy beach was gay with a multitude of fair shapes midi a corresponding number of wonderful party-coloured bathing toilets. There was a casino. Dances and concerts took place almost unceasingly, every- thing was bright and French. Thompson should have enjoyed himself hugely, but, as a matter of fact, he did not. A year ago he would have thrown himself heart and soul into the irrespon-1 sible life and liked every moment of it. To-day, he told himself bitterly, he was a changed man. The easy flirtations of the garden and the beach filled him with disgust, and he answered the kindly glances of attractive French ladies with like as not a sullen scowl. A fortnight did he ' pass at Bleueville-sur-Mer, and lie still thinks it was the wretchedest fortnight of his life. At the end of his second week he received a letter from Madame Pinaudau, which s(nt him back to Poissy by the next train regardless of conseqUence,s. "Cher monsieur," it began, ..(and then followed a number of more or less</p>
        <p>"It's really very kind of you," said the girl, in a\ last protest. Thompson's stronger will .prevailed, and she was soon walking by his side under the shelter of his umbrella.</p>
        <p>"I can't understand why I have never met you before," said he, half mendaciously, as they picked their way through the narrow, cobbled streets, flanked on either side by high, dripping walls, and ornamented at regular intervals with stunted trees. "I thought I knew most of the English people in Foissv." "Well, you see, I don't go much into society," answered the girl. "I am working more or less all day, and very often 1 have something to do in the evening as well." "Do you really work as hard as that ? " "My parents are both dead," she replied, with 3 glance at her black frock, "and they were not well enough off, poor dears, to leave me much money."</p>
        <p>"Arid do you liVo here, all alone IP." "Not quite; there is a Wench girl of about my own age, who shares my rooms with me." Thompson could not help feeling- that she spoke of her life without enthusiasm. The French girl could be in no sort a friend, he thought, and then, not without a sense that he was much too inquisitive, he asked what her work was. The girl hesitated a moment before replying.</p>
        <p>-"I fetich in a school here,." she(said. "Of course, they are glad to get somebody to talk and read English with the children, and at the same time I hope to pick upcii. little French, which will always come in useful." "And do you manage to like the life pretty well ?"</p>
        <p>"Oh, yes; there are bothers, of course, some- times, and one can't help getting a little worried occasionally, but on the whole, I get aiong well enough." Thompson stole a furtive -glance at the little face under the black hat. It seemed to be rather worried now; the cheeks were pale in spite of the keen east wind, and there were tired marks under the demure grey eyes. He would haN!e liked to offer some sort of assistance, and might perhaps have braved her displeasure, had not the silence been interrupted by his companion's voice. "I really won't drag you any further. I live only a few yards round the corner, and the rain has almost stopped how; the sun will be out again in S, few minutes, I think. So I will say good-bye, and many thanks for your kind- ness."</p>
        <p>She held out a little gloved hand, which Thompson grasped for a delicious moment in his big fist. The girl's honest eyes intimated too clearly that he had come quite far enough. "Good-bye," he said, and watched her trip across the road with uetticoats daintily lifted</p>
        <p>uninteresting details) ". . . have you heard of the good fortune of Miss Long? it appears a rich English lady, wanting a companion, has offered her the post. She has almost decided to Lake it, though it is not the sort of thing be would have wished to do. I have advised her to accept." It was late on Saturday afternoon, but Thomp- son packed his things, drove off in a=fly to the station at a break-neck pace, and caught the last train to Poissy. Directly he arrived he drove straight to the Pinandaus' house. Madame received him placidly. "We did not expect you so soon," she said, after the first greetings had been exchanged. "Have you been at home long ? " "I have this minute arrived."</p>
        <p>Madame expressed appreciation of the promptitude of his visit, and launched into a variety of topics, none of which, however, was the one Thompson wanted to hear most. It was some time before he had an opportunity of approaching the subject, so bewildering was madame's volubility. At last he was able to ejaculate: "It was your letter which , brought me here, madame."</p>
        <p>"Indeed! "she replied. "And why, may I ask ? " "Why? What you wrote about Mademoiselle Long. I must see her before she goes." \ "That is awkward. She had arranged to go on Monday." "Sunday, then, I must see her. Will you invite her here ? "</p>
        <p>"Monsieur, you surprise me! Certainly I should not dream of doing it." "Very well, I must follow her to Calais." "Ca strait inconvenant. Stay, there is the church." "The church! What do you mean?"</p>
        <p>"She always comes to mo-rning service, as you ought to know. Very well, then, I will never tell my husband." "Madame, you are an angel." "Monsieur! . . . Remember, it ,is the third pew on the left," said Madame Pinaudau.</p>
        <p>Thompson waited long in the porch next Sunday morning. A crowd of acquaintances streamed in, wearing strictly Sabbatical garments. One or two noticed his tall figure and spoke to him, but his answers were vague and unsatis- factory, and the conversations were not unduly prolonged. It seemed ages before he saw the girlish figure in the black frock coming towards the door. She did not notice him, and went straight to her place in the church. Thompson followed and took a seat in the pew behind her. The simple French Protestant service began. M. Pineudau walked to the pulpit in his plain black gown and read the prayers in a straight- forward, stentorian voice. He was an eloquent extempore preacher, and, as the custom of Protestant churches demands that a certain number of prayers - composed for the occasion shall be given, he was quite in his element on Sundays. The congregation, according to another custom, stood instead of kneeling while this part of the service was proceeding, and listened attentively and almost critically to the fervid utterances of their pasteur. Miss Long, to all appearances, was as much absorbed as the rest. Her eyes had never wandered at all during the first part of the service. There was a rustle of turning leaves as M. Pinaudau gave out the hymn and the congregation took their seats. Miss Long searched everywhere for a book—in the pocket of her dress, on the little shelf in front, on the floor, and then turned round. Her face went scarlet as she met the eyes of Thompson, who, with a grave inclination, handed her his. The girl bowed, mutely thanked him, and then dropped her glance to the book; then, with a deeper blush, she suddenly turned towards the paeteur in the pulpit once more. She tried to sing the hymn-tune with the rest of the congregation, but her eyes would wander to the words written in straggling pencilled letters across the printed page: "I love you. Will you marry me ? " The organ played the voluntary while she was still hardly conscious of the lapse of time since the momentous question had so unexpectedly been put for her to answer. She filed slowly with the other worshippers from the church, and her mind was still in a sweet confusion. Thompson was waiting for her at the top of the steps. His eyes now asked the question, and she was obliged to whisper "Yes." Thus it happened that a certain old English lady lost a companion while Thompson gained one. To this day Madame Pinaudau loves to dilate to her acquaintances (they live in Paris now) on the English lack of spirit. "I assure you, my dear," she said, "had it not been for me it would never have been arranged. Ah, those English! Often I ask myself however they manage in London. If they take so lone a time when there are only two, how long do they take where there are four million. Why, M. Pinaudau--" But further confidences were doubtless meant only for her immediate circle of aequaintance, and any revelations of Madame Pinaudau's romance would probably be in- discreet.</p>
        <p>Such is the composition of the human mind that sometimes even Thompson and the little savage, as he still sometimes calls her, are moved to laughter at pertain recollections of this epoch in their lives. She, in particular, can always be stirred to mirth at the mention of M. Pinaudau's English sermon at the wedding. But she never fails to add in her shy, kindly way: *Dear man. It was so amiable of him to try, that it is a shame to laugh at him." Madame Pinaudau, that shrewd dame, protested against it, it is true, but for once in his life M. Pinaudau was adamant. "C' eat use belle chance," was the only comment he vouchsafed on the attain</p>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS</p>
        <p>A SILVER THIMBLE BATTERY.</p>
        <p>The amount of electricity required for the working of an Atlantic. cable is surprisingly small. Generally a few cells, like those used for electric bells, are emplo:)cd. There may be from five to 100 of .these. And a mes- sage was sent over one of the earliest Atlantic cables by means of a battery con- sisting of a silver thimble in which was a, frag- ment of zinc, and a few drops of sulphuric- acid.</p>
