      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>[THE END.]</head>
        <p>[THE END.]</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES</p>
        <p>St. Peter's Church.</p>
        <p>There was a large congregation at St. Peter's Church, on Sunday evening, when the Rev B. Percival (vicar) preached for the first time since his recent serious illness. He took for his text the words, "And they said to another, did not our heart burn within us, while 'talked with us by the way, and while he opened to us the Scriptures ?" After expressing his thankfulness for his restoratian to health, the rev, gentleman thanked the church officers and all who had to do with the upkeep of the work in the parish during his absence, and also expressed his indebtedness to his colleague (Rev. H. Russell) for his kind and efficient help. The service was of a hearty character, and hymns appro- priated to the occasion were sung. The choir rendered the anthem " O'clap your hands." Mr. F. Webster was at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Sunday School Anniversary.</head>
        <p>Sunday School Anniversary.</p>
        <p>There were large congregations at the Golborne- street Baptist Church on Sunday when the Sunday school anniversary services were hold. The Rev. D. W. Roberts, of Glasgow, a former pastor occupied the pulpit. In the morning he spoke upop the words "For me to live is Christ," and in the evening he gave a special sermon to young people upon the subject One of the Greatest Needs of the 'Age." In the afternoon there was a scholars' service when Mr. Thomas Dale occupied the chair. Mr. David R. Waddell, of Liver- pool, delivered an address on the good work done by Sunday schools, and appealed to all to assist in the cause. Miss J. Cruikshank and Mr. J. Osborne rendered solos in an effective manner. The scholars sang special hymns at each service, Mr. A. Spruce conducted, Miss Hankey was'at the piano and Mr. S. Rickers at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>RECORD EXHIBITION SUCCESSES.</head>
        <p>RECORD EXHIBITION SUCCESSES.</p>
        <p>Official notification has reached Messrs. Raphael Tuck and Sons Ltd. from the Committee of the New Zealand International Exhibition to the effect that the firm have been awarded three gold medals in the three sections of their exhibits at this Exhibition, viz. :—A gold medal for postcards and Christmas cards ; a gold medal for engravings, photogravures, art novelties, &amp;c.; a gold medal for calendars, juvenile gift books, toy books, &amp;c. This- sweep of the board it is believed constitutes a record—certainly in Messrs. Tuck's branch of trade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>FROM EARTH TO CLOUD.</head>
        <p>FROM EARTH TO CLOUD.</p>
        <p>The application of instantaneous photo- graphy to lightning flashes has rendered it possible to say in what direction a flash moves. There are, almost always ramifica- tions, resembling the rootlets of a plant, from the main, discharge, and these ramifi- cations occur in the direction towards which the discharge takes • place. .Thus lightning photographs reveal the actual occurrence of the relatively rare phenomenon of a dis- charge passing from the earth to the clouds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>ORIGINAL MAMMAL.</head>
        <p>THE ORIGINAL MAMMAL.</p>
        <p>The basal mammal, from which all modern beasts (including marsupials) are descended, was possibly a tree-hunting creature with five- fingered hands and five-toed prehensile feet, a lemur-like animal with. some resemblance to the modern opossum. From this generalised form of insect-eating, tree-dwelling, placen- tal, plantigrade beast, possessing a continuous dentition of four molars, four pre-molars, one canine, and four incisor teeth on each side of both jaws, arose, says Sir H. H. Johnston in the Tribune, the lemur, monkey, ape, and finally man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>IS WOMAN OUTGROWING MAN?</head>
        <p>IS WOMAN OUTGROWING MAN?</p>
        <p>Dr. A. T. Scofield asserts that we are ap- proaching an age when woman will outgrow man. In his opinion she not only has a much better body, but whereas man's brain is old, weary, and incapable of further de- velopment, hers is fresh, young, and eager., It is also asserted that girls mature from one to two years later than formerly, and that their inereAse in height continues, in some instances, until they are twenty. Each new generation is physically an -improvement over the preceding, and incidentally runs the brothers close in the matter of education.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>ARTIFICIAL DISPERSAL OF FOG.</head>
        <p>ARTIFICIAL DISPERSAL OF FOG.</p>
        <p>In a communication to the French civil en- gineers on the artificial dispersion of fog. M. pibos stated, according to the Engineer, that, quite independently of Sir Oliver Lodge, he began in 1904 his attempts at fog dispersion by electric waves, clearing spaces 120 m.=131 yards in diameter by means of a mast 25 m.= 82ft. above ground level. He found that forty minutes were required to clear a space of a htue more than the above diameter, and he considers that, with a great number of stations—round a harbour, for instance—the air may be kept clear over a large area. The Cie. des Chemins-de-fer du Nord is much in- terested in the subject, 'and has put up stations for various experiments in connection -with it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>MEASURING CHOKE DAMP.</head>
        <p>MEASURING CHOKE DAMP.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the French Academy of Scientists, M. Nestor Grehaut contributeo some details which are of interest to mine owners and officials, as ,to experiments with the carbonic acid gas, or after-damp of coal- mines, or some emanation from it which he terms formene. M. Grehaut has invented a special apparatus for measuring the quanta! of this gas in the atmosphere of a mine at any time, and be has recently been experimentir,-; with it in three different mines of widel different character: In the result he is able to state the proportions of formene in the air of the workings as 7 per cent., 4 per cent. and nil. M. Grehaut strongly urges tha,, these proportions should be taken every day in all mines.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>CLASSES OF BRAIN POWER.</head>
        <p>CLASSES OF BRAIN POWER.</p>
        <p>Famous brains are of four kinds, states Hausemann. The lowest group contains the minds that are stimulated greatly by alcohol, tea, and other drugs, and by im- pressions derived from the senses; the second group contains the infant prodigies, whose intellectual • powers wane in middle age. In the third group are contained the diseased cases usually terminating in in- sanity; the fourth and highest group is that of true geniuses, whose powers remain un- impaired until old age. Spitzka has come to the conclusion that men eminent in exact sciences like astronomy and mathematics have the greatest average brain weight. Next come the men of action, including statesmen and artists, and after these come the biologists, geologists, and other repre.4 sentatives of the descriptive sciences. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>MAGNESIUM ALLOYS.</head>
        <p>MAGNESIUM ALLOYS.</p>
        <p>For aluminium castings it is necessary to add. some hardening metal. Very light cast- ings are often required, but pure aluminium is found to be too soft. For alloys, zinc and copper have been used considerably, but both of these metals increase the weight of the casting. For this reason magnesium has been considered, for not only is it very light, but it has the property of hardening castings. The material can be purchased in large quantities, but at present its high price prohibits any general use. One of the most interesting facts that has been investigated by the Alloy Re- search Committee of the Institute of Mechani- cal Engineers is the strange influence upon copper of a small quantity of aluminium. It was found that one-tenth of 1 per cent. of aluminium raises the twist angle of copper in torsion 90 per cent., and lowers its electrical conductivity by 23 per cent. We may men- tion that the reports of the above committee represent the labours of all modern metallur- gical science.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>UNSOLVED BIRD PROBLEMS.</head>
        <p>UNSOLVED BIRD PROBLEMS.</p>
        <p>Few observant field naturalists fail sooner or later to become profoundly conscious of the too wide claim which is put forward by the more repetitive and unthinking fol- lowers of Darwin for many of his great and illumining theories. Popular text-books and articles are full, ,for example, of absurd un- truths and exaggerations on the subject of protective mimicry. Whether, says ; the County Gentleman, and to what extent, birds have the faculty of scent is another question on which there is plenty of field for the accumulation of evidence. It is now generally granted that the swiftness with which vultures gather together round a dead animal is due to each seeing its neighbour drop earthwards from its aerial beat, and the theory sometimes held that the pheno- menon was due to a marvelously acute smell-sense has been definitely abandoned. It is distinctly remarkable that birds seem to be so deficient in this respect, when quadrupeds excel in it.</p>
        <p>LUMINOUS., EMANATIONS OVER ORES. The fact that a luminous emanation of variable shape will appear in the dark at such points on the surface of the earth below which there are extensive ore deposits at a more or less considerable depth, was re- corded in Germany as far back as 1747. Im- mediately before or during a thunderstorm these phenomena are said to be especially striking. Similar -obserotions have been made in North America in the neighbour- hood of ore deposits. Though much should be</p>
        <p>ASCRIBED TO SUPERSTITION and to errors of observation, the fact never- theless has been confirmed, says the Scientific American, by recent investigation. The electric emanation given off from fiue surface of the earth has, in fact, been re- peatedly ascertainpd • photographically by Mr. K. Zenger. Plates coated with fluores- cent substances were used. It may thus be taken for granted that the emanations in question occur with an especially high in- tensity at those points of the ground where</p>
        <p>GOOD CONDUCTORS OF ELECTRICITY are found in large amounts in the neighbour- hood of the surface of the earth, in other words, above ore deposits, which are very good conductors of the electric current. Lig- nite and coal, especially when "Containing pyrites, are fairly good conductors. The dif- ference in the intensity of radiation as corn- !Area witn points free trom any ore Nvoata seem to be recognised by means of photography, thus affording to geologists a rather simple means of locating ore and even coal deposits.</p>
      </div>
