      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, May 25, 1907</p>
        <p>sincerity of purpose, however, can never be doubted, and his friends on all sides will hope that his talents may, in the future, be applied to some task that will offer greater hope of success.</p>
        <p>Me. WINSTON" CHURCHILL'S SIR HENRY remark the other day, as to CAMPBELL- the impossibility of the BANNERMAN. opening of the Pref3rence</p>
        <p>door whilst Sir HENRY CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN is the national hall porter, was a concrete expression of what all Liberals feel concerning the head of his Majesty's Government. This confidence in him as the really strong man is, it must be admitted, of recent growth. Until he became Prime Minister very few Liberals, even, knew how strong he was, or at the same time, how well-prepared for attack. He has now arrived at such atim e of life as to make his constant vivacity, readiness to make a point, and extraordinary soundness of judgment increasingly wonderful. It has been said of Sir HENRY that probably no living man has talked less nonsense. Mr. GLADSTONE said of him many years since that there was no office under the Crown for which he was not fit. He justified the saying in regard to one high office when, as Minister of War, he compellel the resignation of the late DUKE of C A.MBRIDGE of the office of Commander-in-Chief. He carried this difficult piece of business through with serene good humour, but by the exercise of an immutable strength of will.</p>
        <p>As head of the Government he has surprised both his friends and his political opponents by the completeness with which he has proved himself to be a type of British cornorm sense, straight- forward vigour, and Parliamentary ability. Every- body will remember the dramatic and effective way in which he brought horns to 'Sir. B.tr,Fouß's consciousness the fact that their relationship had been entirely changed by the election of a new Parliament. The late' PREMIER had been accustomed to treat him in a more than cavalier manner—sometimes without any manners at all, in fact. Then came a day when after one of the flippant speeches of the Conserva- tive leader, not dealing with a subject, but lead- ing as far away from it as possible, his patient antagonist leaped to his feet, uttered a few scorn- ful sentences, and then said : "Let us have no more of this foolery !" It was then 11r. BALFOUR was first made to understand that his power over the House of Commons had departed—that, indeed, his own House of Commons was some- thing belonging entirely to the past—and that the new Prime Minister had aides to his character the existence of which had been quite unsuspected by his political opponents. In a book about living statesmen, published no longer since than 1904, the PRIME MINISTER is spoken of as a comfnrtable, easy-going man of the world. However, it was added : "Mr. CAMP- BEL-BANNERMAN has only himself to thank if he is not better known and better understood." He does not advertise, in fact. Some of his great qualities would now have remained unknown to us but for the EARL OF ROSEBERY'S singular political divagations. He might never have become Prime Minister had not that statesman seemed desirous of banishing the Liberal Party much as CORIO- LANUS would have banished the Romans. In which case we might not have known in our time what it is to have a sound, unhesitat- ing, convinced, and unfearing Radical at the head of affairs. Sir HeNay CAMPBELL-BANNER- MAN is a Liberal Leader who believes in putting Liberal principles into practical operation as speedily as circumstances make it possible to do so. He has done this in the Transvaal with magnificent results,and he is obviously determined to do the same thing over the whole field of Eng- lish politics. If he has been the hall-porter who refused to open the Protectionist door, the Colonial Premiers have not gone home surprised at that. They expected nothing else, th iugh doubtless, it would have pleased some of them to find the Prime Minister and the Government less staunch.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P.. MR. A. H. C.ROSFIILD, has always taken an M.P. AND THE CUP aik enthusiastic interest in VICTORY. the advancement of healthy sports and on Saturday he entertained the Warrington football team at Chester in celebration of the great Cup victory. The picnic was a complete success and was thoroughly enjoyed by the guests who were cordial in their appreciation of Mr. Crosgeld's hospitality. At the dinner the Borough Member delivered two interesting little speeches and was very happy when touching upon Warrington's creditable record in the realm of sport. As he aptly put it, the achievements of our townsmen in that direction are almost as diversified as the variety of industries which have made Warrington celebrated as a town. While heartily encouraging athletics as a means of improving the physique and stamina of the nation, Mr. Crosfield rightly urged that there must be a sense of proportion in our enthusiasm for recreation, lest the more important of affairs of life be overlooked. All reasonable-minded people will agree with Mr. Crosfield's oft-expressed views, for while it is certainly to the advantage of the nation that sports should retain a hold on the affections of the people, we shou!d be on our guard against sports becoming the all in all of existence, and regard them as recreations which fit us all the more to play our part in the sterner affairs of life. 5,1</p>
        <p>The Warrington Licensing AIIGISTRAT ES AND Compensation Authority is . LICENSING LAW. evidently d‘lterrnined to exercise its powers firmly and independently, if the attitude of the magis- trates in fixing the proportions of compensation to be divided among the persons interested in con- fiscated licences may be taken Ps a criterion. The three licences in regard to which compensation had been awarded were those of the Rlpe and Anchor Inn, Buttermarket-street ; Queen's Hotel, Sankey-street ;" and B Jar's Head Inn, Pig Hill ; and the sanction of the Bench was asked to agreements drawn up between owners,tenants and lessees as to the sum to be received by each. In the case of the Rope and Anchor and Queea'a Hotel the arrangements commended themselves to the Court, but the Bench were not satisfied with the proposal submitted with regard to the Boar's Head. The agreement to which sanction was requested in this case was that the lessees and the tenant should have £212 10s. out of the' £1,620 awarded, the remainder to go to the owner. It further transpired that the tenant's share of the compensation was to be £75 clear, with a promise that she would be found another house at the earliest opportunity. When the magistrates demurred, it was urged that the universal custom ! was for these agreements to be accepted by the Court, but we are pleased to see that the justices refused to regard their daty in the matter as a mere formal ratification of terms agreed upon between other parties. If magistrates are to have no voice in these apportionments, the posi- tion is reduced to something approaching the farcical, and the Warrington Compensation Authority showed that they meant to do what they considered fair and reasonable, regardless of pre-arranged schemes. They decided that the tenant Mrs. Woods) should receive £l5O, the lessees (Messrs. Peter Walker and Son) £137 10s. and the</p>
        <p>owner(irs.Fraser), £1,332 10s. We hope that the policy thus laid down by the Bench will be adhered to, for we are convinced that in carefully consider- ing each case upon its merits they are taking the only right and proper view of their powers and responsibilities.</p>
        <p>Congratulations to Sir John A WELL-DESERVED Kirk! Warrington people HoNoult. will hear with particular pleasure that the King has conferred the honour of knighthood upon the secretary of the Ragged School Union who so recently visited this town to encourage the efforts of those who are striving to uplift the slum child- ren. In this case it is no mere platitude to say that honour has been accorded to whom honour is due. Sir John Kirk is one of those untiring social workers who deserve well of the country, for he has done a great deal for suffering humanity. Ho is of very humble origin, being the son of a village tirnnan and brazier, and a more genial, unassuming man iG would be difficult to find. E trly in life he became filled with an entusiasm for work among the helpless and downtrodden children. At the age of twenty-six he was appointed assistant secre- tary to the Ragged School Union, and later to the Open Air Mission. In 1879 he became secretary to the Ragged School Union and Shaftesbury Society. . This work has not absorbed all his activities, for he has acted also as organiser of the Fresh Air Funds of the Christian Guest Guild and of Sir W. Treloar's Christmas HAmper Fund. Ha is a fellow of the Royal Chartered Institute of Secretaries' edits "In His Name," the monthly organ of the Rigged School Union, and otherwise contrinutes to journalism on the topics which he has made his own. The work of his organisation has practically revolution- ised child-life in the slums, and nothing in the great philanthropic effort of the past half-centory has more thoroughly merited commendation. His Majesty has, we feel sure, voiced the sentiments of the nation in so signally honouring one whose benevolent activity has won the regard of all classes.</p>
        <p>The working classes nowa- THE ABUSE OF days have so much provided HOSPITALS. for them free of charge that many people—and those not least intimately acquainted with the lower grades of society—believe that the increase of gratuitous service is having a deteriorating effect upon the moral tone of a considerable portion of the com- munity. A reform movement has, therefore, been begun in London with the object of impressing upon the people generally the desirability of pay- ing for medical treatment where they can afford it. London has many hospitals which give free treat- ment to such patients as apply for it. Bata great many of those who attend as ont-patients are not too poor to pay, and would resent it if they were regarded as paupers. Yet they have no hesitation in taking 'for nothing that which they must know is provided by the generosity and humanity of others. Hospitals are one of the greatest boons of the present day, but it was never the intention of those who founded them to relieve the population at large of its daty to provide for its own treatment in illness as long as it had the means. The reform which is sug- gested is to get the hospitals to exclude from their out-patients those who can afford to pay, and to recommend them to join provident dispensaries where for a small contribution of a few pence a month they can get medical advice. The matter is one of considerable difficulty, but there is a good deal to bq said for those who desire to make some change in the custom with regard to hospitals.</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Hereford is AN OUTSPOKEN certainly one of the most BISHOP. outspoken occupants of the Episcopal Bench and on Thursday he did not mince matters when address- ing the clergy who assembled to hear his triennal charge at Hereford Cathedral. He asked why it was that the ministers of their Nonconformist neighbours were, as a rule,so much more keen and active than the majority of the country clergy in the cause of temperance. Toe clergy in one deanery replied that they were against a temperance conference because temperance meet- ings did more harm than good. It was a singular view to be held by a body of clergy in a district in which a great deal of drunkenness prevailed. He lamented that the younger clergy were not as much as they ought to be a visiting and pastoral • clergy, but were too easily satisfied with the ser- vices in Church and very much attracted and occupied by athletic sports and other social amuse- ments which should be subordinated to their duty and calling. Such things as gambling at bridge or attendance at race meetings were quite out of keeping with a clergyman's profession. Their personal duty was obviously, both by precept and• example, to controvert that craving for excite- ment, amusement, and indulgence so prevalent. These are plain words indeed and the Bishop is to be admired for expressing himself so clearly and unflinchingly.</p>
        <p>The whole of Western THE REVISION OF Christendom is interested THE VI7LGAtE. in the report that the Pope has confided to the Benedic- tine monks the task of revising the Vulgate:* If indeed, the members of the order have undertaken it, they will find it no light work either from the religious or the literary point of view. Perhaps, indeed, the literary diffi- culty is the lesser, since the followers of St. Benedict have, never wanted for scholars. The religious difficulty falls into two divisions, the necessity of maintaining in the revision the teach- ing of the Church, and the opposition which is bound to be experiencA to any alteration, no matter how great the improvement from the point of view of accutacy, of the text to which cen- turies of devout R )man Catholics have become attached, to which they have turned in sorrow and joy, whose sentences have been, as it were, written into the heart of the Church through the ages. The Benedictines are not likely to offend the devout more than is necessary to secure greater accuracy and correctness of expression. But the experience of past revisions is not reassuring. The Vulgate, as it is known to-day, is due to Pope Clement VIII., who had a revision of the Sixtine text prepared. The Sixtine version was an unfor- tunate affair. The Vulgate prepared by Jerome eleven hundred years before had been so altered and maimed that au official version was found necessary and the preparation of this was undertaken by Pope Sixtus V. It was issued to the world with a great fl mrish of trumpets, and the faith- ful were enjoined that it must be accepted as final. Its inaccuracies were, however, so great that it was very son "called in," and the Clementine version. took its place to endue ever since. English Christians of all denominations will trust that the Benedictines will not permit their revision to be open to the reproaches of either the Sixtine or the Clementine. The Roman Church in this matter occupies a peculiar p mition of authority, and owes a corresponding duty to the whole of Christen- dom.</p>
