      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <p>The decision of the Irish THE IRISH BILL. National Convention to</p>
        <p>reject the Irish Council Bill introduced a fortnight ago by Mr. BIRRZLL has, it cannot be doubted, sealed the fate of that measure. The Bill never had from the Irish Party, or, indeed, from any section of the House of Commons, an enthusiastic support. The Liberal Party was pledged to attempt some reform in the government of Ireland. But it was pledged also against that reform taking the shape, as far as this Parliament was concerned, of a scheme of H )rne Rule. The Government was in a position of diffi- culty and delicacy. On the one hand was the sincere desire to put an end, if possible, to the unsettled condition of Iris% affairs in</p>
        <p>manner which commend itself to the majority of the people of England. On the other was :the demand of the Irish Party, led by Mr. Redmond, for a fulfilment of the policy introduced more than twenty years years ago by Mr. GLADSTONE. It would be too much to say that Mr. BIRRELL'S Bill fulfilled either of these requirements. It was an attempt to do away with the Castle system of Govern- ment, which even many Unionists admit has clogged and crabbed the administration of Irish 'affairs. It proposed to set up in Dublin a Council consisting of eighty-two elected members, twenty-four nominated members, and one ex-officio—the permanent Under-Secretary. To this Council was to be delegated the control of eight of the largest and most important Depart- ments of Irish affairs. But the Lord-Lieutenant was to retain a strict veto over the Council, and the Chairmen of the Committees of that body were to be nominated by him. Mr. BALFOUR con- fessed in his speech after the introduction of the bill that it could in no sense be considered as legitim Itely leading to Home Rule. That view also was taken by Mr. REDMOND. But the Irish Leader declared on his part,viewing it as a measure detached entirely from the Home Rule demand, the Irish Party would be prepared to accept it. He has altered his mind. It will not serve any good pur- pose to inquire what are the influences that have induced this change of front. Probably the demonstration of hostility made by influential sections of his own people left no alternative to him if he desired to retain his leadership of the Irish National Party. No man can afford to flout Irish opinion who wishes to retain control. Mr. PARNELL, probably the most powerful of all Irish Leaders, could not, even at the zenith of his power, attempt that, and Mr. REDMIND has shown by his present action that he is not 88 strong perhaps as many people have been led by his leadership to believe. As to the effect of the resolution passed by the Irish Convention there can be little doubt. Mr. REDMOND advised the meeting to reject the bill, and the meeting accepted his guidance with remarkable enthusiasm. In those circumstances it would be folly for the British Government to attempt to proceed with the measure. The sassion is already far advanced, and, practically speaking, no progress has been made with the legislation promised in the King's Speech. If the Govern- ment were to insist upon proceeding with this bill, it would do so with the certainty of eventual defeat. Oppossd in the Lower House by the Irish National Party, by the Unionist Opposition, and only drawing a lukewarm support from many Liberals below the gin,gway, the measure would most certainly and not illogically be thrown out by the House of Lords. The position of the Government in that event would •be unenviable, and the course which reason dictates is, therefore, that the Bill should be withdrawn at once. The miscarriage of this particular measure cannot fail to have considerable effect upon 1 the future of Ireland. It is another step, and an unfortunate one for all parties concerned, in the melancholy history of that distressful country. Regarded dispassionately as its corpse may be, the bill was an honest, if not particularly adroit, attempt to settle the Irish problem. It has resulted only in proving to the British people that the Irish Party retains as its ideal that which was set up by Mr. PARNELL, and would be satisfied with nothing short Of a separate Parliament and a responsible executive. This is something which the English people have refused to grant, and , there is no reason to believe that they have changed their intention. Perhaps most sympathy will be felt in this matter for Mr. BIRRELL, the Chief Secretary, whose second attempt at legislation in this Parlia- ment has been incontinently brought to nought. Mr. BIRRELL is an earnest statesman, and upon this bill, as upon the Education Bill which came to grief last year, he expended a great deal of labour and thought. It is inevitable that he, as well as the Government of which he is a member, must suffer somewhat in credit by the failure of this I attempt to cope with the Irish problem. His I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>NOTES ANu NuriuMS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ANu NuriuMS.</p>
        <p>Alter Whit-Monday there should be strong support for a society for the expulsion of May from the English calendar. rhe merry month, forsooth !—" West- xinster Gazette."</p>
        <p>* * " le patriotism a waning force ?" Mr. C. E. Parker's letter in another column opens up some interesting considerations and we would commend it to the thoughtful perusal of our readers:,</p>
        <p>* * * It is quite untrue that when the Lord Chamberlain 'who was among the guests at the Prime Minister's dinner to Prince Fushimi, was asked if he would take coffee, he replied, absent-mindedly, "No, Ko-Ko."— " London Opinion."</p>
        <p>It was while acting as teacher and hon. secretary of Ann-street School, Camberwell, that Sir John met his future wife. He and his younger brother George, with whom he was then living, worked hard at Pitman's shorthand, and as the future Mrs. Kirk Lad also mastered the art, love letters were exchanged in this way and were safe from prying eyes.</p>
        <p>* * A remarkable instance of gencirosity on the part o of a Privy Councillor has come to light. It appears that this exalted personage on arriving at King's „Cross late on Tuesday night discovered that he had last a wallet containing over £lOO in cash and notes, and diligent search was made for the missing article. Zventually it was found beneath a seat by a boy,upon Whom the Privy Councillor bestowed the munificent lavvard of twopence!</p>
        <p>* Language is fossil poetry. —EIIERSON. When two grow angry he who is first silent is the Iliser. —TALMUD. , The more a man extends and varies his acquaintance Lae better.—De. Jomvsow. The fear of being beaten is the most insidious form Of moral cowardice.. -BAGsnoT. The great art of the lawgiver is to make use of all Lue forces which nature confers.—PlATo. Life gives us nothing betterthan•kindly and sympa- thetic thoughts of one another.—CREIGHTON.</p>
        <p>* .1, A man who had just finished a comfortable meal at a restaurant the other day suddenly rose from his chair and rushed out of the building. "Stop him! " exclaimed the cashier. '" That fellow's gone off ;Without paying !" "I'll stop him," said a determined- looking man, who rose up hasily from a table near Where the other had sat. "He took my gold-handled znibrella. I'll stop him and I'll bring him back with a policeman. The scoundrel!" Without a moment's pause he dashed out of the house in hot pursuit of the conscienceless villain. And the cashier, a cold, hard oneympathetic kind of man,- has begun to suspect that neither of them will come back.—" The Tatler."</p>
        <p>* * A. Bill to provide for the registration of teachers of music, presented by Mr. O'Neil, and supported, atnong others, 'by Sir John Brunner and Mr. T. P. O'Connor, was issued on Wednesday. The memor- andum explaining the Bill states that its object is to Provide the public with an official register of properly qualified teachers of music. The Bill will not prevent anY person from teaching music, nor will it penalise a non-registered teacher, but by providing an official ill it will enable the public to guard itself against Ilcompetence and imposture. In drafting the Bill 81Y effort has been made to consult the wishes of ',LLB great musical institutions and of associations 4epresentative of teachers of music.</p>
        <p>* * * cal337tlke death of Mr. Finch, the member for Rutland- "re, the Prime Minister succeeds to the title of patiler of the House of Commons. Mr. Finch entered -.lliainent in November, 1867, or thirteen months Sir Henry began his long association with the Burghs. One member of the present Parlia- Sir Francis Powell (says the London corres- '—bAent of the "Liverpool Post and Mercury "), cora- tnen It „Fed his Parliamentary career ten years before Sir b • Campbell Bannerman, but there have been several teaks in his record, and his total length of service is veral years short of that of the Prime Minister. Mr. .n.‘"haplin entered the House in the same year as Sir 41.enry, but his defeat at the last general election puts him in the second place for the venerable distinctin that has now fallen to the Prime Minister.</p>
        <p>* * * The Committee and members of the Warrington Pootball Club are to be congratulated on their meeting .0f last. Tuesday night. It was, like the club has been ler some years past, a well managed affair. Most of he speakers kept to the points before them with the esult that there was practically no unnecessary ePeaking. The discussion on the question of having a e„eeial and a smoker was somewhat amusing. The .!-;,emmittee did not appear over anxious to encourage ttle idea. Perhaps the banquettings and feastings they 'ave partaken of lately, are beginning to pall. Iltiw- ver, the members very naturally wished to share in 4lae rejoicings and resolved to celebrate the winning .Itl the Cup, even if only in a small way, so a resolu- f clla Was carried, 'instructing the Committee to arrange Or a" Smoker."</p>
        <p>* * i these are some of the choice pleasantries indulged at the Bank Holiday meeting of the Aberystwyth c'wn Council : " Oh, bless him !" (This ironically.) "These minutes are rotten." "And the whole Council is rotten." 'This roused the Town Clerk. "I will keep you here till morning." "You are the misleader of Aberystwyth." (Retort not heard.) " Tut, tut! " (Disparagingly.) "Cheek and impudence and arrogance of the !arst water." "That's boodleism."</p>
        <p>"It's bombastic ruffianism." "Pity you haven't a poor cook to wallop." "We are done,doomed, finished ! West Ham ! " "A dirty, low-down business ! " (Reply drowned • by uproar.) The meeting lasted four hours. A. correspondent calmly concludes that it was "one the " noisest meetings on record.</p>
        <p>- • - The visit of the Mayor and Mayoress to the whole '9f the Elementary Schools and the Technical Schools 111 the town last Friday was much appreciated and • ioyed by the scholars. The worthy couple were very, littingly accompanied bv Alderman Bennett, the chair- au of the Education committee, and Mr. J. Moore 4411rray, the Director of Education. So far as the Isehools were concerned it was "Empire Day" l.though it with a week in advance according to date, tut it had been decided (seeing that the scholars were ,boing a holiday this week) to celebrate it on the 17th 'stead of the 24th. The MAyor and Mayoress were the recipients of bouquets at some of the schools, 'whilst they presented bronze medals and brooches to those children who bore off the palm for good coa- 1-loot. The children themselves chose the prize winners. Of course speeches appropriate to the ?_ecasion were made, Alderman Bennett as we all "ow from past past experience being quite at home 'Oll the " Empire " subject. •</p>
        <p>•* * * " Memnon" in "The Dawn" has some warm praise for Mr. Asquith's Budget. In fact, his onl a.egret seems to be that the credit of originating old- pensions will not fall to the Conservative party. e says The Imperial Budget, too, was highly. satisfactory. It showed a handsome surplus of con- derably over five millions, and it enabled Mr. tilequith to discriminate to the extent of threepence in e Pound in favour of earned as against unearned 'lncomes up to a total income of £2,000 per year. !lie scheme includes a proner stiffening of " penalties incurred by those who are so contemptibly dishonest as to evade the payment 2t their equitable share of the common burden; a 4erther graduation of the Death Duties, under which tates of three millions and upwards will be taxed to 'le tune of 15 per cent,; a prudent allocation-of an eltra milliu and a half to Sinking Fund; and the rr-marking of two and • a quarter millions with a dif to a scheme of Old Age Pensions to be intro- ced next year. It is a sound and statesmanlike rlejtaleree, and I believe in practically every line of it. e Conservatives,l fear,have lost their chance. They ueed the cry of Old Age Pensions as a counter in the tajt,Y game—a cry to conjure with, by the way.—and re gat have claimed the everlasting credit of onginat- beg !erns far-reaching plan. Now, the Liberals will e It”le to say that this, the greatest of all Social the tetras which are not purely Socialistic, is due to n; although they never tried to tempt the electors Promising it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <p>i Yesterday was Empire Day but beyond the display of flags on the public buildings there was no recognition of the event at Warrington the celebra- tions in the schools having taken place last Friday. 1 Warrington's annual horse parade will be held this I afternoon and promises to be as great a success as Page ever. It is to be regretted, however, that the. respon- , . Football Club Annual Meeting. Bible committee have not included a class,in the list of ; , Women's Chat and Fashions. competitions, for the best decorated vehicles. Perhaps : "The One-Ejed Man." they will sae their way to make this departure next ; year. I 3. "A Little Savage," by Alfred Tennyson. Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., Entertains the * * * . I .. A joke hpnever been made yet that, according to Cup Winners. the majority of critics of the national character, a ' Science Notes. Scotsman has not failed to see. And, believe me,wheni Church News. • I think of this dastardly accusation, my blood boils so 6 , ' Mr. John Kirk Knighted. District News. furiously that nothing but a three days' sitting on the ! Volunteer Prize Distribution. North Pole could possibly Make my temperature normal ! Northern Union. again.—Harry Lauder ii" London Opinion." i 7. Time Table. • ** * 1 These is no reason in the nature of things why ! Cricket and Bowling. Easter should not be held on a fixed date just as , "L -FRANCIS," "PREMIER," and " MUDGE- Christmas is. If this were done, so as to ensure that; WHITwonTH " CYCLES. Price from £4 10s. Cash Whitsuntide fell in the first week of June, the change ! Or easy payments.--J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, would be a very welcome one to the country. All the ' Bridge-street, Warrington.--Advt. other holidays occur at definite dates, and it can only Coming Events. be a matter of time before Easter is made to fit into i the general scheme.—" Country Life." i A meeting in aid of the Bishop of Liverpool's Fund . ~ • • ; will be held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street * It was