        <p>* * * John Davidson, the famous Scotch poet, recently visited Warrington, and this is how he subsequently wrote about us in the "Glasgow Herald" : --" My impression of Warrington, which I only passed through upon a rapid journey from London to Liverpool in a racing motor at a most illegal pace, is of a town marked off for eternal reprobation, evil-smelling, dingy, foul and grim, awaiting consciously, as its only hope, the obliterating fire of dooms- day." This is really too cruel, especially after the arduous efforts of the Beautiful Warrington Society, but it is perhaps as well to take the charitable view of a writer in "The Dawn," who suggests that it is quite evident that Mr. Davidson "passed through Warrington in a racing motor at a most illegal pace," and he was probably deflected by that silly and misleading finger-post at the Bridge. * * A powerful "appeal to reason "on the subject of women's suffrage has just been issued, the object being to induce men to petition members of Parliament in favour of the granting of the vote to women in Parliamentary elections on the same terms as men."We ad:nit" it is stated "that it is for the good of the coun- try as a whole, its order and contentment, its wise and enlightened government, that not only the landlord and the farmer, the:manufacturer and the merchant, but also the labourer and the mechanic, should have a voice in the election of a Parliamentary representa- tive, and should be able to make themselves heard in matters which concern the needs and interest of their own class. Is it not, then, equally for the Food of the country as a whole that women should, in the same ' way, have a voice in promoting or opposing legisla- tion which affects them, whether exclusively, or in I commori with men ?"</p>
        <p>WOW BANK'S Ladies and Gent's Gloves.—Advt. TAILOR-MADE CYCLE BREECHES from 6s. lid.— BAs, Outfitter, Horsemarket st.—Advt. . The Order of Druids.</p>
        <p>i At the annual conference in connection with the Order of Druids at Battersea on Thursday the Grand Treasurer referred to a dispute between the Board of Management and the Warrington district, stating that , the Warrington district, which was not represented at the delegation, had failed to return a dispensation granted for the opening of a new lodge. Certain correspondence was read by the Grand Secretary, and res-lation was passed approving the action of the , board demanding that the di9pensation should be returned.</p>
        <p>Too Drunk to Remember. At the Warrington Police Court, on Thursday, Thomas Walker, a puddler, of Bank-street, was fined £1 and costs for breaking a pane of glass at the shop of William Burge, Bewsey-stteet. On Wednesday night prisoner behaved in a disorderly manner in the sb.op,and when put outside,he deliberately smashed the window with his fist. Prisoner said he Idid not remember anything. He was in the Lord Rodney- public-house all afternoon and evening. The Chair- man remarked that the police ought to make inquiries, and Superintendent Monaghan promised to do so.</p>
        <p>PiTSLIC BSNRFIT, UNDER Tall CLOCK FOR BiUiT BOOTS IN L &amp;NO ASHIRE.—Ad vt.</p>
        <p>* * * Whitsuntide is not quite the greatest of English holidays. Since the passing of the Bank Holiday Act the first Monday in August, which commemorates nothing either in the civil year or the ecclesiastical, may claim that distinction. But the Feast of Pentecost as it is termed by the Church, is doubtless far more important to the working classes than Easter or Christmas. Prolonged daylight and fine weather may reasonably be expected to favour the celebration and by the beginning of the present week people who intend to take a holiday have made their plans. There is not much difficulty in doing so, for the keeping of Whitsuntide is one of our oldest social observances. We read of " Whitsun Ales" in medimval times, and though Chaucer and his fellow-pilgrims did not journey to Canterbury by excursion ,train, they appear to have been animated by the roving instinct equally with the modern Londoner. Englishmen of a certain order seem, in fact, always to have looked upon a holiday as implying a journey, and the Festival of Whitsuntide was ordinarily the earliest period at which a long journey on foot or horseback could be prudently undertaken. Our people are following the ways of their forefathers, only instead of travelling to Canter- bury, they cross the Channel, or even go to the Mediterranean. What they will 'do, and where they Will go, when aerial locomotion has become the rule, it would be hazardous to conjecture. All that is safe to guess is that the Whitsun holiday will not be spent at home.</p>
        <p>Farm Fire. The Warrington Fire Brigade were summoned early on Thursday morning to a fire which had broken out at the farm occupied by John Crosby, Green Farm, Burtonwood. The brigade turned out with promtitude and on reaching the farm' two stacks of oats were found to be well alight. The efforts of the brigade were successful in saving a third of the produce. The fire had been in progress several hours before the Warrington brigade were called. Previously the St. Helens brigade had been on the scene, but were unable to get to work as their appliances would not connect with water mains in that district. The Warrington brigade returned home about eleven o'clock in the morning. The damage, which is roughly estimated at £350 is covered by insurance.</p>
        <p>Whitsuntide. The Whitsuntide holidays have been remarkable for the wintry weather experienced, ,and as a conse- quence the exodus of holiday-makers from Warring- ton was not quite as large as usual. Many people, however, availed themselves of the admirable facilities placed at their disposal by the railway and steamship companies, Liverpool, Manchester, Blackpool, South- port, North Wales, and Cheshire attracting their fair quota of visitors The conditions were not very favourable to cycling, but large numbers of enthusias- tic wheelers took the opportunity of indulging in their favourite runs. The great festival of Whit Sunday found due observance in the churches,many of which were brightly decorated with choice flowers. For those who remained in town on Monday the chief attraction was the exhibition of animated pictures at the Parr Hall, large houses assembling. Animated Pictures at Parr Hall. i During the week Warringtonians have found pleasure n witnessing the series of animated pictures presented in the Parr Hall. Each evening at 7 and 9 o'clock an admirable and varied programme is pre- sented the subjects being largely of a humorous character. In the village Sunday School tea party the misadventures of an effusive curate create considerable amusement, whilst a similar effect is produced by "The short-sighted cyclist" and " A drunken Mattress." The adventures of a dog which helps itself to a string of sausages are very laughable. Amongst the more serious subjects is an excellent presentation of a Venetian tragedy and the unmasking of a villian by a brave wife. Prominent in the programme is a series of singing pictures by the Lynches, in which the verses of songs are splendidly illustrated by lime light pictures. _ _</p>
        <p>- - Old Warringtonlan's Death. The funeral of the Rev. T. Bridge, vicar of Poynton and a native of Warrington; took place on Saturday, and was of a most impressive character. The coffin was carried by the bellringers and sidesmen from time Vicarage to the church, which was crowded with mourners. Walking in front were impresentatives of the Macclesfield Board of Guardians, Macclesfield and District Council, Poynton Primitive Methodist Church, Poynton Baptist Church,Poynton Brass Band, Poynton Cricket Club, Poynton Football Club; and the farmers. Immediately behind were the Vicar's personal friends and the teachers of the Sunday and day schools. The service in church was choral, the Rev. J. G. Elstob, Rural Dean of Macclesfield, and the Rev. J. Begge, M.A., vicar of Woodford, officiating. Mr. Bridge, who was 65 years of age, was born in Bank-street, Warrington, and was educated at the Blue Coat School, where he ultimately became usher.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>INDO-CHINESE OPIUM TRAFFIC. A Call to Prayer. _</head>
        <p>THE INDO-CHINESE OPIUM TRAFFIC. A Call to Prayer. _</p>
        <p>A letter signed by representativ2s-of many denomina- tions has been issued. It states :—The burden of our national share in the degradation of China by continu- ance of the opium traffic lies heavily upon the hearts of all who reoognise the obligation in which our Christian profession involves us, dot only toward God but also toward those" for whom Christ died." It has been suggested, therefore, that all such should join in a concert of prayer that God will move the hearts of those in authority to carry forward with all boldness, and with as little delay as possible, the necessary steps to secure the entire abolition of the traffic ; and we invite Christians everywhere to observe Thursday, May 30th, as a day of intercession to this end. On the corresponding day of last year the following resolution was moved in the House of Commons and carried unanimously : " That this House re-affirms its conviction that the Indo-Chinese Opium Trade is morally indefensible, , and requests his Majesty's Government to take ' such steps as may be necessary for bringing it to a speedy close." In the difficult and evidently slow work of giving practical effect to a righteous policy, we can best strengthen the hands and quicken the energies of our statesmen by earnest prayer; and wetherefore feel that ' it is time to betake us to the Throne of Grace, there to intercede with Him to Whom all power is given and Who judgeth righteously. The fact that the Chinese Government has resolved to suppress opium-smoking within ten years, and that those who are best qualified to judge are unanimous in their regard for the bona-fides of the Chinese authori- ties in these matters, is surely in itself a fresh call to England to facilitate, at however great cost to herself, the liberation of that vast Empire from . what is admittedly its degrading thraldom. The Edict in which the resolution of the Chinese Government is expressed is but a pathetic variation of the Mac&amp;lonian appeal— " Come over and help us ! "—and, as a professedly Christian nation, we canikot righteous], withhold what is in our power alone to afford them. The appeal is endorsed by forty-six leading represen- tatives of various branches of Christian activity, the Bishop of Liverpool being among the signatories.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN LIBRARY COMMITTBE.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Library Committee held in the Council Chamber of the Town. Hall on Friday erening, there were present :—Messrs. F. W. Dutton (who presided), T. Riley and C. Ditchfield, and Mrs. Jones (librarian). On the motion of Mr. Ditch- geld, seconded by Mr. Riley, accounts amounting to £6 13s. 4d. were passed for pasment.—The Librarian stated that the estimated expenditure for the next year would be £220. The balance in hand from last year's working was £6.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>WEDDING.</head>
        <p>WEDDING.</p>
        <p>A pretty, but quiet wedding was witnessed last Thursday afternoon, at St. Mary's Parish Church, the contracting parties being Mr. William Hague, son of Mr. E. Hague, of 31, Mersey-road, and Miss Emily Tanner, daughter of Mr. E. Tanner, of Mersey-road. The Rev. G. Stephen Jones (Vicar) officiated. The bridesmaids were Miss Clara Hague (sister of the bride- groom), and Miss .T. Tanner (sister of the bride), the best man being Mr. M. Hague (cousin). The bride wore a cream dress with hat to match, Miss Hague, as bridesmaid, wore a light brown dress, and Miss Tanner was attired in green.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>HOLIDAYS AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>THE HOLIDAYS AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Monday morning opened fine but chilly. Holiday- makers were early astir, and a great many Widnesians sought the salubrious breezes of the well-known holiday resorts. The White City and the Lancashire-Yorkshire cricket match claimed the attention of many. South- port had its portion, whilst Liverpool, New Brighton, Chester, and Hooton (for the races) were also patronised. Mothers took their children to Frodsham Hills. There was absolutely nothing in Widnes to entice them to stay at home; small wonder that when a holiday Comes round they migrate for these little pleasures. SUNDAY SCHOOL PROCESSIONS.</p>
        <p>The Whit 34onday festivities at Runcorn were not on the usual scale. In the morning only one friendly 'society—the Lily of the Valley Lodge of Oddfellows— turned out to parade, and in the afternoon the number of scholars in procession was much below previous i years. The latter fact is due to a severe epidemic of measles. The Church schools combined in one demon- stration, the figures being :—Parish Church, 900; St. Michael's, 164; Holy Trinity, 200; and Mersey Missions, 242—total, 1,506. The Nonconformist schools also joined forces—namely. Brunswick Wesleyan, 386: St. John's Presbyterian, 130 ; St. Luke's Congregational, 157; Bethesda Congregationol, 104; Primitive Metho- dist, 216; Halton-road Wesleyan, 248 ; United Methodist Free Church, 152; Camden Wesleyan, 320: Nelson-street Wesleyan, 80; and Salvation Army, 109—total, 1,902, which is 500 less than last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>PARAFFIN LAMP AS A WEAPON.</head>
        <p>PARAFFIN LAMP AS A WEAPON.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Sessions, on Thursday, Thomas O'Connell, a young man residing with John Crossley, in Nelson-street, was charged with unlawfully wounding the latter on the 17th. Crossley, whose head was a mass of bandages, said that on the night of the 16th inst., he told prisoner to find fresh lodgings. Prosecutor went to work next morning, but on returning at night asked prisoner why he had not gone. Words followed and O'Connell picked up a parafin lamp, which he broke on his head, inflicting several severe injuries, one a large wound on the side of the head, another on the left side of the face, and others on the back of the head and nose, all penetrating to the bone. Prosecutor was rendered unconscious, and afterwards taken to Dr. Hutchinson. Prosecutor said he gave no provocation beyond asking him to go away. The reason for this was his trouble- some disposition in the house. Prisoner was sent for trial to the asset Liverpool Assizes.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN FOOTBALL CLUB. PRESENTATION OF CUP AND MEDALS.</p>
        <p>The West Lancashire Cup and gold medals were pre- sented to the Runcorn Club at a social gathering held in , the Greenway-road schoolroom last Thursday evening. , The President of the Club (Mr. John Littler) presided, and on either side of him were Mr. Henry Ashton (President of the West Lancashire League) and Mr. W. Cawley (chairman of the committee of the Runcorn Club), the members of the committee, Dr. McDonnell (medical officer of the club) and Messrs. G. T. Wainwright, R. FL Pringle and IG. S. Hollingworth. The Chairman, who had a cordial reception, said he made no apology for saying that the Rnncorn Club played the game even in the presence of the Secretary of the Warrington Club, because it was common know- ledge that if there was another club which played the game it was the Warrington Club. (Applause.) tie believed the success of the Runcorn Club depended upon a continuance in the future of playing the game and of working with the committee in that loyal co-operation which had characterized the club up to the preaent time. (Applause.)—Mr. Ashton, who was introduced by Mr. Littler as the President of the West Lancashire League and the Secretary of the famous Warrington Club, and who was very heartily received, then presented the cup to the club. He said they were told that the Northern Union was on the decline, but looking at the matter from an impartial point of view be had no hesitation in stating that it was not. (Applause). The position of affairs which gave rise to the opinion that the Union was on the decline was simply became of the disparity between the clubs at the top and the clubs at the bottom of the League. There was a want of opportunity for clubs to meet others others upon what he should term their own strength. Ho contended that the re-institution of a first division and a second divi- sion, where clubs would be of nearer equal merit,would lead to better games and increase the interest among the inhabitants of the districts where the games were played. Many of their Rugby Union friends contended that a professional had no right to play football. They said that professionalism led to many abuses. He emphati- cally denied this. Under a strong governing body there was no opportunity for, even if there was a desire for, abutes. It was very encouraging to-know that the game to-day was played under circumstances which very few people could take exception to. (Applauso. He then handed the cup to Captain Jolley, who suitably thanked him, and both were heartily cheered.— The Chairman distributed handsome gold medals to the following players and officials Jolley (captain), .T. Butterworth, S. Walker, J. Williams, J. Moran, E. Hesketh, J. Dixon, A. Richardson, S. Watton, B. Flanagan, R. Padbury, A. Kennedy, E. Harrison, T. Deakin, A. Leyland, S. Dobson, M. McDonald, A. Abel (secretary), and C. Pownall (treasurer). The Chairman said it gave him special plelsure to present a medal from the players to Mr. Cawley, chairman of the com- mittee. Mr. Cawley heartily thankol the players for their magnificent little gift.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS GENTLEMAN'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>General regret was caused at St. Helens on Saturday by the announcement of the death of Mr. B. Bower, of Denton's Green-lane. Nir. Bower formerly resided in I,iverpool, but for over thirty years had been cashier at Nfessrs. Robinson and Cook's foundry. He was a well- known figure in the town: and most highly respected. For some time he had been in failing health,and latterly had been confined to the house for eight weeks. The death took place early in the morning.</p>
        <p>COLLAPSE OF A DISUSED SHAFT AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Some alarm was caused in the neighbourhood of Ravenhead, St. Helens. on Saturday, when the top of an old pit shaft at the Royal Collieries caved in. The collieries had not been in use for about forty years. The shaft which fell in is situated on some waste ground where children often play ; but fortunately at the time of the occurrence no one was in the neigbourhood, and Messrs. Pilkington's workmen covered up thE shaft, which is 300 yards deep and full of water.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>ST. HELENS BURNING CASE</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS BURNING CASE</p>
        <p>Sarah Ann Rodgers, aged seven, daughter of a collier, living at Bramwell-street, Parr, was admitted to the Providence Hospital on Sunday suffering from severe burns. The child was left in the house on Saturday night, when her clothing became ignited at the fire. The mother tried to extinguish the flames, but it was only when a man who was passing ran into the house and used his jacket that the flames were extinguished.</p>
        <p>ASSAULT ON A COLLECTOR. Joseph Cunningham, Morgan-street, St. Helens, was charged on Monday with wounding A. E. Bradbury, collector for Messrs. Griffins Ltd., on Saturday. It appears that Bradbury went to Morgan-street to collect something on account of some furniture which Cunningham had purchased. When Cunningham opened the door he struck at Bradbury and inflicted a severe wound under his left eye. He afterwards followed the man out of the house into the 'street, and beat and kicked him. Dr. Cooke dressed the wound. The case was dealt with summarily, and Cunningham was sent to prison for two months with hard labour.</p>
        <p>INDECENT PRINTS AT ST. HELENS. Henry Turner, a Cockney tipster, who had come into the district in connection with the Haydock Park Races was, at St. Helens, on Saturday, sent to prison for three months with hard labour for selling indecent prints in the streets, and for fourteen days for acting is a pedlar without a certificate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <p>c)RAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>"ROYAL GINGER"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>BISCU I TS.</head>
        <p>BISCU I TS.</p>
        <p>THE NEW TEA TABLE DELICACY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <p>the levels were taken he thought it would be necessary fos the Surveyor to go over the levels again beforetheplanr and specifications were got out and tenders asked for. This course was agreed to.</p>
        <p>SUPPOSED ATTEMPTED SUICIDE. William Critchley, aged fifty-two years, of 62, Charn- wood-street, St. Helens, made what seems to have been a desperate attempt on his life, on Thursday, by cutting his throat with a razor. Critchley had recently been left a widower, with a large family, and some domestic trouble had arisen which is supposed to have been the cause of the rash act. Police Constable O'Donnell was called in by the relatives, and after the man had been attended by a doctor he was removed to the Providence Hospital. SAD SUICIDE AT ST. HELENS. William Glover, aged sixty-five, a labourer of 72, Peckers Hill-road, Sutton, St. Helens, was found hang- ing in the back kitchen of his house on Monday morn- ing at 7.20. Glover had an attack of influenza in January last, and was attended by Dr. Casey. The illness made him very weak and low-spirited. On Saturday night he retired to bed in the usual way, and on Sunday morning when Mrs. Glover wakened he had disappearred. On going downstairs she found him quite dead hanging by the neck from a hook in the ceiling.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>OLD PIT SLIAFT DANGERS.</head>
        <p>OLD PIT SLIAFT DANGERS.</p>
        <p>Much uneasiness has been created in the Thatto Heath district of St. Helens by the collapse of an old and forgotten pit shaft. It appears that Messrs. Pilkington, who are the principal colliery owners in the district. have been using an old pit shaft near the toll-bar as a pumping station, an old shed near the shaft being usbd as the boiler-house. The other morning the man in charge was blowing off steam from the engine when he ' heard a loud report. On going outside to see what was the matter he found that the earth in front of the door of the temporary engine-house had collapsed, revealing the mouth of an old pit shaft some 12ft- or 14ft. in diameter. He made some experiments to ascertain the depth, and came to the conclusion that it was very deep. The mouth of the pit had evidently been bricked over, but the blowing off of the steam had weakened the brickwork, and it bad ultimately collapsed. The man had to wheel his clinkers every morning over the very spot, and it is said that the place was a favourite one for the children of the district. The existence of a second shaft had been forgotten. There is little doubt that there are similar shafts in the district. ST. HELENS MYSTERY EXPLAINED.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy-coroner, inquired at St. Helens Town Hall, on Tuesday, into the death of Jesse Bimpson, blacksmith's striker, aged fifty-five, of Albion- street, who died early on Saturday at the residence of his daughter in Grant-street.—The Coroner said the jury would have to decide whether the man died from injuries received during the course of his employment or from a blow which he received from his son-in-law, but the post-mortem examination showed that the death was due to natural causes. On Saturday, the 4th inst., James Atherton, son-in-law' who lived with Bimpson at Albion-street, had a quarrel with him. Deceased struck at Atherton on account of having received an anonymous postcard which made allegations against him. Ather- ton retaliated and struck deceased under the left eye. Some days later Simpson returned from work and said to his wife wife," Oh, Lizzie, I'm shot at." On Satur- day, the 11th, he went to his daughter's house in Grant- street, where Dr. Officer was sent for. Dr. Officer said the man's body was extensively diseased, and he died from pleurisy and cardiac failure. There were no signs whatever of injury. A verdict of "Accidental death" was returned. The Coroner called Atherton forward and pointed out the serious position he would have been in if the doctor bad said the bruise on the man's face had had any connection with the death. He advised him to keep out of drunken brawls and not to stria anyone unless he was obliged to do in self-defence. ROMAN CATHOLIC PROCESSION.</p>
        <p>The Whit Monday demonstration of the Roman Catholic schools and guilds of the district occupied the principal streets of St. Helens all morning. The thoroughfares were crowded with sightseers, and the various churches made a brilliant display of magnificent banners, which were accompanied by prettily-arranged groups of children dressed iv colours which harmonised with the decorations of the banners. Over 12,000 scholars and church members took part in the proces- sion, and there was a record number of brass bands. The weather was on the whole favourable, though the east wind must have been very trying to some of the younger children. The Rev. W. F. Hearne acted as chief marshal, and this year's demonstration was under the patronage of the Roman Catholic Bishop of Liverpool (Dr. Whiteside), Dean Powell, the Rev. D. Bennett, and Mr. B. Walmsley- Cotham, who were met at the Town Hall by the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Bishop). The Mayoress was presented with a bouquet by Miss Edipprds, of Denton's Green. The St. Thomas of Calltorbury School and Guill had the honour of taking the first place in the procession and providing the equipage for his lordship. The Town Flail was a great centre of interest, and many thousands turned out to watch the proceedings. In the a ftern3on the Parish Church schools and Bible class held their field day at Denton's Green. The St. Thomas School went to Eccleston, and the Sacred Heart occupied the football ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON</p>
        <p>VULC XN FOUNDRY.</p>
        <p>Arrangements are in progress at the Vulcan Foundry for the extension of the premises and of the new insti- tute, which is being erected for the employees. Mr. Curran is the architect, and Mr. 11. Fairclough the contractor. GOLBORNE OFFICIAL'S DE ATI-1.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles S. Rennie, clerk and surveyor of the Golborne Urban District Council, died at his residence, Southolme, Golborne, Newton-le-Willows, on Monday. after a very short illness. fie was one of the best known officialy in the county of Lancashire, and one of the vice-presidents of the Urban District Councils Association. He was barely twenty-ono years of age when appointed clerk to the Golborne Parochial Com- mittee, and when the district became urban he was made the first clerk. Only a few weeks ago he celebrated his thirty years' service at Golborne.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>DEATH OF "DR." CARTER.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF "DR." CARTER.</p>
        <p>Cricket and football enthusiasts will regret to hear of the death of Mr. William Carter, more familiarly known as" Dr." Carter. The deceased, who was prob- ably the oldest resident in the town, was in his 90th year. He came to Earlestown from Manchester in 1853, and was employed the whole of the time in the fitting department at the Viaduct Wagon Works. At the age of 81 he retired on a pension. Since then he has gradually grown feeble, but kept about the streets,even when on crutches, and was the same jovial old soul as in former years, if somewhat less enthusiastic. The Earlestown lads, whether in cricket or football, were always very near his heart, and he could explain away defeat far better than any of the defeated. He attended every match possible, and his famous" rubbing bottle" will linger in the memory for years to come. He was very proud of the town of his adoption, and Earlestown never had a stronger advocate. Latterly he resided with Mr. Henry Jolley, 15, Booth-street, but-for nine weeks he was an inmate of the Warrington Infimrary, his death being due to senile decay. He was never married, was a teetotaller practically all his life, but smoked a good deal.—The funeral took place last Thursday at Wargrave Churchyard, the Rev. J. Carson, M.A.' officiating. Among those present were Messrs. H. Jolley, Jas. Grimshaw, John Grimshaw, A. Peake, C. Charlton, F. Holt, and others, some of the above acting as beaters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>PHYSICAL DRILL.</head>
        <p>PHYSICAL DRILL.</p>
        <p>A physical drill competition was held on the laws at Wargrave House on baturday. There were three medals presented dumb bells, bar bells, clubs, free movements. The first was a gold-centre medal, won by Miss Lottie Ratcliffe; the second, a silver medal, won by Miss Anderson • and the third, a silver one, won by Miss Phythian. The special silver medal for high jump was carried off by Miss Bertha Ball. Books for attend - ance at class were awarded to Miss J. Johnson, Miss C. Ball, and Miss J. Johnson, Miss U. Brown, Miss E. Anderson, and Miss A. Phythian. The judges were Mr. H. K. Honey and Mr. H. daze, of the Balfour Institute, Liverpool, Mr. R. J. Hill, of Earlestown, was the in- structor. The prizes were presented by Mrs. Carson, of Wargrave Rectory. "EMPIRE DAY."</p>
        <p>2mpire Day was fittingly observed last Thursday at the Earlestown District School. During the week the teachers had given lessons suited, to the occasion, being wound up by an address by the headmaster (Mr. A. Brown) to the upper standards on Thursday morning. In the afternoon lessons were suspended, and the • Rev. A. Ainley addressed the scholars and a number of parents, touching on the most interesting event of the day—the election by the boys and girls of the first class (voting by ballot) of the boy and girl who in their opinion were most likely to make worthy citizens. Fred Lewis and Edith Wright were the forunate individuals chosen. Mr. Brown has estab- lished a "Head Master's Reward," which is to be annual, in connection with this event, hoping it will assist to .develop character and good citizenship. A goodly contingent of parents attended the Infant School celebration, an admirable little pro- gramme having been prepared there.. The programme in the mixed school was of a free and informal character and included the following, beside a number of songs, &amp;c., in cliorus :—Speech by Joseph Darbyshire ; Recita- tion, KiMing's "Recessional," Alec Sephton ; "God bless the Prince of Wales," Winnie Williams ; "Last rose of Summer," W. Seddon; Recitation, "Our Colonies," Alice +lin ; Song, "The Maple Leaf," F. Sephton ; Recitation, "Play up," W. Shaw; Song "The Minstrel Boy" Hugh Cooper; Recitations, i "Lives there a man," G. Jones; England," Eva ' Final ; song, "Home sweet Home," Alice Hill. A ' halfpenny collection for the Waifs and Strays Fund realised £1 7s. 3id., and a collection in Infant School children and visitors .£1 15s. 4d.—At most of the other schools a portion of Friday morning was set apart for lessons on patriotism and empire building, and in some cases a holf-holiday was given by the managers. Some of the week's work—geography and object lessons— were co-related and made interesting and appropriate in various ways. At Wargrave Mixed School a number of choruses were sung by the children, a few friends being resent by invitation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>VOICE OF LABOUR.</head>
        <p>THE VOICE OF LABOUR.</p>
        <p>Edited by John Workman.</p>
        <p>Readers of this column are invited to assist in making it interesting and helpful to social workers by contributing themselves. Correspondents must write on one side of the paper only, and endeavour to make their letters and notes short and to the point. A nom-de-plume may be used, but the real name of the writer should be forwarded in confidence to the Editor of the column. .111.5.5. *utast be addressed to John Workman, " Examiner " Office Warrington and should be sent in not later than Wednesday in each week.</p>
        <p>Before actually commencing my notes this week, i I have a duty to perform and that s to most respect- fully beg to offer congratulations to King Alfonso, and to hope that he will soon recover his equanimity. The other day, one of the illustrated papers which has given us a surfeiture of news concerning the Royal trio, contained the following invigorating information: —" King Alfonso's fatherly pride in his little son is the joy of Madrid. For hours he sits at the Queen's bedside, playing with the tiny fingers of the small Prince of the Asturias. Those who have been privi- leged to witness this pretty domestic scene have been quite affected by it." How nice! What with bull- fights, pigeon-shoots, motor-trips, playing with the Prince's tiny fingers one wonders how he finds time to conduct the affairs of State. But then there is, for- tunately, only one King of Spain—and only one small Prince of the Asturias with tiny fingers to be played with. A King's life, after all, is not a happy one! Any honest attempt at the solution of the great social problems which affect the well-being and happiness of the people deserves the commendation of all sincere men and women. Narrowness of mind is a form of mental disease which selfish party tactics can never cure. Selfish workingmen are as objection- able as selfish rich men who despise work. It may be necessary, for the moment, for employ's to com- bine in an endeavour to frustrate the machinations of the employer, but such an effort can only be of temporary service. And, probably for those reasons I cannot altogether endorse the opinions expressed by" The Scout" with reference to the Trades Council. If the desire of the Trades Council is to run only trades unionists for positions of trust in the town's affairs and for Parlia- mentary representation, then, excellent as its inten- tions may be, class war will continue. There are too many Unions, and there is not sufficient combination. ' It is to be regretted that the Trades Council has taken such a short-sighted view of public life. Public servants should be selected not because they are members of a trades union but because of their ability to serve. The policy they have chosen is a re-actionary one and, in my opinion, can only end in disaster. 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>LIBERAL WOMEN.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL WOMEN.</p>
        <p>Miss Balgarnie, who is not unknown to the Liberal women, of Warrington, speaking at the Women's Liberal Federation meetings recently held at the Memorial Hall, London, remarked: "The poorest must be protected. We must have a minimum wages board. I am glad the Liberal party sees this truth. It will be the only salvation of the Liberal party from the great flood of Socialism." . Lady Carlisle was very wrath with Mr. John Burns. He was to have had charge of the Women's Qualifica- tion Bill but at the last moment he announced that it was not to be introduced in the House of Commons, but in another place—the House of Lords. " The Government," said Lady Carlisle, " has said that the House of Lords is not fit to legislate f)r us and yet they have sent up our measure to be dealt with by them."</p>
        <p>Lady McLaren remarked: "If you knew that Elouse of Commons as well as I know it you would not trust them now."</p>
        <p>Mrs.Reamont stated that" they had only one friend in the House of Commons, and that was Mr. Keir Hardie." And strange to Say Mr. Keir Hardie is a Socialist.</p>
        <p>TRADES COUNCIL AND LABOUR REPRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>As indicated by "John Workman" in last week's " Examiner " the Trades Council has decided to secede from the Warrington Labour Representation Com- mittee and to contest elections with candidates selected from the branches constituting the Council. It is a nr-ttter for regret that this has become necessary, but the affiliated members having decided to take this step, there was no alternative for the Council but to accept their decision and act accordingly. In the opinion of some of the L.R.C. members, that organisa- tion might have been saved, had the Trades Council continued its membership, and the Independent Labour party rejoined. The decision of the trades unionists, however, is distinctly antagonistic to that opinion, and it is scarcely probable that the I.L.P. will be anxious just yet to amalgamate with the trades unionists after the bitter opposition shewn by the latter when the I.L.P. was connected with the L.R.C. Mbny of us claim that it is owing to the work of the Independent Labour Party during the past twelve years that labour representation has become possible. The experience of those who are in touch with the general labour movement is that where there is a strong and active branch of the I.L.P. there the chances of success for both local and Parliamentary candidates are brightest. The I.L.P. has always been willing to work with the trades unionists for labour representa- tion on definite independent. lines, but strongly objects to anyone outside the party attempting to dictate what its policy shall be. However, enough has been said on that score for the present. The decision of the Trades Council will mean an alteration in the rules and an increased subscription from the members. The best test of the desire and enthusiasm for labour representation amongft the Warrington trade unionists will be when they ?ire called upon to pro- vide the necessary funds. I trust the rank and file will not shirk the responsibility, but will recognise that if they are to have the representation to which they are entitled, they must be prepared to pay for it. Some weeks ago I outlined a scheme in this column which could serve as a basis for the Trades Council in considering a scheme for raising the necessary funds. Although the decision of the Council is not what 'many of us would have preferred, it is a distinct step in advance of the old regime. The refusal to amalgamate with the Socialists in one organisation, will not, as far as I can judge, prevent the Trades Council from co-operating with the I.L.P. in any Municipal or Parliamentary election, or upon any definite question which directly affects the workers. What is required is tact, and a measure of toleration by those who are at the head of the various local labour organisations. I trust that those who have been opposed to the action of the Trades Council will not " sulk in their tents " but that each will try to work harmoniously, with one object in view, and that object the ultimate unity of labour. •</p>
        <p>THE SCOUT.</p>
        <p>SOCIALISM CRITICISED.</p>
        <p>Sir, —ln your column of Saturday last I noticed tbe following quotation from Mr. Russell-Witliams' Bridge Foot orations :</p>
        <p>"Socialism is the recognition of the law of our social being. It is the principle of State interference carried to its logical conclusion under democratic auspiCes. It is the application of the principle to politics that all things that are socially needed ought to be Socially owned." The generalapplication of the principles embodied in this short paragraph would appear (to the superficial observer), to provide the key which would eventually solve all our great complex social problems , but a close inspection would result in the discovery that (like most Socialists' Utopian dreams), the ideals proclaimed therein are absolutely unattainable and the whole argument is a complete contradiction to recognised historical facts and our every-day human experience. What is this "law of our social being" of which Socialism is supposed to be the recogni- tion? Is it not a generally accepted scientific axiom • that the "struggle for existence with its survival of the fittest is Nature's great fundamental law, " which is, I claim, diametrically opposed to the Sooialist ideal of "each for all and all for each." Further than this, sir, "The principle of State interference carried to its logical conclusion under democratic auspices" is certainly a well- rounded sentence but apparently requires a little explanation. Does Mr. Williams sincerely believe that the Stop should dictate to the individual what shall be the nature of his personal habits or religious creed, interfere with the domestic affairs of the family, i and also undermine parental responsibility on the assumption that as the children of to-day will become the citizens of the future they " ought to be socially owned ?" In .Acts 2nd Verses 41 and 45 we read thus. " And all that believed were together and had all things common. Ahd sold their possessions and parted them Ito all men as every man had need." The apostolic commune, sir, was not by any t means a permanent success aml I would also direct your attention to the recent failure of the Daisy Colony Scheme and th</p>
        <p>unsuccessful attempts of the Doukhobors to live together decently on communistic lines. The heroic, self-sacrificing efforts at cJlonisation made by the late Robert Owen and the Reign of Terror that followed the French Revolution are further striking examples of the impracticability of efficiently controlling a society under "democratic auspices." What sub- itantial reasons have our Socialist friends for suppos- ing that, in spite of the complete failure of all past communistic efforts, their ideals will not prove most unreliable guides for progressive action in the future.?</p>
        <p>In his short essay in your last week's column "Stanley Lord" states (when advocating the Municipalization of the Drink Traffic) :—" a large revenue would be obtained each year which would probably suffice to raze all the slums and build in. their stead healthy and beautiful dwellings ; in. a few years making 'Beautiful Warrington' a fact, and not as now an empty and ironical phrase." Does your contributor sincerely believe that the most effective method to make Warrington beautiful is by the further degradation of its unfortunate besotted inhabitants?</p>
        <p>As a practical alternative to his erroneous quack remedy, I suggest that the only feasible plan for making a "Beautiful Warrington" is by a systematic and persistent policy of individual reclamation which would gradually but surely change our drink-cursed community into a town of sober, intelligent, indus- trious, thrifty citizens, who would necessarily demand and could then well afford improved dwelling accommo- dation, and thus be enabled to live amidst more pleasant surroundings, and in a state of steadily increasing prosperity.</p>
        <p>- "A PRACTICAL REFORMER." ANSWERS TO CORRESPONDENTS. RATIONALIST.—(I) I would advise you to read William Morris's "News from Nowhere." (2) Yes! "Hubert" of "The Sunday Chronicle" is a Fabian. He has recently been re-elected Honorary Treasurer without opposition. (3) There are only two members of the Fabian Society in Warrington, so far as I know, the Rev. John Francis Matthews and Mr. Edmund Osborne. G.H.o.—Vast armaments ought to be superseded by differert methods. It would be serious jest now, to be without an ally in the East. OwEnsm.—(l) There is a handy little "Life of Robert Owen," by Mr. R. E. Davies. The price, I think, is is. 6d. net. The publishers Robert Sutton, 43, The Exchange, Southwark. (2) He is considered to be the founder of English Socialism.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>WARRING fON'S MUSICAL TALENT.</head>
        <p>WARRING fON'S MUSICAL TALENT.</p>
        <p>Some Notable Successes.</p>
        <p>Warrington's musical talent was again to the fore last week-end and some highly creditable successes were achieved.</p>
        <p>At the Shrewsbury Musical Festival on Whit Monday our two local male voice choirs again found themselves in competition and both had the satisfac- tion of winning prizes. The test piece for this contest was "Destruction of Gaza" (De Riltr) and five choirs competed. Dr. MeNaught was the adjudicator and he awarded the first prize of £3O (with gold medal inscribed with Borough Arms, for the conductor) to the Warrington Male Voice Choir conducted by Mr. S. Hassan. He spoke very highly of their interpretation of the piece, pointing out that they were a well-trained and well-balanced choir and infused into their sing- ing that excitement so characteristic of the piece. They were particularly strong in the tenors but the bass did not suffer on that account. The second prize of 15 was awarded to the Warring- ton Apollo Choir, under the conductorship of Mr. Harry Berrey. The total number of marks obtainable was 80, and the adjudicator made his awards as follows :—Warrington Male Voice, 75; Warrington Apollo, 73; Wigan Harmonic, 72; Birmingham Victoria, fl; Midland G-leemen (55. The Lyric Quartette Party (thsses A. Woodland, L Fairhurst and Messrs.R.W.Cook and P.J.George)added another prize to their list of vic ories, by gaining first at the Shrewsbury Festival. The test was open to Mixed Quartettes who gave pieces of their own choice, and proved a great success as there were eight entries, hailing from Birmingham, Wales and Shrewsbury. The Lyric Quartette sang "The parting kiss" (Pinsuiti) and Dr. McNaught in awarding them the prize of £5 complimented the Quartette on rendering such a delicate and beautiful but difficult piece. He said that they were a real quartette and they sang well together. The very essence of quartette singing was for each part to listen to the other and he could see that this party did so. He hoped they would practise well together and make a first-class quartette. It is interesting to note the Lyric Quartette has been suceessful at the following Musical Eisteddfodau : Ashton-in-Makerfield, Prescot, St. Helens, Leigh, Golborne, Warrington, Earlestown and Shrewsbury, losing at Llanwrst, Chester and New Brighton. They were specially coached for the Shrewsbury Festival by Mr. Harry Berrey, of Sr. Helens. The award for the mixed choir competition, in which the test piece was "How sweet the Calm " (Blackburn), went to Tregynon (Montgomeryshire), with Winmatleigh Madgrial Society, Warrington (conductor Mr. Harry Berrey) secmd. There were seven competing choirs.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Crosfield's band has inaugurated what shows every likelihood of proving one of its most successful seasons. On Saturday fast at Darvren it competed with 11 other bands, among which were such notable combinations as Irwell Springs, Shaw and Hebden Bridge, with the result that it won the second place in both March and Selection, the first place in the former going to Irwell Springs, while in the latter Goodshaw won first place, Horwich Old, and Irwell Springs being respectively third and fourth. Even this success was however eclipsed on. Monday last when at the Shrewsbury Ca,ntest, Oros- -fields were beaten in the March only by Lindley and in the Selection carried off the first prize in front of such bands as Royal Oakley, Lindley and Wyte Temperance to which the three following places were awarded. To-day our townsmen compete for the New Brighton Tower Cup, and in view of the honours so recently won it is not surprising that notwithstand- ing that they meet nearly a score of the best bands in England they entertain the highest hopes of success. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON V. PRESTON.-IVarrington had only a poor team for this important match at the West Cliff ground Preston, on Thursday, but thanks to a meritor- ious bowling performance by B. Silcock—seven wickets for 33 runs—Preston were dismissed for the small aggregate of 56. The wicket was all in favour of the bowlers, for rain had fallen consistently throughout the morning. Good batting by W. Richardson H. 0. Adams and A. Duckworth placed Warrington in a favourable position but a " rot " set in and it was not until the last pair of batsmen W. Bennett and J. Maywall became associated that the Preston score was exceeded. Score :</p>
        <p>PRESTON. WARRINGTON. 0. Haworth bCresswell... A T. Wright b Cook 8 H. Matthews b Silcock... W W. Richardson b Cook 11 W. J. Cravin b Siloock... 7 Cresswell b Clew H. G. Sags Cook b Cresswell W. F. Chew b Silcock F. Halida.y c Silcock b- McCall 0 Cresswell C. Eastward c Bennett b A. Gleave b Cook gilcock H. W. Clemeshaw not out 4 Cook 1 McCall st Duckworth b W. Bennett c Edge b Silcock Grindley b Silcock 0 J. Waywell not out ... 6 Extras</p>
        <p>Total ...... 56 BOWLING ANALYSIS.</p>
        <p>'rota ....... 68</p>
        <p>0. N. L. W. Cresswell B. Silcock 19.3 5 ... 33 ... 7</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER AND DISTRICT BREWERY LEAGUE.— Walker's Brewery played their second match in the above league on Saturday with Cornbrook Brewery (Manchester), at Manchester, and after an exciting finish won by eight runs. At tbe fall of the eighth wicket the Brewery required 11 runs to win, and by steady batting by J. Norman these. were • made before the fall of the ninth wicket. J. Dargbn took 4 wickets for 10 runs, and J. Norman 4 for 27.—Cornbrook Brewery, 40; Walker's Brewery, 48.</p>
        <p>AMERICAN CORNED BEEF FOR THE ARMY. Recent instructions have been sent out by the War Office to all the Commanders in the Home Station to issue American Corned Beef weekly to each soldier in the Army. The War Office state in their letter to the various officers in charge that the establishments in which the brands of American tinned meats now being isaued to the troops are manufactured, have been most carefully inspected by officers sent specially to the United States for the purpose of watching the Army contracts packed. The sanitary conditions have been thoroughly examined and the reports of the inspection sent home have satisfied the Army Council that the selection of the cattle, the quality of American Corned Beef used, the conditions under which it is prepared, and the whole process of canning by the firms supplying the Army, are in every way satisfactory. This action of the War Office is a guarantee to every family in the United Kingdom of the high quality and purity of American tinned meats.</p>
      </div>
