      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Character Sketch</head>
        <p>A Character Sketch</p>
        <p>Before Richard Burdon Haldane was two years old his governess one day discovered him busily working away at a pile of dust. When asked the object of his labour, he staggered his questioner with the reply: "If God Made a man out of the dust of the earth, why shouldn't I?" That was a pretty fair beginning for an Army reformer. Always active and sagacious, his elders were wont to be interested in the wise advice he used occasionally to bestow upon his younger brothers. "You should hear and see everything, but do not make it your own," was not bad from a young Socrates of five or six years old. So few have been admitted to the privilege of Mr. Haldane's friendship that less is known of him in his private life than of perhaps any other Cabinet Minister and this fact gives all the more interest to a lively, ,character-sketch which appears with many anecdotes and special photographs in the' Play number of the "Pall Mall Magazine." To understand (writes the author) the busy methodical life he leads one has need to see Mr. Haldane at various hours of the day in his London house, No. 28, Queen Anne's Gate, West- minster. Quite close to the House of Commons, very near to Bnckingham Palace, and' but some few minutes' walk to the new War Office, the house,itself is quaintly old and seductively quiet. Mr. Haldane will generally be found absorbed in work at an early hour in the morning. Frequently at 10.15 a.m. hi a man will enter softly to remind him that there is a Cabinet Council at 10.30 a.m., a,nd that at this hour the active part of the day's public work has real need to commence. First the Cabinet Council meeting, then the attendance at the War Office, to be followed at 2.45 p.m. by answering questions at the House of Commons or parrying jibes from the Opposition benches. This routine does not end his day, but is followed by more departmental work, if the debate in the House does not. need the constant presence of the War Minister. Mr. Haldane was one of the two Ministers of the Cabinet who sat right through the memorable all-night sitting on the Land Tenure Bill. That morning my appointment was fixed with him at ten o'clock,and although this man had been three nights out of bed, there he was on Tuesday morning doing his work in precisely his usual fashion. When quietly asked," How is it you contrive to get through such an enormous amount of work ? " the brief, characteristic reply was contained in the three words" Organization and method." I fancy a strange,- calling at this house for the first time could scarce fail to notice everywhere evidences of careful organization and precision of method, and one wonders if the subject of this article may have perfected during his stay in that land of military exactitude Germany these and other qualities. Even now, if the curious pressed Mr. Haldane as to whether he does not read a good deal of philosophy, ' he will admit that he does, and that late at night, when the pressure of his immense administrative work is temporarily relaxed, he loves to settle down in his armchair here, and, the workaday world forgetting, -commune with the greatest minds in the realms of abstract thought. Naturally enough, such a man is the proud possessor of an excellent collection of books, •covering the widest possible range of subjects ; but 31r. Haldane will tell you that quite a large number of these are upon an oldest and at the same time that most -up-to-date science of all—the science of war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>KING AND THE POOR.</head>
        <p>KING AND THE POOR.</p>
        <p>Ragged School Union Officials Visit Buckingham Palace</p>
        <p>Lord Northampton, the President, and Mr. John Kirk, the Secretary, of the Ragged School Union, were received by the King in Bnckingham Palace at eleven ,o'clock on Thursday morning. The object of this special Royal recognition is to mark the celebration of the fortieth year of Mr. Kirks's official connection with the Union. "The King has always been interested in our Union," said Mr. Kirk to a "Daily Neva" representative. "The late Queen Victoria and the PTince Consort contributed to our funds as early as 1849, and some years later her 141ajesty became our patron. At her death King Edward consented to follow her as patron. "We have always received a great deal of kindness from members of the Royal Family. Princess Christian, for instance, started a Holiday Home for boys at "Windsor years ago, and so pleased has she been with its success that she has collected money to build a new structure at Englefield Grden, just outside the Royal borough ; so we shall be sending poor children down there for many years, I expect." "The present Court of Britain," he declared,fervently, "is a model as regards matters of this kind. If the aristocracy generally would follow Royalty more than they do in this respect I think it would be better for the country." "The outlook" continn edMr.Kirk "is most encourag- ing. Enormous advances have been made, but we haven't reae.hed the goal yet—the State care of poor children. Call it Socialism or what you like; it has to be done if the position of the nation is to be held, physically, mentally, and morally." MR. KIRK KNIGHTED.</p>
        <p>The interview with his Majesty lasted for about half an hour. His Majesty heartily congratulated Mr. Kirk on the magnificent results of his efforts in connec- tion with the institution, and conversed at length with him respecting the progress of the work. The King expressed the warmest sympathy and good wishes for the future prosperity of Mr. Kirk's various charities. At the conclusion of the interview his Majesty con- ferred the honour of knighthood upon Mr. Kirk. Inn statement which he issued shortly after his return from Bneingham Palace, Sir John Kirk says "The King was very much interested in the work of the Society, and knew what an influence it had been nmongst thonsands of children, and to show his appre- ciation of what }-ad been done, and to signalise my forty years' continuous service, he wished to make me a knight. He added that it was also the particular wish of the Prime Minister that this honour should be conferred. I manifested some reluctance, but was urged by Lord Northampton that the favour and kind- ness must he accepted, whereupon his Majesty rang a 'bell. called for a sword, and bade me kneel down. I then felt a distinct tan on each shoulder, and was bade then to rise as " Sir John Kirk," receiving a hearty grip of the hand and his Majesty's warmest congratula- tions to be given to Lady Kirk, and we then quietly retired with his Majesty's good wishes for my future</p>
        <p>service. Tn intnrview snhsequenly. Sir John Kirk says : "I certainly should not have taken the honour if I had known of it, beforehand. I had not the slightest idea that big Mai`ltY would confer so great an honour upon e -roc,. I have found ont since that Lord Northampton did know of it, but he kept it secret."</p>
        <p>THE RATES.</p>
        <p>-- - St. Helens rates have been raised by the remarkable 'sum of 7d. this year, chiefly on account of heavy educa- tion expenditure due to two new schools which will not receive grants this year. , LARGE SEWERAGE SCHEME FOR ST. HELENS. At the meeting of the St. Helens Health Committee on Wednesday, the Town Clerk reported that the Local Government Board had sanctioned the borrowing of £18,040 in connection with the Parr and Sutton sewer- age scheme, and the sum of £750, the amount overspent on the Robins-lane sewer. The Chairman (Alderman Forster) said the Corporation bad already practically approved of the plans,but as it was over five years since</p>
        <p>The charge against John Thomas Eyres. a silk weaver of Maccesfield, of causing the death of Maria Bowrs. found drowned in a.canal,vvas withdrawn on Wednesday. .owing to lack of evidence.</p>
        <p>. • •i. ~. .s*,r. ' , .)-,,,, :, •. . . „ ~. , . ~ , , ..,...,... /1 ...• EA Ns ~. . -r' , for ILIDUSNESS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>LIBERAL WORK.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL WORK.</p>
        <p>[By J. HOWESj.</p>
        <p>The Liberal party has difficulties to contend with quite unknown to its political opponents. When the Tories are in office they have things very much their own way. They have a majority in both Chambers. The Liberals in the House of Commons are always willing to consider, on their merits, any Bills their opponents may introduce ; and if anything for the benefit of the people or country is found therein, the Liberals are not only willing to support them in debate, but even to go into the Tory lobby and vote with the Government, in older to save the Bill from danger. This has frequently happened in the past, n fact every bit of Radicalism obtained from Tory Governments has been obtained by the votes of the Liberals.</p>
        <p>The Liberal Press has also dealt fairly with the Tory party, and when it has been necessary to criti- cise their actions most severely, this has been done without the coarse personal abuse so often indulged in by the other side. When- the Liberals are in power, what a change comes o'er the scene! They have only a majority in one Chambor.with a dead set majority against them in the other. The Tories in the House of Commons at once commence a system of obstruction and delay. No feelings of patriotism prevent them from attacking in the most reckless manner negotiations which the Liberal Government may be carrying out with Colonial friends or Foreign Powers, and the Yellow Press in the country let loose their fury and endeavour to smother the Liberal administration with calumny and abuse. Under these circumstances it is little wonder that from time to time Liberal Governments are unable to carry out the whole of their programme. The wonder is that they ate able to do anything at all. The present Government has had to face all the difficulties referred to. They followed an administra- tion that had left everythin in muddle and confusion. Expenditure high, debt and' taxes increased; foreign complications created; and home legislation i neglected, and although they have only been n office short of two years, they have succeeded in doing an amount of useful work that does credit to them, and will confer great advantage upon the country. In their first Budget, they took one penny off the tea duty and abolished the coal tax, and paid over £20,000,000 of the National debt, besides reducing the duty on tobacco. In their second Budget, they made a further large reduction of debt, graduated the income tax, re- ducing by 2d. in the pound the tax on all incomes earned by trade and industry,and by increasing the death duties upon the enormous fortunes left by the deceased, the Chancellor was able to ear-mark a nest-egg for Old Age Pensions, the commencement of which scheme is promised for next year. . In home legislation, they have done more during the short period of their existence than was accom- plished by their Tory predecessors during the whole of their long reign of power. Something substantial has been done for those who "go down - to the sea in ships." Our merchant seamen have very few champions, and for a very long time their interest has been, sadly neglected. The Merchant Shipping Act of the present Government contains a great many improvements for the British sailor, and protects him from unfair competition. The British loadline must now be adopted by foreign vessels entering our ports. Unsafe foreign ships may be detained. Grain cargoes must be loaded in a safer manner. Foreign seamen are prohibited if they are not familiar with tl- 'English language. Provisions for seamen to be inc-eased in quality. Overcrowding of passengers prohibited. Additional inspection of pro- visions and water. Certified cooks to he provided for foreign going ships. Property and wages of seaman dying on voyage to be preserved for living relatives. Seamen not to be discharged out of United Kingdom. If a seaman receives any butt or injury in the service of the ship, or suffers from any illness, not due to his own wilful act, the expense ok surgical and medical advice, attendance and medicine, also the expense of maintenance until he is cured,or dies,or is returned to a proper return post, and in the case of death the expenses of his burial, must all be defrayed by the owner of the ship, without any deduction on that account from his wages. Provision is also made for relief and maintenance of distressed shipwrecked sailors. British sailors must not be forced on shore or left behind to suit the convenience of owner or commander. Crew space on board ship to be increased; greater facilities to be given to seamen for remitting their wages and several other matters of improve- ment are contained in this most comprehensive enactment.</p>
        <p>The Workmen's Compensation Act provides the worker with compensation from the end of the first week of disablement; prevents contracting out, improves the position of the disabled workers In case of the employer becoming bankrupt, extends the benefits of compensation to seamen, domestic servants, and workmen in employment of the Crown; increases the amount of compensation by allowing revisions of scale during the period of disablement, and is a great boon to all workers who may unfortunately be over- taken by calamitous accidents.</p>
        <p>The trades Disputes Act gives complete prctection to trades union funds, making it impassible for any wealthy corporation or others to divert these funds from the object for which they were contributed. It also establishes the legal right of peaceful picketing. The Agricultural Holdings Act gives the tenant compensation for damage by game; freedom of cropping and disposal of product; compensation for unreasonable disturbance; power to make permanent necessary improvements, without consent of landlord, and to obtain compensation for the same when leaving the holding. The Amended Ground Game Act gives the tenant farmer extended power to kill and take ground game. The Town Tenants (Ireland) Act, gives the tenants of shops and other buildingl'in Ireland, compensation for improvements,_ compensation for unreasonable dis- turbance, and prohibits contracting out. The Provision of Meals Act provides' for meals being given to hungry children in our public schools. The Liberal Government has abolished corporal punishment in military prisons, provided the poor peasantry of Ireland with seed potatoes, and enacted that the receipt of this assistance shall not deprive the tenants of their vote. In- Scotland, where it frequently happens that schools are a considerable distance from the homes of the children, provision is made for the medical inspection and where iequireci for the conveyance to and from school, of epileptic or crippled or defective children between five and 16 years of ao. The property and residential qualification for county magistrates has been abolished. The English farmer receives further substantial advantage from the Fertilisers and Feeding Stuffs Act, and from the Injury to Live Stock by Dogs Act. Great reforms have been inaugurated in the Post Office Department ; trades Unionism recognised ; wages increased etc., and although the matters here recited form only a portion of the achievements of the present Government, yet this work alone should entitle them to the enthusiastic support of the people. If you have a dish filled with water, and you throw into it a large sponge you will speedily discover that the dish becomes empty as the sponge becomes full. Now, if you want to make the sponge give back what it has taken you must wring it well. I don't wish to say that all sponges are Tories,' but it does appear to me that most official Tories are sponges. If you want them to yield anything you must wring them well, but with the Liberal party the wringing is much less necessary. Liberals live to make reform, Tories live to resist it, and that is just the difference between the two sets of politicians.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>FEARNHEAD.</head>
        <p>FEARNHEAD.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S TREA.T.—Tho children of Pearnhead and Houghton Green once again received their annual Whit- Monday treat generously provided by Mrs. Mather,wife of Mr. George :gather, of Cinnamon Brow. After play- lug games in the field daring the afternoon an adjourn- ment was made to the large Dutch barn where tea was served. During the evening the band of the Padgate Cottage Homes, under the directions of Mr. Yorke, played a lively selection of music, and dancing was indulged in by large numbers of young people, who had joined the children. Before dispersing each child was presented with an orange and some nuts, and hearty cheers were given for Mr. and Mrs. Mather.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>BURY 10 LIVERPOOL BY CAR.</head>
        <p>BURY 10 LIVERPOOL BY CAR.</p>
        <p>A through service of tramcars between Bury and Bolton was commenced on Monday morning, the Bury cars going to Bolton Post Office and the Bolton cars to the centre of Bury. This gives direct tramway com- munication with a few changes of cars, from Bury, Rochdale, &amp;c., to Liverpool Landing-stage. On Sunday afternoon the Bury cars ran to Breightmet, Where Patisengers changed to the Bolton cars. The cars were well patronised. The changes of cats at Breightmet is now obviated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 25, 1907.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>ODDS AND ENDS.</head>
        <p>ODDS AND ENDS.</p>
        <p>One of the questions asked at a Bristol school examination was: "What is a prophet?" Ore of the answers was: "A man that's put is a fiery furnace, and he won't burn."</p>
        <p>"I am told you went in for epeculation on the Stock Exchange lately," said one tradesman to another. "Were you a bull or a bear?" "Neither. I was an ass!"</p>
        <p>May: "Do you think one should marry for love or for money?" Chaperon: "My dear, love is an excuse for marriage, but money is a justification."</p>
        <p>Mr. Paurer : "I didn't know you painted." Mr. Newrieh : "I don't." Mr. Paurer : "But your portrait &amp;aye, 'Thomas Newrieh—by Him- self.'" Mr. Newrieh : "Wall, ain't I by my- self? There ain't nobody else about."</p>
        <p>Cynic (savagely) : "They say the fashionable mother of to-day recognises her baby only by looking at the nurse." Fashionable Mother (unmoved) : "How extraordinaTily clever when one changes nurses so often! I always tell ours by the mail-cart."</p>
        <p>Lawyer: "Where is that sign, 'Back in ten minutes?" Boy: "The man in the next office borrowed it. He said he wanted to go to see the international football match."</p>
        <p>Mr. Suburbs: "Do you expect any visitors to-night, my deer?" Mrs. S.: "Well, consider- ing that Bridget is going to leave, baby is teething, the cellar is flooded, and the grooer hasn't called for two dayr3—yes, I do."</p>
        <p>Pa Twaddle": "Why are you spanking Tommy?" Ma Twiddles: "He needs a lesson, and I'm impressing it en his mind." Pa Twatidles: "Well, you've got a queer idea 11.41 to where the lad's mind is situated."</p>
        <p>"I can't decide," she said, "whether to take the hat or not. But it is just the dearest thing I have seen this season." "The dearest?" asked the husband, with it• sardonic laugh. "Then it's certain that you'll take it."</p>
        <p>Tourist: ou must have lived a good many years, my friend?" lodge: "Yee, yevers might there, sir—ever since I were a little lad."</p>
        <p>Geoffrey: "Father, they eay that hiatory repeats itself, don't they?" Parent: "Yes, my son." Geoffrey: "Well, why don't it repeat itself when I'm trying to learn it?"</p>
        <p>Uncle William : "Yes, Willie, I have had my nose to the grindstone all my life." Willie: "Is that what made it so red, uncle?"</p>
        <p>Police-Sergeant: "Constable 4,001 wants • a new uniform, sir. He gays his present one is getting too tight." . Inspector: "Ah! What beat is he on?" Sergeant: "Grosvenor-terrace, sir." Inspector: "Oh! Then transfer him to Candle-lane."</p>
        <p>• Guest: "Bring me a steak. I was here yes- terday. I guess you remember me?" Waiter: "Yes, sir. Will you have the same to-day?" Guest: "Yes! if nobody is using it."</p>
        <p>Counsel: "And what was the defendant doing meanwhile?" Witness: "He was telling me a funny story." "Remember, sir, that yon are under oath." "Well, then, he was telling mie a story."</p>
        <p>"Ah, your languags! Eet ees ECI, difficult." "What's the matter, count?" "First, zits novel eet say ze man was unhorsed." "Yes?" "Zen it say he was cowed."</p>
        <p>Slocombe (proudly): "There isn't a man living Who could successfully forge my name to a cheque and get it ,cashed." Hodges: "Ii your signature so peculiar?" "No; but I haven't any money in a bank."</p>
        <p>She: "Do you know what I'd do if you should try to kiss me?" He: "ITo. Why?" "Oh, nothing; only you don't seem to have any curiosity."</p>
        <p>Dauber: "Look here, old fellow; I'm painting a picture and want to study the expression of surprise—utter, entire, unbounded surprise. How am I to achieve it?" Friend: "Oh, I know; go and pay some of the people you .are owing money to."</p>
        <p>Friend: "I Supp ose you always like to get s patient who has no bad habits?" Doctor: "Indeed', I don't." "How's that?" "Why, I can't order him to stop anything."</p>
        <p>M. Prudhomme : "I received an anonymous letter this morning." M. Bonhomme : ,"Poof ! The vile things. Whenever I receive an anony- mous letter I do not take the trouble to open it."</p>
        <p>"And WIIIS he much oast down when her father refused his consent?" "No—only one flight!"</p>
        <p>"I've got a w-ashing machine here," began the inventor. The capitalist looked at him in the cold, calculating manner common to capitalists, and answered: it I were you, I'd run straight home and use it." That night the Anarchist band received another application for e mbe reh i p</p>
        <p>Artist: "You'd be 'surprised' if you knew the amount of time spent on that picture." Friend: "Yee ; I hear that people eland in ffont of it for hours trying to make out what it ie."</p>
        <p>"Yes," said the emphatic lady "I tell you that women ought to enjoy the same rights as men." "The same rights, Matilda?" returned her hus- band. "Do you mean that they should be tram- car conduotors and policemen?" "Certainly." "And---and soldiers?" "By all means:" "Then lam in favour of it. Don't you think you would like to enlist, Matilda?"</p>
        <p>"Only five years ago I started with our firm sA a pound a week," said Hewitt, "and now I earn ten pounds a week without trouble." "Males so; it's easy to earn that," replied Newitt, "but thow much do you get?"</p>
        <p>Doctor: "I fear your heart is affected, miss. Did you ever experience a smothering sensa- tion?", Miss Spooney : "Oh, yes; often." Doctor: "Ah ! At what times?" Mite Spooney: "Well. usualy right after Algernon turns the gas down."</p>
        <p>He: "There is a, certain young lady deeply intereste4d in me, and while I like her, you know, still I never could love her. I want to put an end to it without breaking the poor girl's heart. Can you suggest ady plan?" She: "Do you ca,ll there often?" He:. "No, indeed; not any oftener than I can poesibly help." She: "Call oftener."</p>
        <p>• Smith: "He took several good-sized sticks sto beat his wife." Brown: "I cannot believe he could be guilty of such cruelty." Smith: "Cruelty? There wasn't any cruelty about it. They were playing golf together."</p>
        <p>"What became of the Cromwell relics you had on exhibition here?" asked a gentleman of the museum attendant. "Let me see," replied that resourceful worthy. "Oh, yes, they wire re- turned to Mr. Cromwell last week."</p>
        <p>Mamma : "Ethel, my dear, do you think that Mr. Spooner, who calls 6o regularly and siAs with you in the parlour every night, has serious intentions?" Ethel: "I neatly don'it know mamma ; he keeps one so much in the dark."</p>
        <p>"But you sale not always bothered with poor light, „are you?," inquired the complaint clerk at the gas-offiee. "Oh, no, not always," replied the quiet citizen. "-Alf, 1 thought so; it's only at certain times that you notice it, eh?" "Yee; only afber dark."</p>
        <p>Did your daughter take physical culture lessons while at college?" asked the visitor. "Oh, yes," replied Mrs. Goldrox proudly. " She's got so now she can elevate her little finger just lovely when she eats soup."</p>
        <p>The distinguished alienist looked worried. "No," he said to the reporter, "I can't give you an opinion as to the sanity of the prisoner." "But surely you have considered the ease?" "It isn't that, ' replied the alienist, "but you see, each side has sent me a retainer, and as these are the same amounts, I am, of course, in temporary doubt."</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NIECES AND NEPHEWS, Well, how have you all enjoyed Whitsun? I suppose most of you received the usual week's holiday, in which case there is no doubt of you having had a fine old time. Now, no more holidays until Midsummer, and consider- ing the swiftness of time, it won't be long to wait. So good luck to you all at school for the next few weeks. I am pleased to say that our "Corner" brightens up with the weather, and the con- tinued increase of members is very, very grati- fying. This week I am presenting the prize to Nellie i Andrews, for the little bit about Old Rinkrank, while next week I am awarding the usual weekly 10 the relation who sends me the funniest drawing of a pig, upon a picture post- card. I don't want you to put a lot of work into it, but see what you can do with just a few lines; in fact, the fewer the better will stand the best chance of the prize. Until the arrival of the piitcards, an revoir. UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>WELCOME' TO Fanny Sikook, Laura Simms, Annie Bateman, Ethel Watts, Norman R. H. Styrin, Arthur Bush, Frank ud(l.</p>
        <p>OLD RINKRANK. "IP Once upon a time there lived a king, who ad a daughter. He ordered a glass mountain o he constructed, and said, "Whoever can walk over the mountain shall have my daughter.". Now, the princess was very fond of one of the courtiers, and he came to the king and asked for her hand. The king said that he could have her if he oould walk over the glass mountain. The princess offered to go with him to help him up if he slipped. So they both started, and when they got to the middle the princess slipped and fell. The glass mountain suddenly, opened, and in she fell. The bridegroom looked for her in vain, but he could not find the place where she was buried, and the king gave orders that the mountain should be pulled down. After this, they could not find her. Meanwhile the princess had descended into a deep pit, where she met an old man with a tremendously long beard. "If you will be my servant," he said to her, "and do what I tell you, you shall live; but otherwise you must die." So she did all that she was bid; she cooked his dinner, made his bed, and cleaned the rooms. At night he used to come home, bringing heaps of gold and silver. After she had served him for a good many years he said, "I will call' you 'Madame Rosy Cheek,' and you must call me Old Rinkrank." One day, when he was out, the princess did her work, and shut up all the windows and doors except one little tram-door. When Old Rinkrank came home he knocked and shouted, "Madame Rosy Cheek, open the door!" but she reprred, "No, I won't!" Then he cried, "Here stands poor Old. Rinkrank, on his seventeen long shanks and his one golden foot. Madame Rosy Cheek, have you washed my dishes?" "Yes," she replied. Then he repeated, "Here stands poor Old Rink- rank," etc., again. "Madame, have you made my bed- "Yes, I have," she said. Then he geld it again, and "Madame, open the door lt.He walked Found the house to see if the trap-door was open, and it was; so he put his head in. When she saw this, she shut the trap-door, and Old Rinkrank began to howl and yell. But she said she would only give him his liberty on condition that he told her where he kept the ladder by which he climbed up on the earth. And at last lie told her. The princess was now able to go back to her father, and she was manied to her faithful lover. NELLIE ANDREWS. THE KITE AND THE PIGEONS.</p>
        <p>Some pigeons had long lived in fear or a kite, but by being always on the alert, and keeping near their dove-cote, they had hitherto escaped the attacks of the enemy. Finding himself unsuccessful, the kite tried craft. "Why,' said he, "do you prefer this life of continual anxiety, when, if you would only make me your king, I would secure you from every attack that could be made upon you?" The pigeons, trusting to his word, called him to the throne; but no sooner was he made king than he abused his authority by devouring a pigeon &amp; day. Whereupon one that yet awaited his turn. said no more than, "It serves us right,"</p>
        <p>HAROLD HALL.</p>
        <p>WHY THE SEA SOBS. . The sea no father has, Nor a.ny mother; A trouble quite enough</p>
        <p>One's mind to bother. That's why,, my dear, Where'er it be, We sometimes hear it A sobbing sea. If we no fathers had, Or loving mothers, No little sisters fair, No baby brothers', We'd shed a tear--</p>
        <p>(Poor you, poor me) And sigh; "Oh, dear," Just like tha sea.</p>
        <p>DAISY STOCKS.,</p>
        <p>LIFE'S SPRING. Oh Breath of Spring most beautiful, We're glad, for thou art near, The gloom of winter to dispel, And bring to us good cheer. ' The farmer is so glad to see</p>
        <p>Thy smiling face once more, And in his mind he pictures now, Another threshing floor. The Silting of life is beautiful As that of Nature dear, But just one thing about it is— It- comes but once a year. This Spring of ours is very short,</p>
        <p>-Sc) we must use it well; Arid if we use 'it otherwise,</p>
        <p>Its wrollgs will have to tell. Sc. youth. v,-11.?lever thou. dost go,</p>
        <p>Scatter thy sunshine fair; that dear, chance will come but onoe, And then for ever disnapear. NORMAN R. H. STYRIN.</p>
