      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>Proposed Alterations to the Rules.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Northern Rugby Union will he held next month when several important pro- posals to alter the existing rules will be made. It is proposed by the Committee to delete the name of Brighouse Rangers from the list of founders having seats on the committee. In connection with the same matter, Swinton and Salford, who are not amongst the founders,are putting forward a resolution to entirely do away with the present privilege of the foundation clubs in the matter of representation and to substitute a committee of twenty members—nine from Yorkshire, nine from Lancashire, and two from Cumberland. The other proposition is made by Hull Kingston Rovers, and seconded by Rnncorn, and, whilst retaining the prin- ciple of the foundation clubs having representation as at present, allocates three seats to Lancashire clubs other than founders, three to similar clubs in Yorkshire, one to Cumberland County, and the remainder to other clubs, districts, or counties at the discretibn of the committee, the seats other than founders to be elected at the annual meeting. - - _</p>
        <p>In the professional rules, the COmmittee propose to take power to grant a transfer to a player, and for that player to play with another club in case of breach of the professional rule, which provides that all agree- ments between clubs and players shall be in writing, and shall not be terminable during a current season without the consent of the Committee.</p>
        <p>The Committee propose to dilvideibe Challenge Cup Competition into a qualifying stage and the competition proper, 26 clubs being exempted from the former, and the remaining entrants drawn to leave six to join the first round proper. They also propose that in the case of a tie having to be replayed on the ground of a prO- test the Union Committee may fix the venue. The Committee also propose to alter the rule with regard to attendances on the field for injured players to read as follows :—" In case of injury to a player the referee shall order his removal from the field of play, and no attendance shall be allowed on the field except for that purpose." The present rule gives the referee power to allow attendance on the field in case of injury, and not only to remove the injured player. Two alterations in the laa,a of the game are pro- posed. Warrington and Runcorn suggest an alteration of the definition of a goal to exclude a kick from a fair catch. The rule et present excludes the possibility of kicking a goal from a Punt, a kick-off, or a drop-out, and the proposed alteration is likely to raise consider- able difference of opinion. The Union Committee propose as an amendment of the knock-on rule :—" When a player knocks on or throws the ball forward any player of the opposite side may play the ball, but should be or any player of his own side play the ball it shall at once be brought back to where such knock-on or throw forward took place, and there put down and scrummaged. A player who accidently knocks the ball on shall not be penalized if he obtains possession before it falls to the ground."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>WORTH THINKING ABOUT.</head>
        <p>WORTH THINKING ABOUT.</p>
        <p>"Currant Bread," said the King's doctor about a year ago,' is a most wholesome food." Such an opinion, coming from so high an authority, set people thinking, and, as a result of British deliberation, there has arisen a steady demand for this bread. The currant loaf is exceedingly thrifty in daily use, for the fruit both enriches the bread and keeps it from getting dry, so that it needs very little butter.</p>
      </div>
