        <p>W. ELLIOTT.—Thanks for pretty card, re change of. address. I hope you will enjoy new sights, etc. Perhaps you might introduce ,some new members to the C.C.L. Louis 8.---I like your story immensely, but please write on one side of paper only, Louis, when you send me anything else. ALBERT B.—No, I have never heard of the verb "To Buff," at least, not in the English language. Perhaps a Japanese dictionary ' would tell you. So- you can go ten times across the "Swan" without coming to. the surface! Bravo!</p>
        <p>THE AUTOMOBILE.</p>
        <p>Three very great ants started out one bright day, • _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>In •i wenderful automobile; • And they said; with a snigger, "Now, isn't this grand? How mean all the horseflies will feel!" The lady ants sighed at the automobile, And thought, "Oh, such rich ants to wed !" They swept past the eatErpillqrs so fast, That the hair stood up straight on eaoh head. And the faster they went, the more reckless they got,</p>
        <p>Till they ran over insects galor2:; And the more that they struck, the more they</p>
        <p>were pleased;</p>
        <p>And they said, "Let us kill a few more • But they did not p,zreeive, as they rushed down</p>
        <p>the road</p>
        <p>A great big p3liecynan, named Bee, Who took out lais sting, and remarked with a frown, "Now, just let then 'l try to hit /Tie."</p>
        <p>The automobile crme along with a dash, Which Poliecnan Bee noticed with ire; So he made a quick jab with his terrible sting. And immediately punctured the tyre. The automobile tumbled down in a wreck, And the ants all flew out in a pile; Next day old Judge Spider said, "Load them</p>
        <p>with chains,. - And keep them in prison. a while." ANNIE BATEMAN.</p>
