        <p>Sold everywhere in boxes, price 1/ (56 pills) &amp; 2/9 (163 pills).</p>
        <p>Before he entered Holy Orders the Archbishop of York was an officer in the Indian Army. He joined when he was in his twenty-first year, and after studying the native languages secured the post of interpreter to his regiment, afterwards retiring 011" a pension.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. C. Hook, 8.A., once went to a picnic where Millais, Holman Hunt, and other eminent artists were present. At Godalining. they saw a countryman's yellow waistcoat in a shop; they purchased it.' and made one of the party wear it at lunch. Then they tossed for it, and Mr. Hook, who won it, has kept it ever since as a souvenir of the happy day they spent. The artist has even introduced the yellow waistcoat into some of his pictures.</p>
        <p>The Earl of Ancaster let, ftir the first time, the forest, of Glenartney, in Perths'h•ire, which, with Drummond Castle, he inherited horn his mother, the late Baroness Willoughby de Eresby. In this forest the late Prince Consort brought down his first stag in Scotland, and in the times of the old Scottish monarchy Glenartney was a Royal appanage. Once it was the scene of an act of barbarity that -led up to the out- lawry of the MacGregor clan. In 1588, a man named Drummond, James IV.'s forester, cut off the ears of some MacGregors whom he found poaching, and in revenge the kinsmen of the game stealers killed Drummond. Bing James then pronounced, by Royal proclamation, sen- tence of outlawry against the clan, which was never subsegFently abrogated.</p>
        <p>Mr. Marsham, of Bow-street, v:ho is one of the oldest stipendiaries on the active list—he is seventy-three—is the wealthiest magistrate now on the London Bench.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Morley used to play football in hi 4 youth, and was also fond of amateur aeting.</p>
        <p>ta.King abroad ,tlv2 Xiag of the Belgians almost invariably carries o.n um- brella, never a stick.. ,</p>
        <p>Dr. Hans Richter, the famous musical con• ductor, can play almost esvery kind of instru, ment to he found in an orchestra. -</p>
