      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>Collecting boots and shoes of famous wearers is a hobby of the German Emperor.</p>
        <p>King Edward used to compose verses whea a boy, and some of his poems were among Queen Victoria's greatest treasures.</p>
        <p>M. Auguste Rodin, perhaps the world's greatest sculptor, was so little thought of up to a few years ago that he lived almost in poverty.</p>
        <p>When at Oxford the Bishop of London took a great deal of interest in boating. Being of a light weight he frequently coached his College eight.</p>
        <p>Sir J. G. Ward, the Postmaster-General of New Zealand, was born in South Melbourne, and went to school there. While in his 'teens he entered the New Zealand postal service in a humble capacity..</p>
        <p>As a small boy Lord Avebury was a keen naturalist. When it Eton the knowledge he displayed of natural history was such that he absolutely astonished a master who set him to write an essay on the bee.</p>
        <p>Mr. Percy Alden. M.P., once asked a little girl who had been enjoying a holiday in connec- tion with the Children's Country Holidays Fund, what she liked best about the country. "Please sir, seeing no dead cats," was her reply.</p>
        <p>Sir Redvers Buller was urged by hi a rela- tives when he was a youth to devote himself to politice, but he announced that he would rather be a private in the regiment than be Prime Minister. Sir Redvers is fond of reading his- torical novels.</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Carlisle (Dr. Dimle), when a boy, was taught to work with his hands, anct learnt, among other accomplishments, to knit stockings. He announced recently with some pride to a school sewing class that he knew how to make a shirt.</p>
        <p>M. Maurice Rouvier, who has twice been French Premier, started his public career as a barrister, but he decided to turn his attention to trade instead, and entered a wholesale grocer's business as a bookkeeper. He then worked his way up, and became one of the lead- ing members of the firm.</p>
        <p>Lord Iveagh has one of the most curious hobbies in the world. This is the collection of old silver potato-rings, which are large silver sockets in the form of rings into which the wooden bowl used for potatoes used to be fitted. These rings have become very scarce, and are much sought after by brie-à-brae hunters.</p>
        <p>Sir Alfred de Bock Porter, K.C.8., the secre- tary of the Ecclesiastical Commission, has charge of 90,000 files and half a million leases. It was in 1860 that Sir Alfred Porter entered the office of the Ecclesiastical Commission, and he has been secretary, financial adviser, and steward of the manors since 1888; yet he is himself a member of he Congregational body, and has been associated with the Finehley Con- gregational Church for over thirty years.</p>
        <p>Mr. Walter Hudson, the Labour M.f(.- for Newcastle-on-Tyne, started life as a railway hand on the North-Eastern, thus following in the footsteps of his father. Progressing little by little, he was eventually appointed guard, .a position which he occupied for five and twenty years. There are now two ex-railway guards in Parliament, Mr. Richard Bell, who was for years in charge of a goods train, being the other.</p>
        <p>The Hon. John Collier inherits some of his talent as an artist from his father, Lord Monks- well. The second son of the distinuished lawyer, the Hon. John Collier was desired by his father to adopt a business career. But he had more faith in the possibilities of art, in which his father had given him his first lesson. Mr. Collier went to the Slade school, and after that he studied in Paris.</p>
        <p>A daring experiment was once performed by Lord Kelvin before a class of students. In the% course of his lecture he said that while a voltage of 3,000 or so would be fatal to a man, a voltage of some 300,000 would be harmless. He was going to give a practical Mustration on him- self, but the students cried out, " Try it on a dog!" Lord Kelvin cast a look of reproach at his class. " Didn't I figure' it out myself ?" he said, quietly, as he walked to the apparatus and safely turned the tremendous voltage into him- self..</p>
        <p>Signor Caruso, the famous tenor, is the son of a Neapolit:N engineer, who was turned adrift by his father as a youth because of his par- tiality for an operatic career, which his parent assured him Tollld infallibly bring him to grief. And, as a matter of fact, he did have a very hard time of it at first, to make both ends meet, and more than once contemplated turning his attention to some more lucrative calling. For- tunately, however, recognition came in time to prevent this.</p>
        <p>The Kaiser has just bought the house in which his grandfather, the first Emperor, passed the night on the eve cf the battle of Ste. Privat in 1870. The place is a ;modest little homestead in the village of ZezonVille, 'belonging to an old farmer named Baron. On learning that Baron was in distressed circumstances, the Em- peror purchased th:i residenci.. for F.,l,ooo—about three times its value—and graciously told the old man that he could remain in the house as long as he lives. Upon Baron's death the Kaiser will turn the place into a museum.</p>
        <p>Lord Muskerry is one of the great champions of the sailor-man in the House of Lords. He has served in the Navy himself, and belongs to the little group of sailor peers who include Lords Glasgo*, Orford, Elibank, and Clavt- william. The Plimsoll of the Peers," as he has been called, owing to his continued and practical interest in the welfare of our sailors, is maternally a cadet of the great house of Fitz- Maurice, * Earls of Kyrry. lie succeeded to the</p>
      </div>
