      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Title Reverend.</head>
        <p>The Title Reverend.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt would be interesting to know whether your correspondent" Ions" made any public protest against the use of the title " Reverend" before Mr. Aked Tentincisted it. If it is a state of things so astounding and preposterous as to stagger human belief how is it that "Iona" did not discover it until Mr. Aked did? I suppose the passages referred to in the Bible have been there a very long time. Perhaps" lone" could tell us if Mr. Aked has taken up the title again in America as I see that New York papers refer to him as the Rev. Mr. Aked. If he has gone to Ar. Rockefeller's church it is to be hoped he is not going to be a "rocky fellow" about titles. We can sympathise with " Iona" in his loneliness on this subject. Now that Ar.Aked has gone I presume there are only two crusaders left in England and if the other one should have a call it will be. very awkward. At present they can have a com- mittee meeting and a mass meeting all at once without' tuu_y ceremony or expense. Won't someone oblige " lone" with a "public renunciation" just to keep the thing alive `.4 If it was well seasoned with New Theology and Women's Suffrage a good collection would doubtless result. There is one passage in " lona's " letter which seems to indicate some research or investi- gation on his part. He says: "Heaven is farther away than London." Would he mind telling us in what direction it lies? Astronomers and scientists are mixing things up .so much that we ought to have an authoritive statement. One very peculiar thing about your correspondent" lone "is that he never makes any attempt to pr ove anything or to support any of his statements by evidence. He must be aware of course that tradition, hearsay, unsupported testimony, and unauthenticated writings prove nothing either about this world or the next. Will he tell us how he knows that Heaven is farther away than London? May I suggest that " lona's " letters would be more readable if he used correct words and terms. The word "reverend" is not a name and is not one of the names of God as he says.—Yours Scc., LACON.</p>
        <p>Patriotism.—is it a Waning Force P Sir,—While politicians are interesting themselves in the consideration of the height of the walls which render industrial nterchange difficult, and tend to divide nations, there are tendencies often unrecognised steadily making for unity. It is interesting to consider the effect of these tendencies as they impinge on the question of patriotism. Does that fact that the interests which are primary national are now becoming inter- national, involve a weakening of national feeling? These tendencies are bound to affect and change the character of our various interests. The worlds of science, art, religion, social ethics, and labour, recognise no national lines of demarcation, and in all their phases are becoming more international, and forming new com- binations. Will the world-wide tendencies which are at work drawing together those who are like-minded in these phases of life which are wide as the race not tend to obliterate the lines of national demarcation, and lessen the influence of patriotism? The tendency is clearly marked in recent democrative developments. Of political associations the Labour Party is the one which stands most for peace amongst nations. This does not at all arise because those who compose it are many way deficient in the fighting instinct. They have their full share of the original savage. Nor does it arise from the fact that they are less patriotic than other classes. It springs from a recognition of other interests which appeal to them quite as powerfully as that form of patriotism the final assertion of which may require the use of the sword. The unionist miners of England are far more in sympathy with their fellow workers in the same i industry n Belgium, France, and America, than they are with the wealthy capitalists who control the English mines. rho Socialists of England are more in sympathy with the Socialists of Germany than they are with those in their own land who neither toil nor spin. In the South African War we had an illustration of the work- ing of these new forces in national life. Rightly or wrongly a considerable proportion of the community looked upon the late war as a capitalist one and believed that it arose out of the limitations which the Boer Government put upon industrial development, by capitalists not of their race, of the mineral wealth of the Transvaal, and the object of the war wits, in essence, to secure to capital greater freedom and power. The dynamite monopoly is believed by many to have had far more to do with the war than the danger to the women and children in Johannesburg, or the treatment of the coloured race by the Boers. Others, notably the Irish, saw in this conflict a nation struggling to retain its national existence. Amid the clash of arms the dominant chord that reached their ears was that of freedom. Whether these ideas be right or no does not affect the present question, but it illustrates that there are forces which are gathering volume, which in the future (if they have occasion to develop themselves) introduction of tend,to disintegrate patriotism by the of affinities which are not circumscribed by national divisions. The loss from one point of view will be great, but from another the gain will be greater. Con- flicts between nation and nation will become rare because the new interests, being independent of nationality, will restrain the causes which produce national conflict. But what will be the effect of this on patriotism? It may be contended that the extension of sympathies over a wider area does not necessarily lessen the interest in the more restricted one. The fact that we are Englishmen and Imperialists does not lessen but rather increases our interest in our native town. If I may be pardoned a personal illustration on account of the aptness, I would mention the case of Alderman Bennett who of all our townsmen holds the welfare of Warrington most dear and at the same time no one stands more for national and imperial interes,ts. May we not reason from this that a recognition of international interests of the essential unity of the race, does not necessarily imply that those who recognise the larger interests and obligations will be less loyal to more restricted interests, less patriotic where their own country is concerned. But this presumes that the interests are of the same kind, at any rate net antagonistic, and only differ in the area of their application. The effect will be very different when the interests conflict as for example, in the case of the South African War. The day has gone by when we can accept the saying" Nly country right or wrong "as a final guide to our actions or as an expres- sion of our sympathies. Moral considerations are more regal than ever, and command the loyalty of the nation in a manner unknown before. Never again will this country plunge into such a war as the late one in South Africa before it understands clearly what it is about, There are few signs that the fighting instinct in our race is likely to disappear, but there are signs in abundance ' that slowly but surely this will assume other forms and make other combinations, and these combinations will not necessarily follow national lines. They will be based upon and inspired by ideals other than love of country, and loyalty to King, they will fight with weapons other than ironclads and rifles.] The union of interests of those who are of like mind in</p>
        <p>the realms of philosophy, science, art, and industry, scorns national barriers. In the higher reaches of human thought there are no tariff walls to retard exchange, neither distance, language, nor race can pre- vent those with mutual interests drawing together and forming associations. What effect this will have upon national unity and patriotism we cannot clearly say, but of this we may IV assured that if the isolation which gives strength to patriotism be broken down it will be replaced by forces and interests which are more perma- nent and enduring. The old order will change, but in giving place to the now may it not take a nobler and more complete form? Should patriotism wane, and in its restricted form die, may we not reverently adopt the thought concerning the greatest of all resurrections,and should we in the early morning of this century sorrow- fully desire to permanently embalm it in its old form will not the spirit of the century say to us" Why seek ye the living amongst the dead? It is not here, it is risen, and risen in a nobler form: the love of race has broadened and deepened into the love of humanity, the love of country has extended till it embraces all lands and peoples." Penketh, May 22nd, 1907.</p>
        <p>CHARLES E. PARKER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>WEEK ENDING ISTII MAY, 1907. . Total. Male. Female. Births Legitimate 52 24 28 , Illegitimate 2 0 2 Deaths</p>
        <p>Excess of births over deaths, 23. The ages of mortality were :—Under 1,2; 1-5, 7; 5-15, 1 ; 15-25, 2 ; 25-65, 10 ; 65 and upwards, 4. Total, 26.</p>
        <p>The deaths included :-2 from measles, 4 from phthisis, 2 from cancer, G from lung diseases, 1 from heart disease, 3 from accidents, 8 from all other -causes.</p>
        <p>The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week :—Scarlet fever, 3 cases; Tuberculous Phthisis, G oases. Number of patients in hospital :—Scarlet. fever, Borough 18, Newton 1 ; total, 19. Enteric fever Borough 2, total 2. Diphtheria, Borough 2, total 2. totals, Borough, 22; Newton, 1; Total, 23. Weather Return --Mean temperature 50 Fah., and there- fore 7 deg. lower than the preceding week. Maximum temperature 78 on the 12th inst. Minimum tempera- ture 3G on the 18th inst. Rainfall *55 inch. General direction of wind W.S.W.</p>
        <p>P. B. GAPPIKIN, Acting Medical ()facer of Health.</p>
        <p>Asked to explain what had become of thirteen £5 notes stolen from a house at Hindhead, Luke White (54), a labourer, at Surrey Sessions, Kingston, on Tuesday,said they got wet and stuck together, so he burnt them. He was sentenced to eighteen months' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 25, 1907. LYMM MAY QUEEN.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 25, 1907. LYMM MAY QUEEN.</p>
        <p>were made, and ten of these, rejected by the Church to which they applied for membership, some little time later formed a class meeting of their own and founded what is now the Primitive Methodist Church. They had zeal, had these early converts, and preached with simplicity and vigour which drew others into their ranks, and from this small beginning the movement spread rapidly. In a northerly direction it embraced the towns and villages in the valley of the Mersey, whilst to the South it extended to Hull and from then 1 , again went north as far as Scotland. Nor has the ; passing of a century witnessed a cessation of its growth ! and last year some 4,32 i members were admitted to the i Church, which is now the fourth in numbers of the 1 Evangelical Free Churches and , second amongst the Methodist Churches. Truly the followers of these early enthusiasts can say " The Lord our God has done great things for us ; whereorwe are glad." The centenary will witness a special effort for the extension of the Church,for which purpose some £250,000 has been granted, whilst the celebrations at Mow Cop , will be on a grand scale. Each afternoon a great camp meeting will be held and addresses will be given from four preaching stands. This evening a mass meeting in the Victoria Hall, Hanley, will be addressed by ten Members of Parliament who are members of the Church :—Messrs. E. Edwards, John Wilson, C. Fee. wick, J. Johnson, El. R. IVlousfield, J. Richardson. J. W. Taylor, L. L. Moise, W. E. Harvey and J. D. Shackle- ton. Sunday's proceedings will commence at seven o'clock with a prayer meeting, whilst camp meetings will be held in the morning and afternoon. Monday's , programme is practically the same except that a mass meeting will be held at Tunstall, Slow Cop and Bradley Green in the evening. In connection with the celebration no little honour has been conferred upon Mr. J. Whitley of Stockton Heath who has been appoiated to conduct one of the preaching stands on Monday afternoon. The occasion has a great significance for Mr. Whitley, as almost to the day he celebtates his fiftieth year as a lay preacher. Throughout the whole of that long period Mr. Whitley has been heart and soul in the movement and as a young man thought nothing of walking fifteen miles in one direction to address a meeting,returning by foot at the close of the evening service. On many occasions has he addressed congregations in thq building at Latchford, where the movement first found a home in \Warrington, and he also assisted in the building of the Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church. One of Mr. Whitley's most treasured possessions was a letter 1 written by Hugh Bourne in May 1845 to one of his 1 followers in Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>WARRINGTON HORSE PARADE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON HORSE PARADE.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>Given fine weather, Warrington's annual horse parade this afternoon promises to surpass any of previous years. Almost one hundred animals have been entered for competition and in addition the procession will include a large number of turnouts other than those entered for the prizes. As in past years the brewers' classes are again the strongest though they are run close by the "heavy miscellaneoas" section. Only three animals have been entered for the silver cup presented by Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., for the best light horse and it is a matter for considerable regret that the com- petition in this class is not much keener. The same thing applies to the entries for the silver cup presented by Sir Gilbert Greenall for the best light lurry or van horse and the committee are hopeful that in future parades greater interest will be manifested in these two sections.</p>
        <p>The President and Reception Committee will meet the judges at'the Lion Hotel at 11-15 a.m., and will then proceed to the ground in Arpley fields where the judging will commence at noon. Non-competing turnouts are required to be in Slutchers-lane at 1.30 as it. is desired that the parade shall start punctually at two o'clock. The route chosen is :—Parker-street, Sankey-street, Battermarket-street, Church-street, round the Lamp, Church-street, Mersey-street, Bridge street, Horsemarket-street, Win wick-street, Tanners- lane, Froghall-le.ne, Priestley-street, Powys-street, Bank Quay, Sankey-street, Bold-street, Wilson-Patten-street- The order of procession will be:— Corporation Fire Brigade Walker's Band Walker's teams Greenall, Whitley and Co.'s Fire Brigade Greenall, Whitley and Co.'s teams Builders' Merchants Builders, Contractors and Carters Corporation Classes _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Crosfield's Fire Engine • Crosfield's Fire Escape and Fire Brigade Coal Merchants and Dealers Butchers, Bakers and Grocers Mineral Water Manufacturers Heavy Miscellaneous Borough Band Light Miscellaneous Pair Horse Miscellaneous Fish Dealers and Milk Dealers Covered Furniture Vans Non-competitors</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>LATEST NEWS.</head>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>EMPIRE EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>A Representative Gathering.</p>
        <p>The Earl of Crewe opened yesterday morning at Caxton Hall, Westminster' the Federal Conference on Education, which is being held each day till the close of the month. The hall, which was decorated with flags., was crowded with official delegates from all parts of the Colonies, many of whom wore their academic robes. Among the British possessions represented were Canada, Australia, South Africa, India, the American Protecto- rate, the Gold Coast, the West Indies, and the Falkland Islands.</p>
        <p>The Earl of Crewe said he owed the position which he occupied, to his connection with the Privy Council, whieh was the only department in the State concerned with the Empire as a whole. The meeting was a great departure, and he hoped it would be repeated many times. He thought too much was made of London as a centre of the Empire, and that conferencas like this ought to be held in various parts of the Empire. He was glad to think that they had none of the special difficulties to face in other parts ~of the Empire that faced educationalists in this country. Lord and Lady Tennyson (his lordship being president of the Leagne of the Empire) held a reception prior to the opening of the Conference.</p>
        <p>TelegraniS wishing success to the conference were read from the Earl of Se'borne and ethers. The Governor of the Falkland Islands the Prime Minister of Victoria (Mr. Bent), a representative of Quebec, the Vice-Chancellors of Oxford and Glasgow, and others having spoken, the conference adjourned.</p>
        <p>MR. CHAMBERLAIN'S HEALTH. The London" Evening News "yesterday 'published a message from its special corresp9ndent at St. Raphael, in which he says : Mr. Chamberlain will be staying abroad for some time yet. It is not likely, however, that the right hon. gentleman will remain at St. Raphael, but that he will leave there in the course of a few days. There is little likelihood of his being seen in England for many months and it is a question whether he will re-enter public life for a year yet. His health gives little cause for alarm, and Mrs. Chamberlain gave the correspondent the fol- lowing reply to an inquiry as to her hnsband's condition: "He is making extremely satisfactory progress." The correspondent adds that Mr. Chamberlain gets all the London papers and indulges in a little light reading. He has discarded his monocle, and it is stated that he has taken a dislike to the orchid and 4ever wears the flower as formerly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>CO-OPERATIVE THRIFT FUND.</head>
        <p>CO-OPERATIVE THRIFT FUND.</p>
        <p>The Committee of the Co-operative Wholesale Society recommended a thrift fund for the employees od the following basis : Each member to pay to the fund 3? per cent. (or 9d. in the pound) on his wages, but employees whose wages ' are 40s. per week or under to have the option of pay- ing on)y 24 per cent. (or Gd. in the pound) ; the Society to contribute to the fund a sum equal to I% per cent. (or Gd. in the pound on the wages of those members who are in receipt of 30s. per week and under, and 1-1 per cent.. (or 3d. in the pound) on the wages of those members who are in receipt of over 30s. per week ; no portion of the funds to be pay- able to any employee or to his representatives before he reaches the age of 60 years unless he is incapacitated through infirmity of body or mind, or unless he die in the service of the Society before reaching that age. In exceptional cases of need the Committee may at their discretion allow a member to withdraw an amount not exceeding one-fourth of the sum standing to his credit. Membership to be optional, but an employee having joined the fund is not to be allowed to withdraw from is obligation to contribute thereto during the continu- ance ot his service with the Society ; any employee, whether on fixed wages or piecework, to be eligible to join the fund after six months' continuous service. The Committee report that the annual cost to the Society. in case all the employees of the Society (both in the distributive and productive departments) joined 1 the fund, would be about £9,800, or 10d. for each £lOO 1 of sales.</p>
        <p>To-Day's Celebrations. To-day Primitive Methodists from all quarters will flock in their thousands to Mow Cop to take part in the celebration of the hundreth birthday of their denomina- tion. Annually what might be termed a pilgrimage has been made to this historic village but this year the great camp meetings will surpass any of those held in the years that are gone, and the bleak and barren Mow Cop. will witness scenes of enthusiasm without parallel , in its history, for if the week-end be fine it is anticipated that at least (30,000 people will join in the proceedings. On the 31st of May 1807 Hugh Bourne For excessive speeds on the London and Brighton- Williana Clowes and others, fired with divine road, fines of £67, with costs amounting to £l4, were enthusiasm, proceeded to the now historic village ordered to be paid by London and other motorists at and from early morning until late at n!ght yward's Heath on Wednesday. It was stated that preached and prayed before the crowd which the speeds ranged from twenty-eight. to thirty-four and impelled by curiosity had assembled. Many converts a half miles an hour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>PICTURESQUE PAGEANT.</head>
        <p>PICTURESQUE PAGEANT.</p>
        <p>Lymm on Thursday, with due pomp and ceremony, crowned its May Queen. In the past this charming old ceremony was conducted annually with the greatest regularity, but a succession of wet days led to depleted coffers and a cooling of enthusiasm and last year it was allowed to die out. Originally it was an event in which the whole of the residents in the district joined, but unfortunately differences which ought never to have existed, arose and led to the secession of the Anglican party and for some years the Nonconformist section kept the pageant alive, but, as above stated, last year they also retired from the field. The discon- tinuing of the spectacle was a matter for great regret, and consequently there was no little rejoicing when this year witnessed a revival, due mainly to the public spirit of the Tradesmen's Association. But still the fates were not kind.. No little expense is entailed in , preparing for the event and to meet this the presence and support of spectators from the surrounding dis- tricts is necessary. To put it mildly the weather on Thursday was unusually depressing, and it was with heavy .hearts that the promoters made for the centre of the village shortly after noon. Rain had fallen all the morning and there was no prospect of an improvement until towards one o'clock, and even then there was no great cause for enthusiasm. True the rain had ceased, but • heavy clouds overhead kept one in a constant state of apprehension lest the weather had called only a brief truce, whilst a cold east wind seemed to temper the spirits of even the most irrepressible enthusiasts. The picturesque old world village, with its narrow windidg and hilly streets and leafy lanes, could hardly be said to wear a holiday attire. True a brave attempt had been made to defy the elements but everywhere was damp and the flags which were displayed hung limp and appeared to share in the general depression. The proceedings were timed to commence at 1.30 and at that hour a fairly large and warmly clad crowd had ventured forth to witness the assembling of the characters taking part in the procession,at the picture- sque old cross. Then the band struck up a lively air and under the influence of the music, in a short ticao all the vague fears of failure were forgotten ; the rain was a thing of the past; depression gave way to more buoyant feelings and all that was remembered was the pageant which the people had either gathered to witness or to join. Quickly the characters were marshalled in their proper order and to further spirited strains the procession set out on its way. Decorated cycles and tradesmen's turn- outs headed the way and following them came a large variety of personages. Behind John Bull came the military heroes who had helped to give him his power, whilst the Duke of Wellington and Napoleon forgot old differences and marched together with an escort of New South Wales Lancers The seasons, delightfully mixed up, followed on the heels of a number of flowe: girls, and preceded the heroes and heroines of tales beloved in the nursery. The Mikado, I#ing delight- fully English, conversed with Lord Nelson whilst two ancient English kings associated themselves with the modern representatives of the nations over which they ruled. Indians in all their war paint were apparently softened by the presence of milkmaids and gipsies and suffered their faces to be wreathed in smiles such as the real article would disdain with scorn. Geisha girls, fairy queens, and Italians and Swiss all added to the general excellent effect, and the spectators, whose ranks were ever increasing, expressed their apprecia- tion in no half hearted manner. The appearance of the Queen (Miss Ethel Mullineaux) was the signal for increased enthusiasm. Preceded by her maids of honour and crown, sword and sceptre bearers, her Majesty comported herself with grent dignity and with stately bows acknowledged the homage of her subjects. The tour of the village completed, an adjournment was made to a field where the Queen took her seat on the throne and was crowned and invested with the insignia of office by Miss Warburton. Then the Lymm Band struck up the familiar strains of the Maypole dance, and this was the signal for the revels to begin. Girls attired in blue and boys in white sailor blouses and blue trousers tripped lightly on the green and successfully plaited the maypole. Others showed their skill in an Irish jig and a Pierrot dance, and an excel- lent character dance was given by the Misses Jackson (Knutsford). Wilson and Wilson, acrobats, indulged in buffoonery and rough humour which appealed to a crowd numbering several thous/olds: whilst Miss Ada Gatley performed graceful evolutions on the tight- rope and juggled with skill. One mistake was made in the programme by the inclusion of the judging for the various prizes. This took some considerable time and tried not a little the patience of the beholders, until there was distinct feel- ing of relief when a march, played by the Northwich Adelaide Prize Band announced that the judging was at an end. The prizes awarded were :—Pair horse lurries (3 competitors): 1, Messrs. J.T. Thornley and Son; 2, J. Marsh, Speke ;3, J. Moore, Lymm. Tradesmen's turn- outs (19 competitors) : 1, C. H. Wood, Altrincham ; 2, J. B. Egerton, Lymm; 3, Burgons, Limited; r, J. B. Taylor; hc, S. N. Green, Warrington. Cyclists, original character: 1, Miss W. Goodwin, Stockport, "Finding of Moses " ; 2, Mr. J. Marsden, Oldham, "Charles II."; 3, Miss F. Rogerson, Lymm, "Wild rose." Humorous character: 1, Mr. T. Shaw, Lymm "Lively Polly "; 2, Mr. A. Eaton, Lymm, " Smash up"; 3, H. Ditohfield, "Suffragette." From noon for the rest of the day, fortunately, the rain kept off and the arranged programme was gone through. In the evening the bands played for dancing in which both young and old joined and only the approach of night put an end to the festivities. The rroceedings were a great success and for this no little praise is due to the officers who, without exception, worked hard and well. They were : Chairman, Mr. Edwin Brown; Vice-Chairman, Mr. Harry Saville ; Hon. Treasurer, Mr. F. A. Morris; Hon. Secretaries, Messrs. F. W. Ashton and W. West; Mr. F. P. Cooke. Ladies' Committee: President, Miss M. Warberton ; Secretary, Miss E. Hatton; Mesdames Hurst, George Brown, F. W. Ashton, Blease, and Grimstead; Misses J. Daniels, L. Winstanley, A. Winstaniey, M. Winstsnley, F. Egerton, F. Webb, E. Wilson, J. Maclntyre, N. Brookman, D. Warburton, M. Beeson, F. Rogerson, N. Jenkins, N. Brundrett, Massey, and M. Daniels. Gentlemen's Committee: Messrs. W. G. Groves, T. Warburton, J. D. B. Smith, R. H. Gotobed, F. W. Drone, W. E. Hillier, J.% Warham, J. Pearcy, G. Willan, J. Nash, D. Daniels, G. H. Howard, C. Richards, W. G. Porter, W. Daniels, S. W. Gould, F. Wilson, H. R. Harness, J. Rogerson, P. Ardern, A. H. Fray, E. Moston, W. Keisall, W. Seymour, P. Peters, N. Worthington, W. Shakeshaft, S. Davies and E. Marsh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>DEPRECIATION OF GOLD.</head>
        <p>THE DEPRECIATION OF GOLD.</p>
        <p>:Br C. E. PARKER].</p>
        <p>The depreciation of the value of a sovereign, which has been going on since 1896, has not received the attention which it deserves. The most reliable figures which illustrate this are those prepared by Mr. Saar- beck. He takes the wholesale prices of 45 leading commodities, strikes their average, which forms an index number, and starting with this marks the rise and fall from month to month. His index number for the average of prices 1867-77 was 100, by the year 1896 it had fallen to 61. In other words, the amount I these commodities which in the period of 1867-77 ' would have purchased a hundred sovereigns, would in ' the year 1896 have only purchased 61 sovereigns. The index number has risen from 61 for 1890 to 80 for January, 1907, a rise of more than 30 per cent. !Applying this to our international trade in 1896 the 'growth, when measured by gold, appears less than it really was, so now by the same standard the growta is not so great as it appears. During the year 1906, by Mr. Saurbeck's figure, materials ros3 10 per cent, but food generally declined 21 per cent. Probably a rise of 10 per cent in materials would represent a rise of about 5 per cent on finished products. Countries owing us money which pay in goods benefit by the gold depreciation, South Africa and other gold-producing. countries lose. Whether the 43 leading articles give an accurate indication for all we do not know, but there is little doubt they are. fairly representative. The effect of this depreciation of gold is very important when we consider the relation it bears to fixed amounts. Everyone who has a fixed income is worse off now than he was ten years ago. Wage earners, and people I with moderate incomes must feel the pinch very hard. It is true that down to 1896 the purchasing power of the sovereign steadily increased, and benefitted all with fixed incomes, but this is cold comfort to those who have experienced the diminishing purchasing power during late years. This article is reprinted from the year book of the Manchester, Liverpool and District Tanners' Federation which has been excellently compiled by Mr. C. E. Parker, hon. secretary of the Association. There are numerous other articles of interest to the trade, Mr. Parker himsslf having contributed several. The Association hes a large membership in this district.</p>
        <p>The War Office has been informed that in case of war the St. John Ambulance Brigade will be prepared to take charge of the military hospitals of the United Kingdom, and so release the R.A,NI.C. for service abroad.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>PRIMITIVE METHODIST CENTENARY.</head>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODIST CENTENARY.</p>
        <p>A Brief History.</p>
        <p>(Continued front, last week.)</p>
        <p>When 1807 was reached, the camp meeting promised in 1801 had had yet to be held. Why had it not been held before? The Harriseahead people were eager for it, and the Methodist Magazines were often telling of the success of such meetings in America. The truth must be written that these revivalistic methods received their check from the Methodism of the time. The authorities of the Methodist Church to which both Bourne and Clowes belonged, did not favour this open air and uncon- ventional evangelism. The Harriseahead friends became very anxious over the decline of revival enthusiasm, and it became a serious inquiry with them as to whether a camp meeting would not again raise the cause. Their resolve to hold such a meeting was precipitated by a visit to their neighbourhood of the famous Lorenzo Dow, who had much to tell about the great open-air gatherings in America. He observed that occasionally something of a Pentecostal power attended them ; and that for a considerable time in America, as much good had been done, and as many souls brought to God, at the camp meetings as at all the meetings put together." It is easy to see what an impetus these reports would be to all who clung tos the early Methodist principles. Now it happened that one of the old-time wakes was about to be held at Norton in August. Hugh Bourne therefore proposed to his Harriseahead friends that a camp meeting be held at Norton to counteract the evil influences of the wake. He received much encourage- ment. They were ready. But Why wait till A.ugust?" they said. August was a long way off," Why not have that day's praying on Mow which had been the dream for six years past ? " And so it came to pass that on the 31st of May 1807 the great historic gathering was held. At six o'clock in the morning William Clowes found a small group singleg under the lee of a wall. The weather was unpromising, but in a short time, the clouds dispersed. As the day wore on, the multitude increaskl to such an extent that even the cautious Hugh Bourne describes it as "immense," "prodigious" and says that it precsented a " sublime and magnificent spectacle." The historian writes that many came from Macclesfield, Congleton, Warrington, Knutsford and other places. Mr. Arthur Mounfield, (in a most interesting article on Lorenzo Dow in "The Dawn" for May. says "among the preachers" on this famous Sunday was Peter Phillips, who, with his wife, had travelled all night in a farmer's cart to be there at sunrise." Four stands were necessary that the vast multitude might be reached, and the organization included " permanent praying companies which did not break up for preach- ing." We are not certain that Hugh Bourne was amongst the preachers. We see him moving over the cround more as a general superintendent. William Clowes, however, was in his element as, draa, ing upon his personal experience, he exhorted the people. "The glory that filled my soul on that day far exceeds my powers of description to explain " he said. He did net quit the field till eight o'clock at night The experiment of holding the camp meeting was thus abundantly justified. Untold good was dc ne. But opposition followed. This first camp meeting had been in a sense tolerated by the official Methodism of the time, but when it was proposed to hold a series of such meetings, the opposition to camp meetings became more pronounced and organised in character. Moreover, the Qonventicle and Five Mile Acts were not yet repealed. Before the time for holding the Norton camp meeting rolled round. the Wesleyan Conference met at Liverpool, and at that Conference all connection with such meeting was disclaimed. The result was that William Clowes was absent from Norton camp meeting and was not seen at another for thirteen months. For a time James Bourne and others wavered and Hugh Bourne sounded the lowest deep of loneliness. Still he remained firm, believing the 'Conference had erred. Norton camp meeting was held, and was a triumphant success. Hugh Bourne was formally exclu'ded from the Wesleyan Society in 1808, and from this date he and his followers were known as the "Camp Meeting Methodists." For fifteen months after the Conference pronounce- ment, William Clowes continued to labour in the Burslena society. Still, although taking but little part in open air work, he kept up his friendship with various members of the camp meeting brotherhood, and notably with Hugh Bourne. Sometimes Bourne would lead Clive's class and together they made several excursions into Lancashire and Cheshire. In 1810 a crucial camp meeting was held at Ramsor. Clowes longed to be there, but Bourne thought it would be better for him not to attend. When, however, an appeal was made to Crawfoot the " old man of the forest," he was ready with an answer that turned the scale. " Tis better to obey God than man " he said and Clowes risked the displeasure his attendance might cause. It was that camp meeting which gave William Clowes to the new movement, for his expulsion from the older Methodism was the direct and retributive result of his share in the day's proceedings. His expulsion meant the with- drawal of many more,. for he had a most winsome and powerful personality. His nature was intensely magnetic. He had been the leader of two classes, one at Kidsgrove, and the other at Tunstall, and now the members of those classes were saying" Whither thou goest we will go." A. fold for this considerable little flock was found in the kitchen of a Mr. Smith at Tunstall, and there was constant preaching in this kitchen on Friday evenings. Very soon this group of believers were known as the " Clowesites."</p>
        <p>In cnmpany with Bonnie and Crawfoot, Clowes paid a visit to Derbyshire where they met with phenomenal success. At this period, too, we find William Clowes in Warrington on a visit to a Mrs. Richardson, a West Indian proprietress, who by liberating her slaves proved the reality of her new religious life. Returning home he found a seriols proposal awaiting him. This was to the effect that he should give himself wholly to the work of a home missionary, for up till now he was still busy as a potter, though on account of depression of trade, he was working litttle more than half-time. Even so be could earn 225. per week, but now, after much pra.yer, and with his wife's consent he agreed to accept 10s. per week that he might be at liberty to open up fresh piaci% and look after those already visited. Thus he becomes a paid home missionary, with his kitchen church as the base of his operations, and his little society separate and distinct from every other organis- ation. Soon the kitchen was inadequate, a larger room placed at his disposal was soon outgrown, and then steps were taken that a chapel might he secured. The Bournes bought the land, the writings were signed and James Crawfont preached the opening sermon. This first church was built in house form, suitable for conversion into cottage property }veins° "it could not be known whether the Connexion would be of long continuance."</p>
        <p>And now for a significant line from Hugh Bourne's journals :" Thursday May 30th 1811 I ordered tickets to be printed for the first time." These tickets were the sign and seal of the union of the camp meeting Methodists and the Clowesites, and it was in the com- ing tofzether of these two groups that Primitive Methodism had its birth. Many thought that this first ticket of membership would be the last, but it has been regularly renewed without a break unto this day. Then in February 1812 Bourne's journal says " We called a meeting and made plans for the next quarter and made some other regulations. In particular we took the name of the Society of the Primitive Methodists." The name was in a sense already earned for them. It stood for their staunch adherence to the methods of the earlier days. Thus the official name was not taken till 1812. Inasmuch however, as Mow Cop camp meeting in 1807, gave such an impulse to the the founders and inasmuch as the first society class was in existence in 1810, 1907-to 1910 have been chosen as the centenary celebration years. It is expected that the Centenary camp meeting to be held at :now Cop to-day (Saturday) to-morrow and Monday w ill be among the greatest of open-air gatherings ever known.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>We would remind secretaries of clubs that reports of Saturday's matches should reach the " Examiner " Office" not later than Wednesday following the date on which they are played. .</p>
        <p>With an eleven weakened by the absence of Bibby aad Thompson Warrington on Saturday journeyed to St. Helens and suffered defeat in their second league fixture. Nor•was there anything half-hearted about the St. Helens victory and the team are evidently making a bold bid for championship honours. The homsters started badly and lost four wickets for 36. Seven more runs were added for two more wickets and Warrington's chances were particularly rosy. Then to their discom- fiture they discovered that St. ' Helens had •tail and Crompton and Rigby proceeded to make merry. Profit- ing by some lax fielding they between them added 51 and being well supported by Tickle the score was carried to 115.</p>
        <p>With consummate caution Creswell and Richardson opened the Warrington innings and for some time were pottering about amongst the singles but they were scoring,and when 25 was recorded Warrington began to feel a little happier. Then dire disaster befel them. The two opening batsmen and Silcock went one after another. Turton and Wright endeavoured to stem the tide but a change in the bowling resulted in the dis- missal of both and as none of the others could get going the innings closed for 71. Marsden was largely responsible for Warrington's failure and took 4 wickets for 13 runs, whilst the batsmen were never happy when facing his deliveries.</p>
        <p>On the Arpley ground the Warrington second eleven treated the spectators to an excellent exhibition of hard hitting. St. Helens had first knock, and largely °ming to Plinston, who took seven wickets for 48, their total score was only 79. This was passed by the War- rington men with several wickets in hand, Hawtin and Bibby knocking up respectively 53 and 49. Five other players between them scored 40,50 that with only seven wickets down Warrington led by 63.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and District League Orford Barracks still hold the first place, and appear likely to do so for some time. On Saturday the soldiers trounced the Castle Rubber representative nby 113 to 77. From the scores it would appear that the Orford eleven is an all-round sound one, as seven of the men reached double figures, Johnson and Price being top scorers with 17 each.</p>
        <p>Holt and Knight are the mainstays of the St. James' team, and on Saturday, in addition, to capturing all their opponents' wickets, they scored between them 43, or one more than the Walker's Brewery , total. Holt was top scorer in the match with 23, though Minards with 22 and Knight and Grounds with 15 and 16 shared the honours of the game.</p>
        <p>Wilderspool Brewery on Saturday met Moore and Daresbury and succeeded in dismissing the homeeters for 48. This total could not be regarded as by any means a big one and the brewery men looked with con- fidence for thevictory. They had however reckoned without their host in the person of S. Taylor who was practically unplayable, and they could only total 18. S. Taylor name out with the splendid average of seven wickets for seven runs, the other wickets going to his namesake.</p>
        <p>The Guardian Office eleven this week-end have done well. On Saturday they inct Cheshire Lines Engineers and running up 50, out of which Woodhead, scored 27, they dismissed the C.L.C. men for 25. On Tuesday they completed their Works Competition fix- ture and after an exciting game won by 9 runs scoring 49 to 40 runs up by the Co-operative eleven.</p>
        <p>On Monday Monks Hall had the temerity to tackle Hough Green and right soundly were they beaten. The homesters scored rapidly and when 129 for seven wickets applied the closure. Monks Hall made but a poor show with the bat and only totalled 43, out of • which Dendy accounted for 19. Two days previously • the eleven had met and defeated St. Paula by 20. The outstanding feature of the St. Thomas and Holy Trinity match was the batting of Worthington and the bowling of Harding. Out of a total of 38, the first named notched 26 by vigorous methods whilst Harding took seven wickets for nine runs. During the match there was quite a heavy crop of ducks twelve players failing to score.</p>
        <p>Bowling. , Representatives 'of Crosfleld's and Monks Hall indulged in friendly rivalry on Saturday, when an • Interesting bowling match was decided on the re6rea- • tion ground connected with the first-named works. Each side placed thirty-three players in the field, and an interesting afternoon's play resulted in a verdict for the soapmakers by 79 points. The fortunes of the game fluctuated not a little, and early on Crosfleld's almost lost the lead which.their first three men secured for them. Towards the close, however, they carried every- th;ng before them. It is to be hoped that the match is , a forerunner of other fixtures of a similar and equally enjoyable character.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire and Cheshire joint organization has selected a strong and representative team to meet the Staffordshire County representatives on June 8. Only one local man, G. Holbrook, has been selected, and the Cemetery Club claims the honour of including him amongst its list of members. It is hoped and expected that he will faithfully uphold the honour of the good old town.</p>
        <p>Saturday saw further progress made in connection with the Warrington Works Competition, the first round of which is now on a fair way towards completion. Three matches were decided, and some excellent play was witnessed. Opening for P. &amp; K. against L. and N.-W., Mee gave a delightful exhibition of trundling, and in nine ends had scored 12 points. His opponent than got a wood home, but failed to further augment his score, and retirel beaten by 15-1. P. &amp;K. evidently believed in making a good start, and after three games led by 45 to 24. Then L. &amp; N.-W. had a turn, and when the match was half over were within six points of their opponents. The want of success in the opening stages disheartened them somewhat, and lacking the confidence necessary to win games they were ultimately defeated by 16 points.</p>
        <p>Neither of the other two matches produced anything like a good finish, as in each case the successful clubs led throughout, and never looked like being beaten. As a result of the game Walkers and Rylands will com- pete in the second round. The best games of the after- noon were witnessed when Rylands and Robinson's tried conclusions. After seven ends Hutchinson led by 9-0 against Stringer,and then the Rylands man found his length. Securing the block he turned the tables, and when 13 ends had been played had drawn level. Three points went to his opponent when he again got going, and ran out 15-12 after a very plucky fight. Humphreys land Hands were very evenly matched, and the points were secured alternately until the score stood 13-all I when Hands went to the front and secured the verdict.</p>
        <p>To-day's mstches are Rylands (No. 2) v. Monks Hall (No. 2) ; Fletcher's (No. 1) v. Crosfleld's (No. 1) and Castle Rubber v. Thew'is and Griffiths.</p>
        <p>Walker's Brewery on Saturday engaged in a fixture in the ganchester and District Brewery League and suc- ceeded in defeating the representatives of Cornbrook Brewery. Cornbrook totalled 40,Dargan taking 4 wickets for 10 rims and Norman 4 for 27, and then their bowlers proceeded to tumble the Warrington men oat. Seven wickets had fallen and they were eleven runs behind when Norman came to the rescue and a determined stand carried the score to 48.</p>
        <p>We had a very poor team for their Warrington fixture with Preston on Thursday but nevertheless they managed to secure the verdict. For this great praise is due to B. Silcock. Bowling a good length he constantly had the Preston men in trouble and captured seven wickets for 33 roes. Preston were only able to run up 56. Warrington asserted themselves with the bat and scored 68 before they were all dismissed. H. 0. Adams, a new recruit to the ranks of the team, Came out with the top score (16) and should in time prove a valuable acquisition.</p>
        <p>Cycling. The Whitsuntide holidays were most disappointing from the cyclist's point of view, for the cold winds and lowering skies detracted a great deal ,from the pleasure of those a-wheel. The tours were therefore not the success which had been anticipated. A small party of members of the Warrington Cycling Club spent the week-end at Ellesmere. and visited Overton, Whitting- ton and several other places of interest. Another section went to Carnarvon, Bettws-y-Coed and other favourite Welsh resorts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>CUP WINNERS ENTERTAINED.</head>
        <p>CUP WINNERS ENTERTAINED.</p>
        <p>The feting of the players was continued on Thursday when, in the evening they were the guests of Mr. Lake at the Blue Lion Inn. Mr. H. Ashton presided over an attendance which numbered about 30. An excellent repsst was served after which the evening was spent in an enjoyable manner. Toasts were honoured and during the evening songs were given by Messrs. Fish, Knight, F. J. Emson and Handley. Mr. A. was the accompanist. Excellent selections on the gramaphone were given by Mr. Lake.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>TittCKS OFF THE LINE.</head>
        <p>TittCKS OFF THE LINE.</p>
        <p>One or tWo coal trucks got off the line at Earlestown on Wednesday night, and the trains between Liverpool and Manchester were delayed. No one was hurt and no great damage was done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>RAILWAY INSTITUTE. Mr. I. T. Williams' Departure.</head>
        <p>THE RAILWAY INSTITUTE. Mr. I. T. Williams' Departure.</p>
        <p>The first annual meeting of the London and North- Western Railway Institute was held on Friday evening last, in the Lecture Room at the Institute, and was pre- sided over by Mr. I. T. Williams, the President, who had come down from London to officiate. There was an excellent attendance of members, whose enthusiasm and appreciation in the working of the Institute was very marked. After the Secretary had presented his report on the first year's working the balance sheet was adopted with little or no discussion. In the short space of 12 months the Institute has become possessed of two billiard tables, bagatelle table, air rifle, and various other games, also the nucleus of a library, to which additions will be made from time to time. The most recent additions are the bowling green and miniature rifle range, and thanks to the generous assistance the Institute has received from the officials of the Corn- pany,and also from the members thernselves,the balance sheet shows that practically all liabilities have been met.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wrr,LiAms, in addressing the meeting, obviously felt his severance from the railway staff of Warrington which his promotion to London has necessitated. Ere said that lie was glad to have that opportunity of speak- ing to the men and assuring them that his interest in them, and in the Institute, which wag in such a pros- perous condition, would continue and any assistance that could be given by him would always be at their disposal. Mr. Williams briefly reviewed the initiation and construction of the Institute, Bowling Green and Rifle Range, and said it was a credit to all who had spent their time and energy in its organization. Mr. WrLLIAms in referring to Mr. Sargent, his successor, (who was unanimously elected President) said: " Yon ate fortunate in having as my successor a sportsman,one whom I know will enter heart and soul into the work I have been interested in, and I am quite satisfied he will not only follow upon the lines wehave already worked, but he will at the same time do his level best to develop it, and extend it in every possible way, and I need scarcely tell you that I am glad such a. gentleman has been appointed to succeed me. Although I may not occupy the position of President you may take it from me that I shall always keep a very keen eye on Warrington, and any help that I can ever give it I shall be only too pleased. Mr. Sargent will have it in his power to render you very material help, and in my_opinion he will make you an excellent President." _</p>
        <p>Mr V7ILLIAM3 thou referred to the excellent work that had, been done by the officers and committee, and specially mentioned the hon. secretary, Mr. Robinson, who had given so much of his time and energy since the inception of the Institute; in fact, but for the enthusi- astic spirit in which he had entered into it, the Institute would not have proved the great success it has, and a special vote of thanks was accorded to him for his past services, and for kindly promising to undertake the duties for another year. The Hon. Treasurer, Assistant Secretary, and Com- mittee of Management were re-appointed, and a cordial vote of thanks passed for their services during tne pre- ceding twelve months. Mr. PARKHOUSE (the Chairman of the Committee), in proposing a vote of thanks to Mr. Williams for presid- ing at the meeting, referred to that gentleman's dis- interested and whole-hearted efforts on behalf of the Institute and members generally, and at the same time endorsed what Mr. Williams had already said with regard to Mr. Sargent. The vote of thanks was carried with musical honours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>YESTERDAYS' POLICE CASES.</head>
        <p>YESTERDAYS' POLICE CASES.</p>
        <p>Before the Mayor (Alderman Smethurst) and</p>
        <p>other magistrates. WIFE DESERTION.—Sarah Ellen Dunn, 90, Battersby- lane, summoned her husband, James Dunn for having deserted her. She stated that since her husband came out of prison on March 16th he had sold the home up, and had not given her any money since. The magis- trates granted her a separation order, with an allowance of 10s. a week.</p>
        <p>Boy's BAD START.—William Henry Tyson, 15 years of age, living at 167, Orford-lane, pleaded guilty to stealing a box containing a repair outfit from a cycle bag in the Parr Hall on Tuesday night, the property of Charles Newton, and also with stealing two bicycle spanners, the property of Albert Newnes, a month ago. The boy was allowed to go on the costs (21 45.) being paid,and the Mayor warned him.as to his future conduct A WRONG TICKET.—James Kelly. 31, Winifred-street was fined 10s. for travellivg on the London and North- Western Railway from Lykam to Warrington without having paid his fare. When the train from Lymm to War- rington arrived at 10.20 the previous night the defendant gave the collector a ticket from Leigh and Bedford to Warrington. As he failed to give a satisfactory expla- ation he was lo3lcel ur).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>LORD METHUEN AND GENERAL BOTHA.</head>
        <p>LORD METHUEN AND GENERAL BOTHA.</p>
        <p>General Lord Methuen on Thursday night unveiled in London a memorial tablet to the men of the 2nd V.B. Royal Fusiliers who fell in South Africa, and also a roll of honour to those of the battalion who served in the late war. He said that he was glad to he there to unveil a memorial to the men who had the good fortune to serve their country and the misfortune to lose tbeir lives. Their sympathies were with their friends and relations, and the happiest reflection for them was that time alone managed to heal the wound which would be always present to them. It must be to them a source of consolation that since the war the feeling of friendship against our enemies was growing warmer and warmer. We had seen within the last few weeks a man who was one of the staunchest of our foes, and also one of the straightest of the men who were fighting against us, received with every good-will in this country, and so. far as we could judge he was leading us in the right way in which the bonds of friendship should be cemented between the Dutch and the English. Let them hope that in the years to come the Dutch and the English might be welded together firmer and firmer, and that they might feel that the men tp whom that memorial was subscribed had not fallen in vain for the country they were all so proud to serve.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>ORIGIN OF EARTHQUAKES.</head>
        <p>THE ORIGIN OF EARTHQUAKES.</p>
        <p>Earthquakes are caused, according to the views of most modern seismologists, by the crumpling of the earth's crust, which most often place where there are weaknesses or breaks in it, and where the presence of great ranges of mountains throws great pressure on weak spots. This view is combated by Professor J. J. See, who believes that earthquakes are nearly always caused by the oceans or surface waters of the earth per- colating to the molten lava which lies twenty or thirty miles below the earth's crust. A summary of his views is to be found in the current number of" Know- ledge." Earthquakes, Professor See contends, must depend on explosive forces within or just under the earth's crust, and frequently spread over a considerable area, and the preponderance of disturbances in the sea and along the shores of continents shows that the forces depend in some way on sea water. That steam is the cause of volcanic eruptions is shown by the distribution of active volcanoes along the shores,and by the innumer- able eruptions which take place in the depths of the ocean, whereas such vents always ?die out inland. And, moreover, by the fact that of the vapours emitted from volcanoes 999 parts in 1,000 are estimated to be steam.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>LOCIii CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>LOCIii CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Services for Sunday.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. An open meeting of this Society will be held at three o'clock, when the Nrayor (Alder— man Smethurst) will preside. An address will be delivered by llr. P. Harrisan (Oldham), and solos will be given by Hr. Pearce (Warrington). Bold-street Wesleyan Church : Preachers, 10.30 Rev. W.Nlellor; 6:30, Rev. J. Gunnell.</p>
        <p>, Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church : Preachers, 10.30 Mr. Wynne Jones ; 6.30, Rev. W. Mellor. Baptist Chum"), 3olborne-street : Preacher 10.30 and 6.30, Bev. J. Smeed, Baptist College, Manchester, and formerly of South Africa. Latchford Baptist Church : Preacher 10.30 and 6.30 Mr. J. T. Beswick, Golborne.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-05-25_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>I Special services in celebration of the Sunday school ! anniversary were held in connection with this church on Snnday. The event is always looked forward to with great interest, and there were, as usual, large congrega- tions. The preacher was the Rev. J. Rowell Jones, of I Rotherham, and formerly of this circuit, whose eloquent !discourses were much appreciated. In the afternoon a I musical service was held in the Parr Hall, Mr. D. I Garnett presiding. • The children sang special hymns in a very pleasing manner, and praise is due to the con- ductor (Kr. CI R. Muir) for the admirable way in which he had trained them. Miss Lily Fairhurst (coatralto) very feelingly sang " For the children's sake." The Rev. W. Mellor delivered a short address. At the even- ing service in the Parr Hall the rthoir effectively sang : the anthem "Light of Life, and Miss Lily Fairhurst gave the solo" Giver of Life." Mr. E. G. 'tickers ably presid6d at the organ at all the services. The collec- tions amounted to £29.</p>
        <p>"She was ejected from a P.H.," said a policeman in a case at North London Police Court on Thursday.</p>
        <p>Magistrate: Say "public-house," .not "1"111.." We aro very busy to-day, but have sullictent time to hear proper English. Ffty-flve thousand immigrants have arrived at Montreal, bound for the Canadian North-west, since January,as compared with 40,000 during the correspond- ing period of last year.</p>
      </div>
