        <p>WARRINGTON SEAWEED IN KIDNEY DISEASES. Successful Warrington Athletes. t. Dr. James Walker, Kidney and Urinary Specialist At the annual sports held at Shrewsbury on Monday New York, says :--7" I never treat a case where the H. Mounfield, Warrington (55), gained second prize in kidneys, urinary organs or stomach are involved without the half-mile bicycle handicap. At the Wigan seaweed. I regard it as indispensable." Infirmary sports on Monday, L. Green, Warrington, The species of seaweed used by .Dr. Walker is the siafemneo,as_ etahat !ed T. . won the second prize in the quarter-mile fiat race off s S weecontair •in Veno's Seaweed lonic. d Tonic is used by many prominent the 28 yards mark. doctors in all forms of kidney and urinary diseases Death of an Old Whitley Resident. ; because of its great strengthening, healing and purifying effect. It cures nephritis, Bright's disease, uric acid Many people will hear with regret of the death of troubles, weakness, dropsy and backache; especially Mr. Hayes, of Whitley Superior, who passed away at successful in constipation. Pleasant to take. Cures his residence early on Tuesday morning, after a very permanently. No return. short illness. Mr. Hayes was one of the oldest resid- Ask for Veno's Seaweed Tonic, at chemists every- , ents in the parish, having lived in it for upwards of 60 where, one &amp; three-halfpence and two-Sr-nine. years. "LEA-FRANCIS," "PREMIER," and " BUDGE- WHITWORTH " CYCLES. Price from £4 10s. Cash or easy payments.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Wycliffe P.S.A. There was a fair attendance at the meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. society, on Sunday. Mr. G. Smiley presided and Mr. F. J. Griffen, of Liverpool, spoke upon "Going :forward." He also sang two solos, "Sun of my soul" and "Guiding light." The address and solos were much enjoyed. Mr. W. Pearce pre- sided at the organ and Mr. C. Cotton also took part in the meeting. Music in the Park. Mr. T. J. Down's Military Band will play the following selection of music (weather permitting), on Thursday evening, commencing at 7.15 :—March, "Hoch Habsburg," (Kral) ; valse, " Midsummer " (Marigold) ; overture, "La Dame Blanche" (Boiel- dieu) ; dance, "Pas Seul " (Floridan.) ; selection, "Haddon Hall " (Snllivan) •, scene, " L' Assedio di Arlem "(Verdi) ;' galop, " Iris" (Faust). God save the King. Mr. Arthur Down will conduct. Sum made to measure.—BAs, Outfitter.—Advt. Sankey Homing Society. Twenty-seven members competed in the Worcester race with 327 birds, which Mr. S. Taylor liberated at 11.20. Result :-Ist and special, Boyle, 1,005; 2nd, Aughton, 993 ; 3rd, *Woolley, 991 ; Susamerfield 987, Houghton 985, Walker 976, Longmore 971, Hartland 968, Lucas and Noon 960, Turner 959, Bond and Taylor 955, Dixon 954, *Houghton 944, Wilcock 941, *Lucas and Noon 934, Hardy and Thottias 930, 'Dixon 929, -',Deakin and Greaves 928, Lloyd 920, Bond and Taylor, 917. °Nominated birds. Run Over by a Lurry.. sio William Vernon, hawker, 34, Winwick-road, was charged at the Borough Police Court, on Tuesday,with having been drunk whilst in charge of a pony and cart. P.C. Holland stated that at three o'clock on Monday afternoon defendant drove a pony and cart over Warrington Bridge. Witness noticed that he was un- steady,but before heCHolland)could get to him,defendant who was drink, fell off the cart and the wheels of a coal lurry passed over his legs. Witness conveyed him to the Infirmary, and after Dr. Brown attended to his injuries he was allowed to proceed home.—Vernon was fined ss. and costs (11s. altogether). BLOUSES, a grand selection from is. 110.— BANKS, Outfitter, Horsemarket st.—Advt. Cheshire Brigade at Abergavenny. Nearly 1,000 volunteers forming the Cheshire Brigade, and comprising five battalions, are under- going their' annual training at Abergavenny. The brigade is under the command of Brigadier General Ommaney. On Monday there was sectional drill under the officers commanding the respective battalions. The Ist and 3rd battalions were paraded in Coldbrook Park, by permission of Sir Arthur Herbert, British Ambassador at Copenhagen. There were also signalling exercises under Captain Fennell. Massed bands played in the historic grounds of Abergavenny Castle. On Friday the brigade manceuvred on the Sugar Loaf Mountain, 2,000 ft. above sea level. "LEA-FRANCIS," "PREMIER," and " ItuDGE- WHITWORTH CICLES. Price from £4 10e. Cash or easy payments.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Works Theft. Before the Mayor f(Alderman Smethurst), at the Warrington Pollee! Court, on Wednesday, Oharles Daniels, of Fairclough's-avenue, waa charged with stealing or receiving a quantity of brass and iron, value 1.2 12s. Bd., the property of the Warrington and District Threshing Company. Mr. T. S. Steel said for some time past numerous articles had been missed from the yard in Lytbgoe's-lane. In consequence of a conversation Mr. Farrington, the manager of the company, visited the yard of a broker named Matthews, and there found a brass ring, which was a part of the machinery of a threshing machine belonging to the company. The matter ,was placed in the hands of the police, and Daniels was subsequently arrested. The Mayor, in committing Daniels to prison for three months, strongly condemned the action of Matthews, and hoped that the police would make further nquiries with a view to prosecution. CELLULAR SHIRTS from 2/6—BANKs,Ontfitter.-Advt. Old Warringtonian's Death. The funeral of the Rev. T. Bridge, vicar of Poynton and a native of Warrington, took place on Saturday' and was of a most impressive character. The coffin was carried by the bellringers and sidesmen from the Vicarage to the church, which was crowded with mourners. Walking in front were representatives of the Macclesfield Board, of Guardians, Macclesfield and District Council, Poynton Primitive Methodist Church, Poynton Baptist Church,Poynton Brass Band, Poynton Cricket Club, Poynton Football Club, and the farmers. Immediately behind were the Vicar's personal friends and the teachers of the Sunday and day schools. The service in church was choral, the Rev. J. G. Elstob, Rural Dean of Macclesfield, and the Rev. J. Begge, M.A., vicar of Woodford, officiating. Mr. Bridge, who was 65 years of age, was born in Bank-street, Warrington, and was educated at the Blue Coat School, where he ultimately became usher. NORFOLK SUITS from 18/6 —BANK s,Outfitter. —Advt. Warrington's New Chief Constable. "The Windsor and Eton Express" commenting upon the appointment of Mr. Martin Nicholls as Chief Constable of Warrington, says: —" During the five years he has been Chief Constable of Windsor, he has raised the tone and calibre of the police force, and has earned the respect and confidence of the Watch Com- mittee, the Council and the whole town, while his con- duct of police ' matters and investigation of crime has left nothing to be desired. He has always taken a keen interest in the welfare of the men under him, and in the police court •he has never been known to unduly press to Severity charges against prisoners, but has often induced magistrates to temper justice with mercy. Mr. Nicholls has had a successful career. He passed through all the grades of police.life at Reading, where he held the rank of inspector when he was appointed Chief Constable of Windsor. He is only thirty-six years of age, a most useful time of life for such an appointment as he has now received. He will go to a district—very much less picturesque than Windsor— accompanied by the good wishes of all with whom he came in contact in the Royal Borough." At the annual Government inspection of the Windsor police, Mr. Nicholls was congratulated on his promotion by Captain Terry, H.M. Inpsector of Prisons for the Southern Division. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Tha White City. Great success attended the opening of the White City at the Botanical Gardens, Old Trafford, on Whit Monday. In spite of the cold vreather,crowds swarmed into the place the visitors numbering 58,000 and in- cluding a great many people from Warrington and district. Many devices for securing amusement were placed at the disposal of public for a small fee,and there is every indication that the White City will prove one of the most popular centres of amusement in this part of the country. The most popular free spectacle was that of the captive balloon Its regular rising and descend- ing was watched by a great crowd of people. The next most interesting thing to watch was the "Figure gravity railway." A ride on a swift car over this undulating and tortuous course seemed to be one of the most invigorating pastimes to be found in thN Gardens. Some of the. side shows had not been com- pleted, and there were one or two unfinished corners which detracted from the appearance of the. White Fair. But the shows and games that were at work were numerous enough teisatisfy and to tire the most energetic pleasure-seeker. The band of the. Royal Marines (Portsmouth Division) provided excellent music, and the great fountain spouted its pillars of spray high into the air. In the evening, by the aid of many electric lights, the scene was made much more attractive. The fountain glowed in blended colours, and there was a firework display by Messrs. Pain. The White City will be open for the next six months and further particulars of the attractions appear in our advertising columns.</p>
